WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.600
Recorded books present an unabridged recording of The Great Gatsby by F. Scott Fitzgerald,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.920
narrated by Frank Muller.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.020
CHAPTER I.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.680
In my younger and more vulnerable years, my father gave me some advice that I've been

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.840
turning over in my mind ever since.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.480
Whenever you feel like criticizing anyone, he told me, just remember that all the people

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.960
in this world haven't had the advantages that you've had.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:37.360
He didn't say any more, but we've always been unusually communicative and a reserved

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.800
way, and I understood that he meant a great deal more than that.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:47.040
In consequence, I'm inclined to reserve all judgments, a habit that has opened up many

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.320
curious natures to me and also made me the victim of not a few veteran boars.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.360
The abnormal mind is quick to detect and detach itself to this quality when it appears

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.080
in a normal person, and so it came about that in college I was unjustly accused of being

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:08.560
a politician, because I was privy to the secret griefs of wild, unknown men.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:15.120
Most of the confidences were unsought, frequently I have feigned sleep, preoccupation, or a hostile

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.800
levity when I realised by some unmistakable sign that an intimate revelation was quivering

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.680
on the horizon, for the intimate revelations of young men, or at least the terms in which

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.440
they express them, are usually plagiaristic and marred by obvious suppression.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:36.160
Reserving judgments is a matter of infinite hope.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:40.520
I am still a little afraid of missing something if I forget that, as my father's snobbishly

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:46.800
suggested, and I snobbishly repeat, a sense of the fundamental decencies is parceled

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.640
out unequally at birth.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:57.040
And after boasting this way of my tolerance, I come to the admission that it has a limit.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.200
Conduct may be founded on the hard rock or the wet marshes, but after a certain point

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.680
I don't care what it's founded on.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.600
When I came back from the East last autumn I felt that I wanted the world to be in uniform

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.680
and at a sort of moral attention forever.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.520
I want to know more riotous excursions with privileged glimpses into the human heart.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.240
See Gatsby, the man who gives his name to this book, was exempt from my reaction.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:28.480
Gatsby, who represented everything for which I have an unaffected scorn.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:34.360
If personality is an unbroken series of successful gestures, then there was something gorgeous

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:40.760
about him, some heightened sensitivity to the promises of life, as if he were related

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:46.320
to one of those intricate machines that register earthquakes ten thousand miles away.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.760
This responsiveness had nothing to do with that flabby impressionability which is dignified

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.960
under the name of the creative temperament.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:03:00.560
It was an extraordinary gift for hope, a romantic readiness such as I have never found in any

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.120
other person, and which it is not likely I shall ever find again.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.160
No, Gatsby turned out all right at the end.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:16.200
It is what prayed on Gatsby, what foul dust floated in the wake of his dreams that temporarily

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.760
closed out my interest in the abortive sorrows and short-winded elation.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.360
My family and I have been prominent well to do people in this middle-western city for

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.000
three generations.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.080
The caraways are something of a clan, and we have a tradition that were descended from

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:37.200
the dukes of Baklou, but the actual founder of my line was my grandfather's brother,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.440
who came here in 51, sent a substitute to the Civil War, and started the wholesale hardware

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:45.400
business that my father carries on today.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.280
I never saw this great uncle, but I'm supposed to look like him, a special reference to the

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.360
rather hard-boiled painting that hangs in father's office.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.320
I graduated from New Haven in 1915, just a quarter of a century after my father, and a little

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:04.680
later I participated in that delayed, tutonic migration known as the Great War.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:10.480
I enjoyed the counter-raid, so thoroughly, that I came back restless.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.520
Instead of being the warm center of the world, the middle-west now seemed like the ragged

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:21.160
edge of the universe, so I decided to go east and learn the bond business.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:25.280
Everybody I knew was in the bond business, so I suppose it could support one more single

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:26.280
man.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.760
All my aunts and uncles talked it over as if they were choosing a prep school for me, and

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:35.640
finally said, well, yes, with very grave hesitant faces.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.440
There agreed to finance me for a year, and after various delays I came east, permanently,

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:44.760
I thought, in the spring of 22.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:49.360
The practical thing was to find rooms in the city, but it was a warm season, and I had

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:55.520
just left a country of wide lawns and friendly trees, so when a young man at the office suggested

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:59.880
that we take a house together in a commuting town, it sounded like a great idea.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:05.480
He found the house, a weather-beaten cardboard bungalow at 80 a month, but at the last minute

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:09.320
the firm ordered him to Washington and I went out to the country alone.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:15.080
I had a dog, nor at least I had him for a few days until he ran away, and an old dodge,

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:19.600
and a Finnish woman who made my bed and cooked breakfast and muttered Finnish wisdom to herself

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:22.400
over the electric stove.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.480
It was lonely for a day or so until one morning some man more recently arrived than I stopped

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.560
me on the road.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:31.840
How do you get to West Egg Village?

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:33.680
He asked helplessly.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.920
I told him, and as I walked on I was lonely no longer.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.720
I was a guide, a pathfinder, an original settler.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:45.400
He had casually conferred on me the freedom of the neighborhood.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:50.920
And so, with the sunshine and the great bursts of leaves growing on the trees, just as things

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:57.160
grow in fast movies, I had that familiar conviction that life was beginning over again with the

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:58.160
summer.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.520
There was so much to read for one thing, and so much fine health to be pulled down out

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:05.880
of the young breath-giving air.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:11.080
I bought a dozen volumes on banking and credit and investment securities, and they stood on

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:17.000
my shelf in red and gold, like new money from the mint, promising to unfold the shining

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:22.120
secrets that only mightest and morgan and my seeners knew.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.880
And I had the high intention of reading many other books besides.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.320
I was rather literary in college.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:33.080
One year I wrote a series of very solemn and obvious editorials for the Yale News.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.440
And now I was going to bring back all such things into my life, and become again that

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.320
most limited of all specialists, the well-rounded man.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:44.240
This isn't just an epigram.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:49.880
Life is much more successfully looked at from a single window after all.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:54.320
It was a matter of chance that I should have rented a house in one of the strangest communities

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:55.880
in North America.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:00.800
It was on that slender riotous island which extends itself to the natural curiosity.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:04.080
These two unusual formations of land.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:09.640
Twenty miles from the city, a pair of enormous eggs, identical in contour and separated only

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:15.240
by a courtesy bay, jut out into the most domesticated body of saltwater in the western

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:16.520
hemisphere.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:20.000
The great wet bar and yard of Long Island sound.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.920
They are not perfect ovals like the egg in the Columbus story.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.320
They are both crushed flat at the contact end.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:31.040
But their physical resemblance must be a source of perpetual confusion to the goals that

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:37.000
fly overhead, to the wingless or more arresting for now and on as their dissimilarity in every

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:40.640
particular except shape and size.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:46.040
I lived at West Egg, the, well, the less fashionable of the two.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:50.720
Though this is a most superficial tag to express the bizarre and not a little sinister contrast

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:52.120
between them.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:57.120
My house was at the very tip of the egg, only fifty yards from the sound, and squeezed

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:02.560
between two huge places that rented for twelve or fifteen thousand a season.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:06.240
The one on my right was a colossal affair by any standard.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:12.200
It was a factual imitation of some hotel divi in Normandy with a tower on one side,

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:18.080
spanking new under a thin beard of raw ivy, and a marble swimming pool in more than forty

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:21.160
acres of lawn and garden.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:27.240
It was Gatsby's mansion, or rather as I didn't know Mr. Gatsby who was a mansion inhabited

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.200
by a gentleman of that name.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:36.160
My own house was an eye-sour, but it was a small eye-sour, and it had been overlooked,

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:41.040
so I had a view of the water, a partial view of my neighbor's lawn, and the consoling

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:46.240
proximity of millionaires, all for eighty dollars a month.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:51.280
Across the courtesy bay, the white palaces of fashionable east egg glittered along the

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.800
water, and the history of the summer really begins on the evening I drove over there to

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:59.040
have dinner with the Tom Buchanan's.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.520
Daisy was my second cousin once removed, and I'd known Tom in college, and just after

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.960
the war I spent two days with him in Chicago.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:13.000
Her husband, among various physical accomplishments, had been one of the most powerful ends that

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:17.420
ever played football at New Haven, a national figure in a way, one of those men who reached

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:23.120
such an acute, limited excellence at twenty-one that everything afterwards savours of anti-clamags.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:27.280
His family were enormously wealthy, even in college, his freedom with money was a matter

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:32.480
for a reproach, but now he'd left Chicago and come east in a fashion that rather took

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:34.080
your breath away.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.680
For instance, he'd brought down a string of polo ponies from Lake Forest.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:42.480
It was hard to realize that a man in my own generation was wealthy enough to do that.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.280
Why, they came east, I don't know.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:51.320
They had spent a year in France for no particular reason, and then drifted here and there unrestfully

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:55.160
wherever people played polo and were rich together.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:59.160
This was a permanent move, said Daisy over the telephone, but I didn't believe it.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:04.160
I had no sight into Daisy's heart, but I felt that Tom would drift on forever, seeking

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:08.560
a little wistfully, for the dramatic turbulence of some irrecoverable.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:13.400
And so it happened that on a warm windy evening I drove over to East Egg to see two old

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:16.000
friends who might scarcely knew at all.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.640
Their house was even more elaborate than I expected, a cheerful, red and white, Georgian,

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:23.240
colonial mansion overlooking the bay.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:27.800
The lawn started at the beach and ran toward the front door for a quarter of a mile, jumping

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:31.400
over sundials and brick walks and burning gardens.

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:35.440
Finally when it reached the house drifting up the side in bright vines as though from the

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:37.360
momentum of its run.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:42.720
The front was broken by a line of French windows glowing now with reflected gold and wide

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:48.140
open to the warm windy evening, and Tom Buchanan in riding clothes was standing with his legs

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.360
apart on the front porch.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:53.120
He had changed since his new haven years.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:57.640
Now he was a sturdy, straw-haired man of thirty with a rather hard mouth and a supercilious

00:10:57.640 --> 00:10:58.640
manner.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:04.320
Two shining, arrogant eyes had established dominance over his face, and gave him the appearance

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:07.080
of always leaning aggressively forward.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:11.800
Even the effeminate swank of his riding clothes could hide the enormous power of that body.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:16.120
He seemed to fill those glistening boots until he strained the top leasing, and you could

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:21.320
see a great pack of muscle shifting when his shoulder moved under his thin coat.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:26.560
It was a body capable of enormous leverage, a cruel body.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:31.560
His speaking voice, a gruff, husky tenor, added to the impression of fractiousness he conveyed.

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:36.640
There was a touch of paternal contempt in it, even toward people he liked, and there were

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:38.920
a man at New Haven who had hated his guts.

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:43.280
Now, don't think my opinion on these matters is final, he seemed to say, just because

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.440
I am stronger and more of a man than you are.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:50.480
We were in the same senior society, and while we were not in the same time, I always had

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:55.560
the impression that he approved of me, and wanted me to like him with some harsh defiant

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.120
whistfulness of his own.

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:00.720
We talked for a few minutes on the sunny porch.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:06.280
I've got a nice place here, he said, his eyes flashing about restlessly.

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:11.120
Turning me around by one arm, he moved a broad flat hand along the front vista, including

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:17.120
in its sweep a sunken Italian garden, a half acre of deep, pungent roses, and a snub-nosed

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:19.880
motorboat that bumped the tide off shore.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:25.400
It belonged to the main, the oil man, he turned me around again, politely and abruptly.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.360
We'll go inside.

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:32.800
We walked through a high hallway into a bright rosy coloured space, frangely bound into

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:35.680
the house by French windows at either end.

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:40.480
The windows were a jar and gleaming white against the fresh grass outside that seemed to

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:42.680
grow a little way into the house.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:48.440
A breeze blew through the room, blue curtains in at one end and out the other like pale flags

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:53.040
twisting them up toward the frosted wedding cake of the ceiling, and then rippled over

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:58.640
the wine-coloured rug, making a shadow on it as windows on the sea.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:04.400
The only completely stationary object in the room was an enormous couch, on which two

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:08.800
young women were buoyed up as they were upon an anchored balloon.

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:12.680
They were both in white, and their dresses were rippling and fluttering as if they had

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:16.560
just been blown back in after a short flight around the house.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:21.480
I must have stood for a few moments listening to the whip and snap of the curtains, and

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.280
the groan of a picture on the wall.

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:29.520
Then there was a boom as Tom Buchanan shut the rear windows, and the caught wind died

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:34.240
out about the room and the curtains and the rugs and the two young women, but the younger

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:36.440
of the children was a stranger to me.

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:41.080
She was extended full length at her end of the divan, completely motionless, and with

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:45.120
a chin raised a little as if she were balancing something on it which was quite likely to

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:46.120
fall.

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:48.000
She saw me out of the corner of her eye.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:49.000
She gave no hint of it.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.840
Indeed, I was almost surprised into murmuring an apology for having disturbed her by coming

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:54.160
in.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:57.240
The other girl, Daisy, made an attempt to rise.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:00.720
She leaned slightly forward with a conscientious expression.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:03.880
Then she laughed, and absurd, charming little laugh.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:06.880
And I laughed too, and came forward into the room.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:09.560
I'm paralyzed with happiness.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:13.640
She laughed again as if she said something very witty, and held my hand for a moment looking

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:17.680
up into my face, promising that there was no one in the world she so much wanted to

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:18.680
see.

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:21.080
That was the way she had.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:25.400
She hinted in a murmur that the surname of the balancing girl was Baker.

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:29.960
I've heard it said that Daisy's murmur was only to make people lean toward her in a

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:33.240
relevant criticism that made it no less charming.

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:36.160
At any rate, Miss Baker's lips fluttered.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:40.160
She nodded at me, almost imperceptibly, and then quickly tipped her head back again.

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:43.280
The object she was balancing had obviously taught her to little and give her something

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:44.280
of a fright.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:47.200
Again a sort of apology arose to my lips.

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:52.840
Almost any exhibition of complete self-sufficiency draws a stunned attribute from me.

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:58.000
I looked back at my cousin who began to ask me questions in her low, thrilling voice.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:03.640
It was the kind of voice that the ear follows up and down, as if each speech is in a

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:06.760
arrangement of notes that will never be played again.

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:13.240
Her face was sad and lovely, with bright things in it, bright eyes, and a bright passionate

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:14.240
mouth.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:18.400
But there was an excitement in her voice that men who had cared for her found difficult

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:19.400
to forget.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:26.000
A singing compulsion, a whispered listen, a promise that she had done gay, exciting things

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:31.200
just a while since, and that there were gay, exciting things hovering in the next hour.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:35.240
I told her how I had stomped off in Chicago for a day on my way east, and how a dozen people

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:37.240
had sent their love through me.

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:38.920
Do they miss me?

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:40.720
She cried ecstatically.

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:42.640
The whole town is desolate.

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:47.200
All the cars have the left rear wheel painted black as a morning wreath, and there's a persistent

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.680
whale all night along the north shore.

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.400
How gorgeous!

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:53.880
Let's go back, Tom, tomorrow!

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:55.560
Then she added her relevantly.

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:57.360
You ought to see the baby.

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:58.360
I'd like to.

00:15:58.360 --> 00:15:59.360
She's asleep.

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:00.360
She's three years old.

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:02.280
Haven't you ever seen her?

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:03.280
Never.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:04.560
Well, you ought to see her.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:09.040
She's Tom Buchanan, who had been hovering restlessly about the room stopped and rested

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:11.000
his hand on my shoulder.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:12.800
What are you doing, Nick?

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:14.680
I'm a bond man.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:15.680
Who with?

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:16.680
I told him.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:19.640
Never heard of him, he remarked decisively.

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:21.120
This annoyed me.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:22.840
You will, I answered shortly.

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:24.360
You will if you stay in the east.

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:28.440
Oh, I'll stay in the east, don't you worry, he said, glancing at Daisy, and then back

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:30.840
at me as if he were alert for something more.

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:34.520
I'd be a god damned fool to live anywhere else.

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:39.520
At this point Miss Baker said, absolutely, with such sudden as that I started.

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:42.920
It was the first word she had uttered since I came into the room.

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:46.400
I've evidently had surprised her as much as it did me, for she yawned, and with a series

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:50.160
of rapid, deafed movements stood up into the room.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.000
I'm stiff, she complained.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:55.240
I've been lying on that sofa for her as long as I can remember.

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:56.920
Don't look at me, Daisy, retorted.

00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:59.520
I've been trying to get you to New York all afternoon.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:04.080
No thanks, said Miss Baker, to the four cocktails just in from the pantry.

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:06.240
I'm absolutely entraining.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:08.160
Her host looked at her incredulously.

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:09.440
You are!

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:13.040
He took down his drink as if it were a drop in the bottom of a glass.

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:16.160
How you ever get anything done is beyond me.

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:19.640
I looked at Miss Baker, wondering what it was she got done.

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:20.880
I enjoyed looking at her.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:25.440
She was a slender, small, breasted girl with an erect carriage, which she accentuated

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:29.160
by throwing her body backward at the shoulders like a young cadet.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:34.000
Her grey, sun-strained eyes looked back at me with polite reciprocal curiosity, out

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:37.560
of a one charming, discontented face.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:43.080
It occurred to me now that I had seen her, or a picture of her somewhere before.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:44.760
You live in West Egg.

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:46.040
She remarked contemptuously.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:47.760
I know somebody there.

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:50.040
I don't know a single—you must know Gatsby.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:54.600
Gatsby could reply that it was my neighbor, dinner was announced.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:59.560
Wedging his tense arm, imperatively under mine, Tom Buchanan compelled me from the room

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:03.080
as though we were moving at checker to another square.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:07.880
Slendily, languidly, their hands set lightly on their hips.

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:13.720
The two young women proceeded us out into a rosy-colored porch, open toward the sunset,

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:17.800
where four candles flickered on the table and the diminished wind.

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:21.040
Why candles, objected Daisy frowning?

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:23.040
She snapped them out with her fingers.

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:25.840
In two weeks it'll be the longest day in the year.

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:27.400
She looked at us all radiantly.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:31.120
Do you always watch for the longest day of the year and then miss it?

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:39.720
We ought to plan something yawned Miss Baker, sitting down at the table as if she were

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:41.200
getting into bed.

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:44.240
All right, said Daisy, what on we plan?

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:45.400
She turned to me helplessly.

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:47.600
What do people plan?

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:51.840
Before I could answer her eyes, fastened with an odd expression on her little finger.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:52.840
Look, she complained.

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:54.080
I heard it.

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:55.080
We all looked.

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:57.600
The knuckle was black and blue.

00:18:57.600 --> 00:18:58.880
You did it, Tom.

00:18:58.880 --> 00:18:59.880
She said acutingly.

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:02.160
I know you didn't mean to, but you did do it.

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:07.040
That's what I get for marrying a brute of a man, a great big hulking physical specimen of

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:12.160
a eye-hate that word hulking, objected to him crossly, even in kidding.

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:15.200
Hulking insisted Daisy.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:20.240
Sometimes she and Miss Baker talked at once, unobtrusively, and with a bantering, inconsequence

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:25.120
that was never quite chatter, that was as cool as their white dresses and their impersonal

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:28.200
eyes and the absence of all desire.

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:33.380
They were here, and they accepted Tom and me, making only a polite, pleasant effort to

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:35.960
entertain or to be entertained.

00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:39.760
They knew that presently dinner would be over and a little later the evening, too, would

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:42.680
be over and casually put away.

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:47.400
It was sharply different from the west, where an evening was hurried from phase to phase

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:52.680
toward its close, in a continually disappointed anticipation, or else in sheer nervous dread

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:54.880
of the moment itself.

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:57.000
You make me feel uncivilized, Daisy.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:01.920
I can fast on my second glass of quirky, but rather impressive, claret.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:04.360
Can't you talk about crops or something?

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:09.360
I meant nothing in particular by this remark, but it was taken up in an unexpected way.

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:12.880
Civilization's going to pieces, broke out Tom violently.

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:16.440
I've got to be a terrible pessimist about things.

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:20.080
Have you read the rise of the colored empires by this man, Goddard?

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:23.880
Why no, I answered rather surprised by his tone.

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:27.560
Well, it's a fine book and everybody ought to read it.

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:31.880
The idea is, if we don't look at the white race, we'll be utterly submerged.

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:33.040
It's all scientific stuff.

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:34.440
It's been proved.

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:40.520
Tom's getting very profound, said Daisy, with an expression of unfauteful sadness.

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:44.560
He reads deep books with long words in them.

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:45.960
What was that word?

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:50.640
Well, these books are all scientific and sisted, Tom, glancing at her impatiently.

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:53.080
This fellow has worked out the whole thing.

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:57.560
It's up to us who are the dominant race to watch out, or these other races will have control

00:20:57.560 --> 00:20:58.560
of things.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:03.720
We've got to beat them down, whispered Daisy, winking ferociously toward the fervent

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.320
sun.

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:10.080
You ought to live in California, began, Miss Baker, but Tom interrupted her by shifting heavily

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:11.640
in his chair.

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:13.960
This idea is that we're Nordics.

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:19.360
I am, and you are, and you are, and after an infinitesimal hesitation, he included Daisy

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:22.360
with a slight nod, and she winked at me again.

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:25.080
And we've produced all the things that go to make civilization.

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:31.160
No, science, and that aren't his concentration, as if his complacency more acute than of old

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:33.160
was not enough to him anymore.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:37.640
When almost immediately the telephone rang inside, and the butler left the porch, Daisy

00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:41.120
seized upon the momentary interruption and leaned toward me.

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:44.680
I'll tell you a family secret, she whispered enthusiastically.

00:21:44.680 --> 00:21:46.360
It's about the butler's nose.

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:49.360
Do you want to hear about the butler's nose?

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:50.880
That's why I came over tonight.

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:53.240
Well, it wasn't always a butler.

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:57.040
It used to be the silver polisher for some people in New York that had a silver service

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:58.880
for two hundred people.

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:04.240
He had to polish it from morning till night, until finally it began to affect his nose.

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:06.720
Things went from bad to worse, suggested Miss Baker.

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:12.400
Yes, things went from bad to worse, until finally he had to give up his position.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:17.960
For a moment the last sunshine fell with romantic affection upon her glowing face.

00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:22.000
Her voice compelled me forward breathlessly as I listened.

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:27.520
Then the glow faded, each light deserting her with lingering regret, like children leaving

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:30.360
a pleasant street at dusk.

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:34.600
The butler came back and murmured something close to Tom's ear, whereupon Tom frowned,

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:37.800
pushed back his chair and without a word went inside.

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:42.000
As if his absence quickened something within her, Daisy leaned forward again, her voice glowing

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:43.000
and singing.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:48.160
I love to see you at my table, Nick, you remind me of a rose, an absolute rose, doesn't

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:49.160
he?

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:50.840
She turned to Miss Baker for confirmation.

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:52.880
An absolute rose?

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:53.880
This was untrue.

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:56.880
I'm not even faintly like a rose.

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:02.360
She was only extemporizing, but a stirring warmth flowed from her.

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:06.560
As if her heart was trying to come out to you concealed in one of those breathless, thrilling

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:07.560
words.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:11.360
Then suddenly she threw her napkin on the table and excused herself and went into the

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:12.360
house.

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:17.480
Miss Baker and I exchanged short clans, consciously devoid of meaning.

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:21.760
I was about to speak when she sat up alertly and said, shh, in a warning voice.

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:26.800
A subdued, impassioned murmur was audible in the room beyond, and Miss Baker leaned forward

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:29.040
on a shame, trying to hear.

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:34.840
The murmur trembled on the verge of coherence, sank down, mounted excitedly, and then ceased

00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:37.120
altogether.

00:23:37.120 --> 00:23:39.080
This Mr. Gatsby, you spoke of, is my neighbor.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:40.480
I said, don't talk.

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:42.800
I want to hear what happens.

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:44.280
Is something happening?

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:45.480
I inquired innocently.

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:47.680
You mean to say you don't know?

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:49.360
Said Miss Baker, honestly surprised.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:51.720
I thought everybody knew.

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:52.720
I don't.

00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:53.720
Why?

00:23:53.720 --> 00:23:54.960
She said hesitantly.

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.960
Tom's got some woman in New York.

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:00.160
Got some woman?

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:01.680
I repeated blankly.

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:03.520
Miss Baker nodded.

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:07.720
She might have the decency not to telephone him at dinner time, don't you think?

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:11.720
Just before I had grasped her, meaning there was the flutter of a dress and a crunch of

00:24:11.720 --> 00:24:15.600
leather boots and tarman daisy were back at the table.

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:19.320
It couldn't be helped cry daisy with tense gayity.

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:24.400
She sat down, glanced, searchingly, Miss Baker, and then at me, and continued.

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:28.040
I looked outdoors for a minute, and it was very romantic outdoors.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:32.440
There's a bird on the lawn that I think must be a nightingale come over on the Cunard

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:34.360
or white star line.

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:37.160
He's singing away, her voice sang.

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:39.760
This romantic isn't it Tom?

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:43.760
Very romantic, he said, and then miserably to me.

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:48.080
If it's light enough after dinner, I want to take you down to the stables.

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:53.120
The telephone rang inside, startlingly, and as daisy shook her head decisively at Tom,

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:58.440
the subject of the stables, in fact, all subjects vanished into air.

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:02.960
Among the broken fragments of the last five minutes at table, I remember the candles being

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:05.280
lit again, pointlessly.

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:11.120
But I was conscious of wanting to look squarely at everyone, and yet to avoid all eyes.

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:14.680
I couldn't guess what daisy and Tom were thinking, but I doubt if even Miss Baker, who

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:20.800
seemed to have mastered a certain hardy skepticism, was able utterly to put this fifth guest's

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:24.240
shrill metallic urgency out of mind.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:27.040
To a certain temperament the situation might have seemed intriguing.

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:31.240
My own instinct was to tell for a reason, if it was to say we're not mentioned again.

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:35.480
Tom and Miss Baker, with several feet of twilight between them, strolled back into

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:40.720
the library as if to a vigil beside a perfectly tangible body, while trying to look pleasantly

00:25:40.720 --> 00:25:45.480
interested in a little death, I followed daisy around a chain of connecting verandas

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:47.480
to the porch in front.

00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:53.160
In its deep gloom we sat down, side by side, on a wicker sety.

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:59.440
Daisy took her face in her hands, as if feeling its lovely shape, and her eyes moved gradually

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:01.760
out into the velvet dusk.

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:05.560
I saw that turbulent emotions possessed her, so I asked what I thought would be some

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:08.960
sedative questions about her little girl.

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:12.360
We don't know each other very well, Nick, she said suddenly.

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:16.200
Even if we are cousins, you didn't come to my wedding.

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:18.360
I wasn't back from the war.

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:20.600
That's true, she hesitated.

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:25.840
Well, I've had a very bad time, Nick, and I'm pretty cynical about everything.

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:28.040
Evidently she had reason to be.

00:26:28.040 --> 00:26:31.880
I waited but she didn't say any more, and after a moment I returned rather feebly to

00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:33.520
the subject of her daughter.

00:26:33.520 --> 00:26:38.160
I suppose she talks and eats and everything.

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:40.760
Oh yes, she looked at me absently.

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:45.400
Listen, Nick, let me tell you what I said when she was born, would you like to hear?

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:46.400
Very much.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:49.760
It'll show you how I've gotten to feel about things.

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:54.600
Well, she was less than an hour old, and Tom was God knows where.

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:58.280
I woke up out of the ether with an utterly abandoned feeling and asked the nurse right

00:26:58.280 --> 00:27:00.800
away if it was a boy or a girl.

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:04.360
She told me it was a girl, and so I turned my head away and wept.

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:09.360
All right, I said, I'm glad it's a girl, and I hope she'll be a fool.

00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:13.320
That's the best thing a girl can be in this world, a beautiful little fool.

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:17.120
You see, I think everything's terrible anyhow.

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:19.520
She went on in a convinced way.

00:27:19.520 --> 00:27:22.320
Everybody thinks so, the most advanced people.

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:26.520
And I know I've been everywhere and seen everything and done everything.

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:31.200
Her eyes flashed around her in a defiant way rather like Tom's, and she laughed with thrilling

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:35.400
scorns, sophisticated God, I'm sophisticated.

00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:41.280
The instant her voice broke off, ceasing to compel my attention, my belief, I felt the

00:27:41.280 --> 00:27:45.000
basic insincerity of what she had said.

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:49.920
It made me uneasy, as though the whole evening had been a trick of some sort to exact a

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:52.480
contributory emotion from me.

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:57.680
I waited, and sure enough in a moment she looked at me with an absolute smirk on her lovely

00:27:57.680 --> 00:28:03.120
face, as if she had asserted her membership in a rather distinguished secret society to

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:08.760
which she and Tom belonged.

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:12.400
Inside the crimson room bloomed with light.

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:16.840
Tom and Miss Baker sat at either end of the long couch, and she read aloud to him from

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:22.480
the Saturday evening post, the words murmurous and uninflected running together in a soothing

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:23.800
tune.

00:28:23.800 --> 00:28:29.120
The lamp light brightened on his boots and dull on the autumn leafy yellow of her hair,

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:33.960
glinted along the paper, as she turned a page with a flutter of slender muscles in her

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:35.400
arms.

00:28:35.400 --> 00:28:39.600
When we came in she held a silent for a moment with a lifted hand.

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:45.160
To be continued, she said tossing the magazine on the table in our very next issue.

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:49.720
A body asserted itself with a restless movement of her knee, and she stood up.

00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:54.540
Ten o'clock, she remarked apparently, finding the time on the ceiling, time for this good

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:56.680
girl to go to bed.

00:28:56.680 --> 00:29:00.480
Jordan's going to play in the tournament tomorrow, explained Daisy over at Westchester.

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:04.120
Oh, your Jordan Baker!

00:29:04.120 --> 00:29:06.480
I knew now why her face was familiar.

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:10.600
Its pleasing contemptuous expression had looked out at me from many rotogravere pictures

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:14.280
of the sporting life at Asheville and hot springs and Palm Beach.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:17.080
I had heard some story of her too.

00:29:17.080 --> 00:29:19.280
A critical, unpleasant story.

00:29:19.280 --> 00:29:22.480
Got what was I to discuss awfully, with me at eight, won't you?

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:25.160
If you'll get up, I will.

00:29:25.160 --> 00:29:27.600
Good night, Mr. Carroway, see you anon.

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:29.760
Of course you will, confirmed Daisy.

00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:32.080
In fact, I think I'll arrange a marriage.

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:35.440
Come over often, Nick, and I'll sort of vote, fling you together, you know, lock you

00:29:35.440 --> 00:29:39.800
up accidentally in linen closets and push you out to see in a boat and all that sort of

00:29:39.800 --> 00:29:40.800
thing.

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:42.720
Good night, called Miss Baker from the stairs.

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:43.960
I haven't heard a word.

00:29:43.960 --> 00:29:45.720
She's a nice girl.

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:47.840
It's a time after a moment.

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:50.880
They oughtn't to let her run around the country this way.

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:52.160
Who oughtn't to?

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:54.360
In quiet Daisy coldly.

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:55.600
Her family.

00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:59.400
Her family is one aunt about a thousand years old.

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:01.640
Besides Nick's going to look after her, aren't you Nick?

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:04.240
She's going to spend lots of weekends out here this summer.

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:07.400
I think the home influence will be very good for her.

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:10.760
Daisy and Tom looked at each other for a moment in silence.

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:13.040
Is she from New York?

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:14.760
I asked quickly.

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:15.760
From Louisville.

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:19.080
Our white girlhood was passed together there.

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:20.760
Our beautiful white.

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:23.720
Did you give Nick a little heart to heart talk on the veranda?

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:25.200
Demanded Tom suddenly.

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:27.080
Did I?

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:28.400
She looked at me.

00:30:28.400 --> 00:30:29.720
I can't seem to remember.

00:30:29.720 --> 00:30:32.000
But I think we talked about the Nordic race.

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:33.440
Yes, I'm sure we did.

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:34.760
It sort of trepped up on us.

00:30:34.760 --> 00:30:37.640
And the first thing, you know, don't believe everything you hear, Nick.

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:39.040
He advised me.

00:30:39.040 --> 00:30:41.960
I said lightly that I heard nothing at all.

00:30:41.960 --> 00:30:44.400
And a few minutes later I got up to go home.

00:30:44.400 --> 00:30:49.200
They came to the door with me and stood signed by side in a cheerful square of light.

00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:51.720
As I started my motor Daisy paramptorily called,

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:52.720
�Wait!�

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:54.160
I forgot to ask you something.

00:30:54.160 --> 00:30:55.400
It's important.

00:30:55.400 --> 00:30:57.560
We heard you were engaged to a girl out west.

00:30:57.560 --> 00:31:00.120
That's right, corroborated Tom kindly.

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:02.320
We heard that you were engaged.

00:31:02.320 --> 00:31:03.320
It's a libel.

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:04.320
I'm too poor.

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:08.120
But we heard it, insisted Daisy, surprising me by opening up again in a flower-like

00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:09.120
way.

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:11.120
We heard it from three people.

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:12.480
So it must be true.

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:17.520
Of course, I knew what they were referring to, but I wasn't even vaguely engaged.

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:21.320
The fact that Gossip had published the bands was one of the reason I had come east.

00:31:21.320 --> 00:31:25.880
You can't stop going with an old friend on account of rumours.

00:31:25.880 --> 00:31:29.880
And on the other hand, I had no intention of being rumoured into marriage.

00:31:29.880 --> 00:31:33.920
Their interest rather touched me and made them less remotely rich.

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:38.240
Nevertheless, I was confused and a little disgusted as I drove away.

00:31:38.240 --> 00:31:42.320
It seemed to me that the thing for Daisy to do was to rush out of the house, child in

00:31:42.320 --> 00:31:46.120
arms, but apparently there were no such intentions in her head.

00:31:46.120 --> 00:31:51.120
As for Tom, the fact that he had some woman in New York was really less surprising than

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:54.240
that he had been depressed by a book.

00:31:54.240 --> 00:31:59.560
Something was making him nibble at the edge of stale ideas, as if his sturdy physical

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:05.840
egotism no longer nourished his paramptory heart.

00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:11.400
Already it was deep summer on road-house roofs and in front of wayside garages, when you

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:15.280
read the gas pumps sat out in pools of light.

00:32:15.280 --> 00:32:20.680
And when I reached my estate at West Egg, I ran the car under its shed and sat for a while

00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:24.040
on an abandoned grass roller in the yard.

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:30.360
The wind had blown off, leaving a loud, bright night, with wings beating in the trees,

00:32:30.360 --> 00:32:36.800
and a persistent organ sound as the fold bellows of the earth blew the frogs full of life.

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:41.620
The silhouette of a moving cat waved across the moonlight, and turning my head to watch

00:32:41.620 --> 00:32:44.280
it I saw that I was not alone.

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:48.600
Fifty feet away a figure had emerged from the shadow of my neighbour's mansion, and

00:32:48.600 --> 00:32:54.520
was standing with his hands in his pockets regarding the silver pepper of the stars.

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:59.480
Something in his leisurely movements and the secure position of his feet upon the lawn

00:32:59.480 --> 00:33:04.600
suggested that it was Mr. Gatsby himself, come out to determine what share was his of

00:33:04.600 --> 00:33:06.760
our local heavens.

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:08.880
I decided to call to him.

00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:12.720
Miss Baker had mentioned him and did her and that would do for an introduction, but I

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:14.360
didn't call to him.

00:33:14.360 --> 00:33:16.920
So he gave a sudden intimation that he was content to be alone.

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:22.800
He stretched out his arms toward the dark water in a curious way, and far as I was from

00:33:22.800 --> 00:33:25.720
him I could have sworn he was trembling.

00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:31.960
In voluntarily I glanced seawood and distinguished nothing, except a single green light, my

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:37.040
newt and far away that might have been the end of a dark, when I looked once more of our

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:45.160
Gatsby he had vanished, and I was alone again in the unquiet darkness.

00:33:45.160 --> 00:33:47.520
Chapter 2

00:33:47.520 --> 00:33:53.440
About halfway between West Egg and New York, the motor road hastily joins the railroad

00:33:53.440 --> 00:33:58.240
and runs beside it for a quarter of a mile, so as to shrink away from a certain desolate

00:33:58.240 --> 00:34:00.520
area of land.

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:08.000
This is a valley of ashes, a fantastic farm where ashes grow like wheat into ridges and

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:14.520
hills and grotesque gardens where ashes take the forms of houses and chimneys and rising

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:21.680
smoke and finally with a transcendent effort of men who move dimly and already crumbling

00:34:21.680 --> 00:34:25.000
through the powdery air.

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:30.440
Occasionally a line of grey cars crawls along an invisible track, gives out a Gatsby

00:34:30.440 --> 00:34:37.360
creek and comes to rest, and immediately the ash-gray men swarm up with laden spades

00:34:37.360 --> 00:34:44.640
and stir up an impenetrable cloud which screens their obscure operations from your sight.

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:50.560
But above the grey land and the spasms of bleak dust which drift endlessly over it,

00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:56.080
you perceive after a moment the eyes of Dr. T. J. Eckelberg.

00:34:56.080 --> 00:35:01.000
The eyes of Dr. T. J. Eckelberg are blue and gigantic.

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:04.080
Their retinas are one-yard high.

00:35:04.080 --> 00:35:08.800
They look out of no face, but instead from a pair of enormous yellow spectacles which

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:12.360
pass over a non-existent nose.

00:35:12.360 --> 00:35:16.760
Evidently some wild wag of an oculist set them there to fatten his practice in the

00:35:16.760 --> 00:35:22.800
borough of queens and then sank down himself into eternal blindness or forgot them and moved

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:29.160
away, but his eyes dimmed a little by many paintless days under sun and rain brewed

00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:32.920
on over the solemn dumping ground.

00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:39.400
The valley of ashes is bounded on one side by a small, foul river, and when the drawbridge

00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:44.120
is up to let barges through the passengers on waiting trains can stare at the dismal

00:35:44.120 --> 00:35:46.760
scene for as long as half an hour.

00:35:46.760 --> 00:35:51.040
There is always a halt there of at least a minute, and it was because of this that I

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:54.720
first met Tom Buchanan's mistress.

00:35:54.720 --> 00:35:58.760
The fact that he had one was insisted upon wherever he was known.

00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:02.960
His acquaintances resented the fact that he turned up in popular restaurants with her and

00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:07.600
leaving her at a table sauntered about chatting with whomsoever he knew.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:12.120
Though I was curious to see her, I had no desire to meet her, but I did.

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:16.160
I went up to New York with Tom on the train one afternoon, and when we stopped by the ash-heap

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:20.660
seat, he jumped to his feet and taking hold of my elbow, literally forced me from the

00:36:20.660 --> 00:36:21.660
car.

00:36:21.660 --> 00:36:23.440
We're getting off, he insisted.

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:25.760
I want you to meet my girl.

00:36:25.760 --> 00:36:30.120
I think he tanked up a good deal at luncheon, and his determination to have my company boarded

00:36:30.120 --> 00:36:31.600
on violence.

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:36.560
The super-siliest assumption was that on Sunday afternoon I had nothing better to do.

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:41.120
I followed him over a low white-washed railroad fence, and we walked back a hundred yards

00:36:41.120 --> 00:36:44.720
along the road under Dr. Eckerberg's persistent stare.

00:36:44.720 --> 00:36:48.880
The only building in sight was a small block of yellow brick sitting on the edge of the

00:36:48.880 --> 00:36:54.600
wasteland, a sort of compact, mainstreamed, ministering to it, and contiguous to absolutely

00:36:54.600 --> 00:36:55.760
nothing.

00:36:55.760 --> 00:36:59.900
One of the three shops it contained was for rent, and another was an all-night restaurant

00:36:59.900 --> 00:37:02.520
approached by a trail of ashes.

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:04.560
The third was a garage.

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:07.840
Repairs, George B. Wilson, cars, bought and sold.

00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:11.040
The interior was unprostorous and bare.

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:16.040
The only car visible was the dust-covered wreck of a Ford, which crouched in a dim corner.

00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:20.600
It had occurred to me that this shadow of a garage must be a blind, and that sumptuous

00:37:20.600 --> 00:37:25.720
and romantic apartments were concealed overhead when the proprietor himself appeared in the door

00:37:25.720 --> 00:37:29.240
of an office wiping his hands on a piece of waste.

00:37:29.240 --> 00:37:34.240
He was a blonde, spiritless man, anemic and faintly handsome.

00:37:34.240 --> 00:37:38.880
When he saw us a damp gleam of hope sprang into his light blue eyes.

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:42.800
Hello, Wilson, old man, said Tom, slapping him joevely on the shoulder.

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:44.360
How's business?

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:48.400
I can't complain, answered Wilson, uncomfensingly.

00:37:48.400 --> 00:37:50.640
When are you going to sell me that car?

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:53.600
Next week I've got my man working on it now.

00:37:53.600 --> 00:37:55.240
Our expertise slowed, only.

00:37:55.240 --> 00:37:57.440
No, he doesn't, said Tom coldly.

00:37:57.440 --> 00:38:00.800
And if you feel that way about it, maybe I'd better sell it somewhere else after all.

00:38:00.800 --> 00:38:03.400
I don't mean that, explained Wilson quickly.

00:38:03.400 --> 00:38:09.680
I just meant his voice faded off, and Tom glanced impatiently around the garage.

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:13.880
Then I heard footsteps on a stairs, and in a moment the thick-ish figure of a woman

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:16.720
blocked out the light from the office door.

00:38:16.720 --> 00:38:22.320
She was in the middle thirties and faintly stout, but she carried her surplus flesh sensuously

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:24.160
as some women can.

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:31.040
Her face above a spotted dress of dark blue-craped a sheen contained no facet or gleam of beauty,

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:34.560
but there was an immediately perceptible vitality about her.

00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:38.800
As if the nerves of her body were continually smoldering.

00:38:38.800 --> 00:38:43.520
She smiled slowly, and walking through her husband as if he were a ghost shook hands

00:38:43.520 --> 00:38:46.160
with Tom looking him flush in the eye.

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:51.840
Then she wet her lips, and without turning around spoke to her husband in a soft coarse voice.

00:38:51.840 --> 00:38:54.880
Get some chairs wet on you so somebody can sit down.

00:38:54.880 --> 00:39:00.160
Oh, sure, agreed Wilson hurriedly, and went to the little office mingling immediately

00:39:00.160 --> 00:39:02.200
with the cement collar of the walls.

00:39:02.200 --> 00:39:07.320
A white, ashen dust veiled his dark suit and his pale hair as it veiled everything in the

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:11.960
vicinity, except his wife, who moved close to Tom.

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:14.320
I want to see you, Sir Tom, and gently.

00:39:14.320 --> 00:39:15.720
Get on the next train.

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:16.720
All right?

00:39:16.720 --> 00:39:19.600
I'll meet you by the newsstand on the lower level.

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:23.320
She nodded and moved away from him, just as George Wilson emerged with two chairs from

00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:25.160
his office door.

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:28.000
We waited for her down the road and out of sight.

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:32.640
It was a few days before the 4th of July, and the grey, scrawny Italian child was setting

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:36.120
torpedoes in a row along the railroad track.

00:39:36.120 --> 00:39:40.600
Terrible place, isn't it, Sir Tom, exchanging a fron with Dr. Ekelberg?

00:39:40.600 --> 00:39:41.600
Awful.

00:39:41.600 --> 00:39:43.240
It does her good to get away.

00:39:43.240 --> 00:39:45.240
Doesn't her husband object?

00:39:45.240 --> 00:39:46.240
Wilson?

00:39:46.240 --> 00:39:48.440
Anything she goes to see her sister in New York.

00:39:48.440 --> 00:39:51.440
He so dumbly doesn't know he's alive.

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:55.840
So Tom, you can and his girl and I went up together to New York.

00:39:55.840 --> 00:40:00.800
Or not quite together for Mrs. Wilson sent to screetly in another car.

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:05.960
Tom deferred that much to the sensibilities of those east eggers who might be on the train.

00:40:05.960 --> 00:40:10.480
She had changed her dress to a brown-figured muslin, which stretched tighter, where her

00:40:10.480 --> 00:40:14.720
rather wide hips as Tom helped her to the platform in New York.

00:40:14.720 --> 00:40:19.760
At the newsstand, she bought a copy of Town Tatl, and a moving picture magazine.

00:40:19.760 --> 00:40:24.040
And in the station drugstore, some cold cream and a small flask of perfume.

00:40:24.040 --> 00:40:25.960
She wears in the solemn echoing drive.

00:40:25.960 --> 00:40:31.560
She let four taxi cabs drive away before she's selected a new one, lavender colored with

00:40:31.560 --> 00:40:33.360
gray upholstery.

00:40:33.360 --> 00:40:37.480
And in this, we slid out from the mass of the station, and she took loads from the window

00:40:37.480 --> 00:40:40.520
and leaning forward, tapped on the front glass.

00:40:40.520 --> 00:40:42.880
I want to get one of those dogs, she said earnestly.

00:40:42.880 --> 00:40:44.240
I want to get one for the apartment.

00:40:44.240 --> 00:40:46.640
They're nice to have a dog.

00:40:46.640 --> 00:40:52.520
We backed up to a gray old man who bore an absurd resemblance to John D. Rockefeller.

00:40:52.520 --> 00:40:57.360
And a basket swung from his neck coward, a dozen very recent puppies of an indeterminate

00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:58.360
breed.

00:40:58.360 --> 00:40:59.680
What kind are they?

00:40:59.680 --> 00:41:03.080
Ask Mrs. Wilson eagerly as he came to the taxi window.

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:05.360
All kinds of what kind you want, lady?

00:41:05.360 --> 00:41:09.080
I'd like to get one of those police dogs, I don't suppose you got that kind.

00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:13.600
The man peered doubtfully into the basket, plunged in his hand, and drew one up wriggling

00:41:13.600 --> 00:41:15.120
by the back of the neck.

00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:17.840
That's no police dog, said Tom.

00:41:17.840 --> 00:41:21.960
No, it's not exactly a police dog, said the man with disappointment in his voice.

00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:23.720
It's more of an air-dail.

00:41:23.720 --> 00:41:27.040
He passed his hand over the brown wash rag of a back.

00:41:27.040 --> 00:41:31.400
Look at that coat, some coat, that's a dog I'll never bother you with catching cold.

00:41:31.400 --> 00:41:34.400
I think it's cute, said Mrs. Wilson, enthusiastically.

00:41:34.400 --> 00:41:35.400
How much is it?

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:40.920
That dog, he looked at it admiringly, that dog will cost you ten dollars.

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:45.240
The air-dail, undoubtedly there was, an air-dail concerned in it somewhere, though its feet

00:41:45.240 --> 00:41:50.280
were startlingly white, changed hands and settled down into Mrs. Wilson's lap, where

00:41:50.280 --> 00:41:53.200
she fondled the weather-proof coat with rapture.

00:41:53.200 --> 00:41:55.280
Is it a boy or a girl?

00:41:55.280 --> 00:41:56.640
She asked delicately.

00:41:56.640 --> 00:41:58.920
That dog, that dog's a boy.

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:00.800
It's a bitch, said Tom, to size of the leaf.

00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:04.280
Here's your money, going by ten more dogs with it.

00:42:04.280 --> 00:42:09.400
We drove over to Fifth Avenue, so warm and soft, almost pestilous on the summer, Sunday

00:42:09.400 --> 00:42:13.760
afternoon, that I wouldn't have been surprised to see a great flock of white sheep turn the

00:42:13.760 --> 00:42:14.760
corner.

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:17.360
Hold on, I said, I have to leave you here.

00:42:17.360 --> 00:42:19.840
No, you don't, interposed Tom quickly.

00:42:19.840 --> 00:42:23.120
Turtle, it'll be heard if you don't come up to the apartment, won't you, mortal?

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:24.440
Come on, she urged.

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:26.440
I'll telephone my sister, Catherine.

00:42:26.440 --> 00:42:29.360
She said to be very beautiful by people who ought to know.

00:42:29.360 --> 00:42:33.240
Well, I'd like to, but we went on.

00:42:33.240 --> 00:42:36.760
Cutting back again over the park toward the West Hunters.

00:42:36.760 --> 00:42:41.560
At a hundred and fifty-eighth street, the cab stopped at one slice in a long white cake

00:42:41.560 --> 00:42:43.640
of apartment houses.

00:42:43.640 --> 00:42:47.400
Throwing a regal, homecoming glance around the neighborhood, Mrs. Wilson gathered up her

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:51.320
dog and her other purchases and went hottily in.

00:42:51.320 --> 00:42:54.720
I'm going to have the monkeys come up, she announced, as we rose in the elevator, and

00:42:54.720 --> 00:42:57.960
of course I got a call up my sister, too.

00:42:57.960 --> 00:43:00.400
The apartment was on the top floor.

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:05.560
A small living room, a small dining room, a small bedroom and a bath.

00:43:05.560 --> 00:43:09.760
The living room was crowded to the doors with a set of tapestry furniture entirely too large

00:43:09.760 --> 00:43:14.320
for it, so that to move about was to stumble continually over scenes of lady swinging in

00:43:14.320 --> 00:43:16.600
the gardens of Versailles.

00:43:16.600 --> 00:43:22.120
The only picture was an over and large photograph apparently a hen sitting on a blurred rock.

00:43:22.120 --> 00:43:26.320
Looked at from a distance, however, the hen resolved itself into a bonnet, and the countenance

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:30.480
of a stout old lady beamed down into the room.

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:34.880
Several old copies of town tattled, they on the table, together with a copy of Simon called

00:43:34.880 --> 00:43:39.280
Peter, and some of the small scandal magazines of Broadway.

00:43:39.280 --> 00:43:41.480
Mrs. Wilson was first concerned with the dog.

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:46.320
A reluctant elevator boy went for a box full of straw and some milk to which he added

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:51.600
on his own initiative a tin of large, hard dog biscuits, one of which decomposed empathetically

00:43:51.600 --> 00:43:54.280
in the saucer of milk all afternoon.

00:43:54.280 --> 00:43:58.560
Meanwhile Tom brought out a bottle of whiskey from a locked bureau door.

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:05.000
I have been drunk just twice in my life, and the second time was that afternoon.

00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:10.880
So everything that happened has a dim hazy cast over it, although until after eight o'clock

00:44:10.880 --> 00:44:13.800
the apartment was full of cheerful sun.

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:18.320
Coming on Tom's lap Mrs. Wilson called up several people on the telephone.

00:44:18.320 --> 00:44:22.480
Then there were no cigarettes, and I went out to buy some of the drugstore on the corner.

00:44:22.480 --> 00:44:26.600
When I came back they had disappeared, so I sat down discreetly in the living room and

00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:29.040
read a chapter of Simon called Peter.

00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:33.800
Either it was terrible stuff or the whiskey distorted Tom and Mertle after the first drink

00:44:33.800 --> 00:44:37.200
Mrs. Wilson and I called each other by our first names.

00:44:37.200 --> 00:44:41.880
Reappeared, company commenced to arrive at the apartment door.

00:44:41.880 --> 00:44:47.640
The sister Catherine was a slender, worldly girl of about thirty, with a solid, sticky

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:51.920
barb of red hair and a complexion powdered milky white.

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:57.400
Her eyebrows had been plucked and then drawn on again at a more rakeish angle, but the

00:44:57.400 --> 00:45:01.960
efforts of nature toward the restoration of the older line meant gave a blurred air to

00:45:01.960 --> 00:45:03.520
her face.

00:45:03.520 --> 00:45:09.040
When she moved about there was an incessant clicking, as a new marble pottery bracelets jingled

00:45:09.040 --> 00:45:11.320
up and down upon her arms.

00:45:11.320 --> 00:45:16.720
She came in with such a proprietary hasten, looked around so possessively at the furniture

00:45:16.720 --> 00:45:21.480
that I wondered if she lived here, but when I asked her she laughed immororately, repeated

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:25.880
my question aloud and told me she lived with a girlfriend at a hotel.

00:45:25.880 --> 00:45:30.640
Mr. McKee was a pale feminine man from the flat below.

00:45:30.640 --> 00:45:35.000
He had just shaved, for there was a white spot of leather on his cheekbone and he was most

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:38.120
respectful in his greeting to everyone in the room.

00:45:38.120 --> 00:45:43.320
He informed me that he was in the artistic game, and I gathered later that he was a photographer

00:45:43.320 --> 00:45:48.200
and had made the dim enlargement of Mrs. Wilson's mother which hovered like an ectoplasm on

00:45:48.200 --> 00:45:49.760
the wall.

00:45:49.760 --> 00:45:53.680
His wife was shrill, languid, handsome and horrible.

00:45:53.680 --> 00:45:58.040
She told me with pride that her husband had photographed her 127 times since they had

00:45:58.040 --> 00:46:00.440
been married.

00:46:00.440 --> 00:46:06.160
Mrs. Wilson had changed her costume sometime before and was now retired in an elaborate afternoon

00:46:06.160 --> 00:46:10.440
dress of cream-colored chiffon which gave out a continual rustle that she swept about

00:46:10.440 --> 00:46:11.960
the room.

00:46:11.960 --> 00:46:15.480
With the influence of the dress, her personality had also undergone a change.

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:20.720
The intense vitality that had been so remarkable in the garage was converted into impressive

00:46:20.720 --> 00:46:21.720
hot tour.

00:46:21.720 --> 00:46:27.880
Her laughter, her gestures, her assertions became more violently affected moment by moment.

00:46:27.880 --> 00:46:32.200
And as she expanded, the room grew smaller around her.

00:46:32.200 --> 00:46:37.560
So she seemed to be revolving on a noisy creaking pivot through the smokey air.

00:46:37.560 --> 00:46:42.360
My dear, she told her sister in a high, mincing shout.

00:46:42.360 --> 00:46:44.720
Most of these fellows of cheat every time.

00:46:44.720 --> 00:46:46.160
All they think of is money.

00:46:46.160 --> 00:46:49.400
I had a woman up here last week to look at my feet and when she gave me the bill you'd

00:46:49.400 --> 00:46:52.520
have thought she'd had my appendicitis out.

00:46:52.520 --> 00:46:55.480
What was the name of the woman, as Mrs. McKee?

00:46:55.480 --> 00:46:59.280
Mrs. Eberhardt, she goes around looking at people's feet in their own homes.

00:46:59.280 --> 00:47:01.440
I like your dress, remarked Mrs. McKee.

00:47:01.440 --> 00:47:03.240
I think it's adorable.

00:47:03.240 --> 00:47:07.280
Mrs. Wilson rejected the compliment by raising her eyebrow in disdain.

00:47:07.280 --> 00:47:09.520
It's just a crazy old thing, she said.

00:47:09.520 --> 00:47:13.040
I just slip it on sometimes when I don't care what I look like.

00:47:13.040 --> 00:47:17.520
But it looks wonderful on you, if you know what I mean, pursued Mrs. McKee.

00:47:17.520 --> 00:47:21.600
If Chester could only get you in that pose, I think it could make something of it.

00:47:21.600 --> 00:47:26.640
We all looked in silence at Mrs. Wilson, who removed a strand of hair from over her eyes

00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:29.280
and looked back at us with a brilliant smile.

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:34.400
Mr. McKee regarded her intently with his head on one side and then moved his hand back

00:47:34.400 --> 00:47:36.960
and forth slowly in front of his face.

00:47:36.960 --> 00:47:39.560
I should change the light, he said, after a moment.

00:47:39.560 --> 00:47:41.760
I'd like to bring out the modeling of the features.

00:47:41.760 --> 00:47:43.760
I wouldn't need to change it back.

00:47:43.760 --> 00:47:48.880
I think it's her husband said, and we all looked at the subject again.

00:47:48.880 --> 00:47:53.040
Whereupon, Tom, you can and yawned audibly in God to his feet.

00:47:53.040 --> 00:47:55.480
You, McKee's, I have something to drink, he said.

00:47:55.480 --> 00:47:59.440
It's a more ice and mineral water, a mortal for everybody goes to sleep.

00:47:59.440 --> 00:48:04.240
I told that boy about the ice, a mortal raised her eyebrows and despair at the shiftlessness

00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:05.920
of the lower orders.

00:48:05.920 --> 00:48:09.240
These people, we have to keep after them all the time.

00:48:09.240 --> 00:48:11.960
She looked at me and laughed pointlessly.

00:48:11.960 --> 00:48:16.400
Then she flounced over to the dog, kissed it with ecstasy and swept into the kitchen, implying

00:48:16.400 --> 00:48:19.280
that a dozen chefs awaited her orders there.

00:48:19.280 --> 00:48:24.400
I've done some nice things out on Long Island, Mr. McKee.

00:48:24.400 --> 00:48:26.280
Tom looked at him blankly.

00:48:26.280 --> 00:48:28.880
Two of them we have framed downstairs.

00:48:28.880 --> 00:48:31.280
Two what, demanded Tom.

00:48:31.280 --> 00:48:38.200
Two studies, one of them I call, Montauk Point, the goals, and the other I call, Montauk Point,

00:48:38.200 --> 00:48:39.720
the sea.

00:48:39.720 --> 00:48:42.440
The sister Catherine sat down beside me on the couch.

00:48:42.440 --> 00:48:46.640
Do you live down on Long Island, too, she inquired?

00:48:46.640 --> 00:48:48.360
I live at West Egg.

00:48:48.360 --> 00:48:49.360
Really?

00:48:49.360 --> 00:48:52.200
I was down there at a party about a month ago, at a man named Gatsby's.

00:48:52.200 --> 00:48:53.600
Do you know him?

00:48:53.600 --> 00:48:55.120
I live next door to him.

00:48:55.120 --> 00:48:59.080
Wow, they say he's a nephew or a cousin of Kaiser Wilhelms.

00:48:59.080 --> 00:49:00.760
That's where all his money comes from.

00:49:00.760 --> 00:49:01.760
Really?

00:49:01.760 --> 00:49:02.760
She nodded.

00:49:02.760 --> 00:49:03.960
I'm scared of him.

00:49:03.960 --> 00:49:06.680
I'd hate to have him get anything on me.

00:49:06.680 --> 00:49:11.160
This absorbing information about my neighbor was interrupted by Mrs. McKee's pointing suddenly

00:49:11.160 --> 00:49:12.160
at Catherine.

00:49:12.160 --> 00:49:15.160
Chester, I think you could do something with her.

00:49:15.160 --> 00:49:20.280
She broke out, but Mr. McKee only nodded in a bored way, and turned his attention to Tom.

00:49:20.280 --> 00:49:23.280
I'd like to do more work on Long Island if I could get the entry.

00:49:23.280 --> 00:49:26.200
All I ask is that they should give me a start.

00:49:26.200 --> 00:49:30.560
Ask Myrtle said Tom, breaking into a short stout of laughter, as Mrs. Wilson entered with

00:49:30.560 --> 00:49:34.280
a tray, she'll give you a letter of introduction, won't you, Myrtle?

00:49:34.280 --> 00:49:35.280
Do what?

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:36.880
She asked, startled.

00:49:36.880 --> 00:49:40.680
You'll give McKee a letter of introduction to your husband so he can do some studies of

00:49:40.680 --> 00:49:41.680
him.

00:49:41.680 --> 00:49:44.360
His lips moved silently for a moment as he invented.

00:49:44.360 --> 00:49:48.120
George B. Wilson at the gasoline pump or something like that.

00:49:48.120 --> 00:49:49.120
Catherine lean.

00:49:49.120 --> 00:49:51.040
He's a man who can't stand me and whispered in my ear.

00:49:51.040 --> 00:49:54.120
Neither of them can stand the person they're married to.

00:49:54.120 --> 00:49:55.120
Can't they?

00:49:55.120 --> 00:49:56.360
Can't stand them.

00:49:56.360 --> 00:49:58.360
She looked at Myrtle and then at Tom.

00:49:58.360 --> 00:50:01.640
What I say is why go on living with them if they can't stand them.

00:50:01.640 --> 00:50:05.400
If I was them, I'd get a divorce and get married to each other right away.

00:50:05.400 --> 00:50:07.560
Doesn't she like Wilson, either?

00:50:07.560 --> 00:50:09.200
The answer to this was unexpected.

00:50:09.200 --> 00:50:13.760
It came from Myrtle who had overheard the question and it was violent and obscene.

00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:16.560
You see, cried Catherine triumphantly.

00:50:16.560 --> 00:50:17.960
She lowered her voice again.

00:50:17.960 --> 00:50:19.920
It's really his wife that's keeping them apart.

00:50:19.920 --> 00:50:22.880
She's a Catholic and they don't believe in divorce.

00:50:22.880 --> 00:50:27.840
Daisy was not a Catholic and I was a little shocked at the elaborateness of the lie.

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:30.160
When they do get married, continued Catherine.

00:50:30.160 --> 00:50:32.960
They're going west to live for a while until it blows over.

00:50:32.960 --> 00:50:35.640
It'd be more discreet to go to Europe.

00:50:35.640 --> 00:50:37.560
Oh, do you like Europe?

00:50:37.560 --> 00:50:38.800
She exclaimed surprisingly.

00:50:38.800 --> 00:50:41.160
I just got back from Monte Carlo.

00:50:41.160 --> 00:50:42.360
Really?

00:50:42.360 --> 00:50:43.360
Just last year?

00:50:43.360 --> 00:50:44.360
I went over there.

00:50:44.360 --> 00:50:45.360
No.

00:50:45.360 --> 00:50:46.360
We just went to Monte Carlo and back.

00:50:46.360 --> 00:50:48.160
We went by way of Marseille.

00:50:48.160 --> 00:50:52.760
We had over $1,200 when we started but we got gipped out at all in two days in the private

00:50:52.760 --> 00:50:53.760
rooms.

00:50:53.760 --> 00:50:54.760
We had an awful time getting back.

00:50:54.760 --> 00:50:58.360
I can tell you how I hated that town.

00:50:58.360 --> 00:51:04.280
The late afternoon sky bloomed in the window for a moment like the blue honey of the Mediterranean.

00:51:04.280 --> 00:51:07.200
Then the shrill voice of Mrs. McKee called me back into the room.

00:51:07.200 --> 00:51:08.800
I almost made a mistake too.

00:51:08.800 --> 00:51:10.320
She declared vigorously.

00:51:10.320 --> 00:51:12.760
I almost married a little kikud bin after me for years.

00:51:12.760 --> 00:51:14.080
I knew it was below me.

00:51:14.080 --> 00:51:17.680
He kept saying to me, Lucille, that man's way below you, but if I hadn't met Chester, he

00:51:17.680 --> 00:51:19.080
had got me sure.

00:51:19.080 --> 00:51:23.440
Yes, but listen, said Mertel Wilson nodding her head up and down.

00:51:23.440 --> 00:51:24.960
At least you didn't marry him.

00:51:24.960 --> 00:51:26.440
I know I didn't.

00:51:26.440 --> 00:51:29.800
Well, I married him, said Mertel, ambiguously.

00:51:29.800 --> 00:51:32.920
And that's the difference between your case and mine.

00:51:32.920 --> 00:51:35.040
Why did you, Mertel, demanded Catherine?

00:51:35.040 --> 00:51:36.920
Nobody forced you to.

00:51:36.920 --> 00:51:37.920
Mertel considered.

00:51:37.920 --> 00:51:42.200
I married him because I thought he was a gentleman, she said finally.

00:51:42.200 --> 00:51:47.000
I thought he knew something about breeding, but he wasn't fit to lick my shoe.

00:51:47.000 --> 00:51:49.920
You were crazy about him for a while, said Catherine.

00:51:49.920 --> 00:51:54.160
Crazy about him, cried Mertel incredulously, who said I was crazy about him.

00:51:54.160 --> 00:51:58.240
I never was any more crazy about him than I was about that man there.

00:51:58.240 --> 00:52:01.440
She pointed suddenly at me, and everyone looked at me accusingly.

00:52:01.440 --> 00:52:05.960
I tried to show by my expression that I had played no part in her past.

00:52:05.960 --> 00:52:08.920
The only crazy I was was when I married him.

00:52:08.920 --> 00:52:10.520
I knew right away I made a mistake.

00:52:10.520 --> 00:52:14.480
He borrowed somebody's best suit to get married in and never even told me about it, and the

00:52:14.480 --> 00:52:17.160
man came after it one day when he was out.

00:52:17.160 --> 00:52:18.880
She looked around to see who was listening.

00:52:18.880 --> 00:52:21.560
Oh, is that your suit, I said?

00:52:21.560 --> 00:52:24.960
This is the first I ever heard about it, but I gave it to him, and then I laid down and

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:27.880
cried to beat the band all afternoon.

00:52:27.880 --> 00:52:31.200
She really ought to get away from him, resumed Catherine to me.

00:52:31.200 --> 00:52:35.640
They've been living over that garage for 11 years, and Tom's the first sweetie she ever

00:52:35.640 --> 00:52:37.160
had.

00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:42.920
The bottle of whiskey, a second one, was now in constant demand by all present excepting

00:52:42.920 --> 00:52:47.120
Catherine, who felt just as good on nothing at all.

00:52:47.120 --> 00:52:51.480
Tom rang for the janitor and sent him for some celebrated sandwiches, which were a complete

00:52:51.480 --> 00:52:53.320
supper in themselves.

00:52:53.320 --> 00:52:57.600
I wanted to get out and walk eastward toward the park through the soft twilight, but each

00:52:57.600 --> 00:53:02.560
time I tried to go I became entangled in some wild, strident argument, which pulled me

00:53:02.560 --> 00:53:05.600
back as if with ropes into my chair.

00:53:05.600 --> 00:53:10.800
And high over the city our line of yellow windows must have contributed their share of human

00:53:10.800 --> 00:53:17.280
secrecy to the casual watcher in the darkening streets, and I was him too, looking up and

00:53:17.280 --> 00:53:18.440
wondering.

00:53:18.440 --> 00:53:25.240
I was within and without simultaneously enchanted and repelled by the inexhaustible variety

00:53:25.240 --> 00:53:27.040
of life.

00:53:27.040 --> 00:53:31.720
Myrtle pulled her chair close to mine, and suddenly her warm breath poured over me the story

00:53:31.720 --> 00:53:34.200
of her first meeting with Tom.

00:53:34.200 --> 00:53:38.000
It was on the two little seats facing each other that are always the last ones left on

00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:39.000
the train.

00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:42.040
I was going up to New York to see my sister and spend the night.

00:53:42.040 --> 00:53:46.120
He had on a dress suit and patent leather shoes, and I couldn't keep my eyes off him, but

00:53:46.120 --> 00:53:49.720
every time he looked at me I had to pretend to be looking at the advertisement over his

00:53:49.720 --> 00:53:50.720
head.

00:53:50.720 --> 00:53:55.120
When we came into the station he was next to me in his white shirt front pressed against

00:53:55.120 --> 00:53:59.440
my arm, and so I told him I'd have to call a policeman, but he knew I lied.

00:53:59.440 --> 00:54:03.280
I was so excited that when I got into a taxi with him I didn't hardly know I wasn't

00:54:03.280 --> 00:54:05.400
getting into a subway train.

00:54:05.400 --> 00:54:09.080
All I kept thinking about over and over was you can't live forever, you can't live

00:54:09.080 --> 00:54:10.080
forever.

00:54:10.080 --> 00:54:15.040
If you turn to Mrs. McKee in the room rang full of her artificial laughter.

00:54:15.040 --> 00:54:19.000
My dear, she cried, I'm going to give you this dress as soon as I'm through with it.

00:54:19.000 --> 00:54:21.240
I've got to get another one tomorrow.

00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:23.640
I'm going to make a list of all the things I've got to get.

00:54:23.640 --> 00:54:27.640
A massage, you know, a wave and a collar for the dog, and one of those cute little ass

00:54:27.640 --> 00:54:31.920
trays where you'd touch a spring and a wreath with a black silk bow from mother's grave

00:54:31.920 --> 00:54:34.040
that'll last all summer.

00:54:34.040 --> 00:54:37.440
I got to write down a list where I won't forget all the things I got to do.

00:54:37.440 --> 00:54:41.640
Almost immediately afterward I looked at my watch and found it was ten.

00:54:41.640 --> 00:54:46.600
Mr. McKee was asleep on a chair with his fists clenched in his lap, like a photograph of

00:54:46.600 --> 00:54:48.400
a man of action.

00:54:48.400 --> 00:54:52.360
Taking out my hand could sheep by a wipe from his cheek, the remains of the spot of dry

00:54:52.360 --> 00:54:55.960
lather that had worried me all the afternoon.

00:54:55.960 --> 00:55:01.040
The little dog was sitting on the table looking with blind eyes through the smoke, and from

00:55:01.040 --> 00:55:03.880
time to time groaning faintly.

00:55:03.880 --> 00:55:08.600
People disappeared, reappeared, made plans to go somewhere, and then lost each other,

00:55:08.600 --> 00:55:12.160
searched for each other, found each other a few feet away.

00:55:12.160 --> 00:55:16.680
Sometime toward midnight, Tom Buchanan and Mrs. Wilson stood face to face, discussing

00:55:16.680 --> 00:55:22.080
an impassioned voices whether Mrs. Wilson had any right to mention Daisy's name.

00:55:22.080 --> 00:55:26.720
Daisy, Daisy, Daisy shouted Mrs. Wilson, I'll say it whenever I want to.

00:55:26.720 --> 00:55:27.960
Daisy, Daisy!

00:55:27.960 --> 00:55:33.200
Taking a short-deft movement, Tom Buchanan broke her nose with his open hand.

00:55:33.200 --> 00:55:38.680
Then there were bloody towels upon the bathroom floor, and women's voices scolding, and high

00:55:38.680 --> 00:55:43.040
over the confusion a long, broken wail of pain.

00:55:43.040 --> 00:55:47.400
Mr. McKee awoke from his doves and started in a dazed or the door.

00:55:47.400 --> 00:55:51.360
When he had gone half way he turned around and stared at the scene.

00:55:51.360 --> 00:55:56.320
His wife and Catherine scolding and consoling as they stumbled here and there among the crowded

00:55:56.320 --> 00:56:01.480
furniture with articles of aid, and the despairing figure on the couch bleeding fluently

00:56:01.480 --> 00:56:06.400
and trying to spread a copy of town-tattle over the tapestry scenes of Versailles.

00:56:06.400 --> 00:56:11.960
Then Mr. McKee turned and continued on out the door, taking my hat from the chandelier

00:56:11.960 --> 00:56:12.960
I followed.

00:56:12.960 --> 00:56:18.880
Come to lunch someday, we suggested, as we groaned down in the elevator.

00:56:18.880 --> 00:56:19.880
Where?

00:56:19.880 --> 00:56:20.880
Anywhere.

00:56:20.880 --> 00:56:23.000
Keep your hands off a lever, snap the elevator boy.

00:56:23.000 --> 00:56:26.200
I beg your pardon, Mr. McKee with dignity.

00:56:26.200 --> 00:56:28.360
I didn't know I was touching it.

00:56:28.360 --> 00:56:29.520
All right, I agreed.

00:56:29.520 --> 00:56:31.120
I'll be glad to.

00:56:31.120 --> 00:56:35.880
I was standing beside his bed and he was sitting up between the sheets clad in his underwear

00:56:35.880 --> 00:56:38.720
with a great portfolio in his hands.

00:56:38.720 --> 00:56:40.680
Beauty in the beast?

00:56:40.680 --> 00:56:41.680
Loneliness?

00:56:41.680 --> 00:56:44.480
Old, the grocery-horse?

00:56:44.480 --> 00:56:46.760
Broken bridge?

00:56:46.760 --> 00:56:52.480
Then I was lying half asleep in the cold lower level of the Pennsylvania station, staring

00:56:52.480 --> 00:56:57.040
at the morning tribune and waiting for the four o'clock train.

00:56:57.040 --> 00:56:59.560
CHAPTER III

00:56:59.560 --> 00:57:03.520
There was music from my neighbor's house through the summer nights.

00:57:03.520 --> 00:57:09.000
In his blue gardens, men and girls came and went like malls among the whisperings and

00:57:09.000 --> 00:57:11.480
the champagne and the stars.

00:57:11.480 --> 00:57:16.560
At high tide in the afternoon I watched his guests diving from the tower of his raft,

00:57:16.560 --> 00:57:21.360
or taking the sun on the hot sand of his beach, while his two motorboats slit the waters

00:57:21.360 --> 00:57:26.160
of the sound, drawing aqua plains over cataracts of foam.

00:57:26.160 --> 00:57:30.800
On weekends his rolls-roast became an omnibus, bearing parties too and from the city between

00:57:30.800 --> 00:57:36.440
nine in the morning and long past midnight while his station wagon scampered like a brisk yellow

00:57:36.440 --> 00:57:39.120
bug to meet all trains.

00:57:39.120 --> 00:57:44.000
And on Mondays, eight servants, including an extra gardener, toiled all day with mobs

00:57:44.000 --> 00:57:49.160
and scrubbing brushes and hammers and garden shears, repairing the ravages of the night

00:57:49.160 --> 00:57:50.960
before.

00:57:50.960 --> 00:57:55.960
Every Friday, five crates of oranges and lemons arrived from a fruiterer in New York.

00:57:55.960 --> 00:58:01.120
Every Monday, these same oranges and lemons left his back door in a pyramid of popluss

00:58:01.120 --> 00:58:02.120
haves.

00:58:02.120 --> 00:58:06.000
There was a machine in the kitchen which could extract the juice of two hundred oranges

00:58:06.000 --> 00:58:11.640
in half an hour if a little button was pressed two hundred times by a butler's thumb.

00:58:11.640 --> 00:58:16.720
At least once a fortnight, a core of caterers came down with several hundred feet of canvas

00:58:16.720 --> 00:58:21.240
and enough colored lights to make a Christmas tree of Gatsby's enormous garden.

00:58:21.240 --> 00:58:26.600
On buffet tables garnished with glistening orders, spiced baked hams, crowded against salads

00:58:26.600 --> 00:58:32.680
of harlequin designs and pastry pigs and turkeys bewitched to a dark gold.

00:58:32.680 --> 00:58:37.880
In the main hall of bar with a real brass rail was set up and stocked with jeans and

00:58:37.880 --> 00:58:42.600
liquors and with cordials so long forgotten that most of his female guests were too young

00:58:42.600 --> 00:58:45.440
to know one from another.

00:58:45.440 --> 00:58:48.000
By seven o'clock the orchestra has arrived.

00:58:48.000 --> 00:58:53.960
No thin, five piece of hair but a whole pitful of oboes and trombones and saxophones and

00:58:53.960 --> 00:58:58.360
vials and cornets and piccolos and low and high drums.

00:58:58.360 --> 00:59:02.480
The last swimmers have come in from the beach now and are dressing upstairs.

00:59:02.480 --> 00:59:08.080
The cars from New York are parked five deep in the drive and already the halls and salons

00:59:08.080 --> 00:59:14.680
and verandas are gaudy with primary colors and hair shorn in strange new ways and shawls

00:59:14.680 --> 00:59:17.280
beyond the dreams of Castile.

00:59:17.280 --> 00:59:23.040
The bar is in full swing and floating rounds of cocktails permeate the garden outside until

00:59:23.040 --> 00:59:28.760
the air is alive with chatter and laughter and casual innuendo and introductions forgotten

00:59:28.760 --> 00:59:34.560
on the spot and enthusiastic meetings between women who never knew each other's names.

00:59:34.560 --> 00:59:39.760
The lights grow brighter as the earth lurches away from the sun and now the orchestra is

00:59:39.760 --> 00:59:44.960
playing yellow cocktail music and the opera voices pitches a key hire.

00:59:44.960 --> 00:59:49.320
Laughter is easier minute by minute spilled with prodigality tipped out at a cheerful

00:59:49.320 --> 00:59:50.320
word.

00:59:50.320 --> 00:59:55.240
The groups change more swiftly, swell with new arrivals, dissolve and form in the same

00:59:55.240 --> 00:59:56.240
breath.

00:59:56.240 --> 01:00:00.560
Already there are wanderers, confident girls who weave here and there among the stouter

01:00:00.560 --> 01:00:06.080
and more stable become for a sharp joyous moment to the centre of a group and then excited

01:00:06.080 --> 01:00:12.020
with triumph glide on through the sea change of faces and voices and colour under the

01:00:12.020 --> 01:00:17.920
consis gypsies and trembling opal seizes a cocktail out of the air dumps it down for courage

01:00:17.920 --> 01:00:22.200
and moving her hands like frisco dances out alone on the canvas platform.

01:00:22.200 --> 01:00:27.000
A momentary hush the orchestra leader varies his rhythm obligingly for her and there is

01:00:27.000 --> 01:00:31.280
a burst of chatter as the erroneous news goes around that she is gilded a graze under

01:00:31.280 --> 01:00:33.200
study from the follies.

01:00:33.200 --> 01:00:37.240
The party has begun.

01:00:37.240 --> 01:00:41.160
I believe then on the first night I went to Gatsby's house so as one of the few guests

01:00:41.160 --> 01:00:43.480
who had actually been invited.

01:00:43.480 --> 01:00:46.080
People were not invited, they went there.

01:00:46.080 --> 01:00:49.640
They got into automobiles which bore them out to Long Island and somehow they ended

01:00:49.640 --> 01:00:51.560
up at Gatsby's door.

01:00:51.560 --> 01:00:54.960
Once there they were introduced by somebody who knew Gatsby and after that they conducted

01:00:54.960 --> 01:01:00.080
themselves according to the rules of behaviour associated with amusement parks.

01:01:00.080 --> 01:01:04.700
As they came and went without having that Gatsby at all came for the party with a simplicity

01:01:04.700 --> 01:01:08.000
of heart that was its own ticket of admission.

01:01:08.000 --> 01:01:10.560
I had been actually invited.

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:15.120
A chauffeur in a uniform of Robin's egg blue crossed my lawn early that Saturday morning

01:01:15.120 --> 01:01:18.040
with a surprisingly formal note from his employer.

01:01:18.040 --> 01:01:22.320
The honour would be entirely Gatsby's, it said, if I would attend his little party that

01:01:22.320 --> 01:01:23.320
night.

01:01:23.320 --> 01:01:27.680
You'd seen me several times and had intended to call on me long before but a peculiar

01:01:27.680 --> 01:01:34.320
combination of circumstances had prevented it, signed J. Gatsby in a majestic hand.

01:01:34.320 --> 01:01:38.760
Dressed up in white flannels I went over to his lawn a little after seven and wandered

01:01:38.760 --> 01:01:43.560
around rather illidies among swirls and eddies of people I didn't know.

01:01:43.560 --> 01:01:47.440
No here and there there was a face I had noticed on the commuting train.

01:01:47.440 --> 01:01:52.200
I was immediately struck by the number of young Englishmen darted about all well dressed,

01:01:52.200 --> 01:01:57.640
all looking a little hungry and all talking in low earnest voices to solid and prosper

01:01:57.640 --> 01:01:59.720
as Americans.

01:01:59.720 --> 01:02:04.040
I was sure that there were selling something bonds or insurance or automobiles.

01:02:04.040 --> 01:02:09.040
They were at least agonizingly aware of the easy money and the vicinity and convinced

01:02:09.040 --> 01:02:12.760
that it was theirs for a few words in the right key.

01:02:12.760 --> 01:02:16.160
As soon as I arrived I met an attempt to find my host but the two or three people of whom

01:02:16.160 --> 01:02:20.520
I asked his whereabouts stared at me in such an amazed way and denied so vehemently

01:02:20.520 --> 01:02:25.680
any knowledge of his movements that I slunk off in the direction of the cocktail table.

01:02:25.680 --> 01:02:29.360
The only place in the garden where a single man could linger without looking purposeless

01:02:29.360 --> 01:02:30.360
and alone.

01:02:30.360 --> 01:02:35.720
I was on my way to getting roaring drunk from sheer embarrassment when Jordan Baker came

01:02:35.720 --> 01:02:39.760
out of the house and stood at the head of the marble steps, meaning a little backward

01:02:39.760 --> 01:02:44.200
and looking with contemptuous interest down into the garden.

01:02:44.200 --> 01:02:48.240
Welcome or not I found it necessary to attach myself to someone before I should begin to

01:02:48.240 --> 01:02:51.360
address cordial remarks to the passengers by.

01:02:51.360 --> 01:02:56.640
Hello, I roared advancing toward her, my voice seemed unnaturally loud across the garden.

01:02:56.640 --> 01:03:01.240
I thought you might be here, she responded absolutely as I came up.

01:03:01.240 --> 01:03:05.720
I remembered you live next door to—she held my hand in personally as a promise that

01:03:05.720 --> 01:03:10.080
she'd take care of me in a minute and gave a year to two girls in twin yellow dresses

01:03:10.080 --> 01:03:12.200
who stopped at the foot of the steps.

01:03:12.200 --> 01:03:15.400
Hello, they cried together, sorry you didn't win.

01:03:15.400 --> 01:03:19.640
That was for the golf tournament she had lost in the finals the week before.

01:03:19.640 --> 01:03:23.080
You don't know who we are, said one of the girls in yellow, but we met you here about

01:03:23.080 --> 01:03:24.960
a month ago.

01:03:24.960 --> 01:03:29.920
You've died your hair since then, remarked Jordan and I started, but the girls had moved

01:03:29.920 --> 01:03:34.520
casually on, and her remark was addressed to the premature moon, produced like the

01:03:34.520 --> 01:03:37.440
sub-anode out of a caterer's basket.

01:03:37.440 --> 01:03:42.080
With Jordan's slender golden arm resting in mind, we descended the steps and sauntered

01:03:42.080 --> 01:03:43.480
about the garden.

01:03:43.480 --> 01:03:48.680
A tray of cocktails floated at us through the twilight, and we sat down at a table with

01:03:48.680 --> 01:03:52.720
the two girls in yellow and three men each one introduced to us as Mr. Mumble.

01:03:52.720 --> 01:03:55.040
Do you come to these parties, or last night?

01:03:55.040 --> 01:03:59.240
The one who had a magnificent dance at the girl in an alert, confident voice.

01:03:59.240 --> 01:04:00.440
She turned to her companion.

01:04:00.440 --> 01:04:02.360
Wasn't it for you, Lucille?

01:04:02.360 --> 01:04:04.560
It was for Lucille, too.

01:04:04.560 --> 01:04:06.360
I like to come, Lucille said.

01:04:06.360 --> 01:04:09.400
I never can't what I do, so I always have a good time.

01:04:09.400 --> 01:04:13.840
When I was here last, I tore my gown on a chair and he asked me my name and address.

01:04:13.840 --> 01:04:18.600
Inside of a week I got a package from Coriaze with a new evening gown in it.

01:04:18.600 --> 01:04:20.760
Did you keep it, as Jordan?

01:04:20.760 --> 01:04:21.760
Sure I did.

01:04:21.760 --> 01:04:25.240
I was going to wear it tonight, but it was too big in the bust and had to be altered.

01:04:25.240 --> 01:04:29.080
It was gas-blue with lavender beads, $265.

01:04:29.080 --> 01:04:33.520
There's something funny about a fellow that'll do a thing like that, said the other girl

01:04:33.520 --> 01:04:34.520
eagerly.

01:04:34.520 --> 01:04:36.520
He doesn't want any trouble with anybody.

01:04:36.520 --> 01:04:38.760
Who doesn't, I inquired?

01:04:38.760 --> 01:04:39.760
Gatsby!

01:04:39.760 --> 01:04:45.520
Somebody told me the two girls in Jordan leaned together confidentially.

01:04:45.520 --> 01:04:48.680
Somebody told me they thought he killed a man once.

01:04:48.680 --> 01:04:50.800
A thrill passed over all of us.

01:04:50.800 --> 01:04:53.640
The three Mr. Mumbles bent forward and listened eagerly.

01:04:53.640 --> 01:04:58.440
I don't think it's so much that argued Lucille skeptically.

01:04:58.440 --> 01:05:01.960
It's more that he was a German spy during the war.

01:05:01.960 --> 01:05:04.400
One of the men nodded in confirmation.

01:05:04.400 --> 01:05:06.600
I heard that from a man who knew all about him.

01:05:06.600 --> 01:05:09.000
Group up with him in Germany, he assured us positively.

01:05:09.000 --> 01:05:12.880
Oh, no, said the first girl, it couldn't be that because he was in the American army

01:05:12.880 --> 01:05:14.360
during the war.

01:05:14.360 --> 01:05:16.320
Those are credulities switched back to her.

01:05:16.320 --> 01:05:18.520
She leaned forward with enthusiasm.

01:05:18.520 --> 01:05:21.400
You look at him sometimes when he thinks nobody's looking at him.

01:05:21.400 --> 01:05:23.600
I'll bet he killed a man.

01:05:23.600 --> 01:05:25.600
She narrowed her eyes and shivered.

01:05:25.600 --> 01:05:27.200
Lucille shivered.

01:05:27.200 --> 01:05:29.680
We all turned and looked around for Gatsby.

01:05:29.680 --> 01:05:34.880
It was testimony to the romantic speculation he inspired that there were whispers about him,

01:05:34.880 --> 01:05:39.800
from those who had found little that it was necessary to whisper about in this world.

01:05:39.800 --> 01:05:44.600
The first supper, there would be another one after midnight, was now being served.

01:05:44.600 --> 01:05:48.240
And Jordan invited me to join her own party, who was spread around a table on the other

01:05:48.240 --> 01:05:49.760
side of the garden.

01:05:49.760 --> 01:05:54.200
There were three married couples, and Jordan's escort a persistent undergraduate, given to

01:05:54.200 --> 01:05:58.520
violent innuendo, and obviously under the impression that sooner or later Jordan was

01:05:58.520 --> 01:06:03.360
going to yield him up her person to a greater or lesser degree.

01:06:03.360 --> 01:06:08.000
Instead of rambling, this party had preserved a dignified homogeneity and assumed to itself

01:06:08.000 --> 01:06:12.120
the function of representing the state nobility of the countryside.

01:06:12.120 --> 01:06:18.960
East egg condescending to West egg, and carefully on guard against its spectroscopic gaity.

01:06:18.960 --> 01:06:25.200
Let's get out, whispered Jordan, after a somehow wasteful and inappropriate half hour.

01:06:25.200 --> 01:06:27.760
This is much too polite for me.

01:06:27.760 --> 01:06:31.680
We got up, and she explained that we were going to find the host I had never met him,

01:06:31.680 --> 01:06:32.680
she said.

01:06:32.680 --> 01:06:34.200
It was making me uneasy.

01:06:34.200 --> 01:06:38.440
The undergraduate nodded in a cynical melancholy way.

01:06:38.440 --> 01:06:42.400
The bar, where we glanced first, was crowded, but gaitsy was not there.

01:06:42.400 --> 01:06:46.320
She couldn't find him from the top of the steps, and he wasn't on the veranda.

01:06:46.320 --> 01:06:51.280
On a chance we tried an important-looking door, and walked into a high gothic library,

01:06:51.280 --> 01:06:57.400
panelled with carved English oak, and probably transported complete from some ruin overseas.

01:06:57.400 --> 01:07:03.480
A stout, middle-aged man, with enormous owl-eyed spectacles, was sitting somewhat drunk on

01:07:03.480 --> 01:07:09.160
the edge of a great table, staring with unsteady concentration at the shelves of books.

01:07:09.160 --> 01:07:13.480
As we entered a wheeled, excitedly around, an examined Jordan from head to foot.

01:07:13.480 --> 01:07:16.920
What do you think, they demanded impetuously?

01:07:16.920 --> 01:07:18.160
About what?

01:07:18.160 --> 01:07:19.920
We waved his hand toward the bookshelves.

01:07:19.920 --> 01:07:20.920
About that!

01:07:20.920 --> 01:07:23.640
As a matter of fact, you needn't bother to ascertain.

01:07:23.640 --> 01:07:24.640
I ascertained.

01:07:24.640 --> 01:07:26.440
They're real.

01:07:26.440 --> 01:07:27.440
The books.

01:07:27.440 --> 01:07:28.440
He nodded.

01:07:28.440 --> 01:07:29.440
Absolutely real.

01:07:29.440 --> 01:07:31.440
Have pages and everything.

01:07:31.440 --> 01:07:33.760
I thought they'd be nice, durable cardboard.

01:07:33.760 --> 01:07:36.440
Matter of fact, they're absolutely real.

01:07:36.440 --> 01:07:37.440
Pages.

01:07:37.440 --> 01:07:39.200
And here, let me show you.

01:07:39.200 --> 01:07:43.960
Taking our skepticism for granted, he rushed to the bookcases and returned with volume

01:07:43.960 --> 01:07:46.440
one of the stardered lectures.

01:07:46.440 --> 01:07:48.480
See, he cried triumphantly.

01:07:48.480 --> 01:07:51.800
It's a bona fide piece of printed matter.

01:07:51.800 --> 01:07:53.080
It fooled me.

01:07:53.080 --> 01:07:54.520
This fellow's a regular blasco.

01:07:54.520 --> 01:07:56.360
It's a triumph.

01:07:56.360 --> 01:07:57.360
What thoroughness.

01:07:57.360 --> 01:07:58.360
What realism.

01:07:58.360 --> 01:08:00.480
You went to stop, too, didn't cut the pages.

01:08:00.480 --> 01:08:01.920
And what do you want?

01:08:01.920 --> 01:08:03.600
What do you expect?

01:08:03.600 --> 01:08:07.280
He snatched the book from me and replaced it hastily on its shelf, muttering that if one

01:08:07.280 --> 01:08:11.080
brick was removed, the whole library was liable to collapse.

01:08:11.080 --> 01:08:13.600
Who brought you, he demanded?

01:08:13.600 --> 01:08:15.000
Or did you just come?

01:08:15.000 --> 01:08:16.240
I was brought.

01:08:16.240 --> 01:08:18.040
Most people were brought.

01:08:18.040 --> 01:08:21.440
Jordan looked at him alertly, cheerfully, without answering.

01:08:21.440 --> 01:08:26.120
I was brought by a woman named Roosevelt, he continued.

01:08:26.120 --> 01:08:27.120
Mrs.

01:08:27.120 --> 01:08:29.640
Claude Roosevelt, you know her?

01:08:29.640 --> 01:08:30.640
Better somewhere last night.

01:08:30.640 --> 01:08:34.800
I've been drunk for about a week now, and I thought it might sober me up to sit in

01:08:34.800 --> 01:08:35.800
the library.

01:08:35.800 --> 01:08:36.800
Has it?

01:08:36.800 --> 01:08:40.440
A little bit, I think I can't tell you I'd have only been here an hour.

01:08:40.440 --> 01:08:42.440
Can I tell you about the books?

01:08:42.440 --> 01:08:43.440
They're real.

01:08:43.440 --> 01:08:45.120
They're up, you told us.

01:08:45.120 --> 01:08:52.200
We shook hands with him gravely and went back outdoors.

01:08:52.200 --> 01:08:55.080
This ends disc one, Gatsby.

01:08:55.080 --> 01:08:58.320
Disc two.

01:08:58.320 --> 01:09:01.720
There was dancing now on the canvas in the garden.

01:09:01.720 --> 01:09:07.960
Old men pushing young girls backward in eternal, graceless circles, superior couples holding

01:09:07.960 --> 01:09:12.320
each other tortuously, fashionably, and keeping in the corners.

01:09:12.320 --> 01:09:17.280
And a great number of single girls dancing individualistically or relieving the orchestra

01:09:17.280 --> 01:09:20.960
for a moment of the burden of the banjo were the traps.

01:09:20.960 --> 01:09:23.520
By midnight, the hilarity had increased.

01:09:23.520 --> 01:09:28.280
A celebrated tenor had sung in Italian and a notorious contralto had sung in jazz.

01:09:28.280 --> 01:09:32.840
And between the numbers, people were doing stunts all over the garden, while happy,

01:09:32.840 --> 01:09:37.360
vacuous bursts of laughter rose toward the summer sky.

01:09:37.360 --> 01:09:42.360
A pair of stage twins who turned out to be the girls in yellow did a baby act in costume,

01:09:42.360 --> 01:09:46.880
and champagne was served in glasses bigger than finger bowls.

01:09:46.880 --> 01:09:52.440
The moon had risen higher, and floating in the sound was a triangle of silver scales,

01:09:52.440 --> 01:09:57.600
trembling a little to the stiff, tinny drip of the banjo's on the lawn.

01:09:57.600 --> 01:10:00.200
I was still with Jordan Baker.

01:10:00.200 --> 01:10:04.280
We were sitting at a table with a man of about my age and a rowdy little girl who gave

01:10:04.280 --> 01:10:08.360
way upon the slightest provocation to uncontrollable laughter.

01:10:08.360 --> 01:10:09.680
I was enjoying myself now.

01:10:09.680 --> 01:10:14.320
I had taken two finger bowls of champagne, and the scene had changed before my eyes into

01:10:14.320 --> 01:10:18.320
something significant, elemental and profound.

01:10:18.320 --> 01:10:21.880
At a lull in the entertainment the man looked at me and smiled.

01:10:21.880 --> 01:10:23.880
Your face is familiar, he said politely.

01:10:23.880 --> 01:10:26.440
Why don't you win the third division during the war?

01:10:26.440 --> 01:10:30.160
Why, yes, I was in the ninth machine-gun battalion.

01:10:30.160 --> 01:10:36.080
I was in the seventh infantry until June 1918, I knew I'd seen you somewhere before.

01:10:36.080 --> 01:10:40.560
We talked for a moment about some wet grey little villages in France.

01:10:40.560 --> 01:10:44.720
Evidently he lived in this vicinity for he told me that he had just bought a hydroplane

01:10:44.720 --> 01:10:46.520
and was going to try it out in the morning.

01:10:46.520 --> 01:10:50.240
I want to go with me, old sport, just near the shore, along the sound.

01:10:50.240 --> 01:10:51.240
What time?

01:10:51.240 --> 01:10:54.040
Any time that suits you best?

01:10:54.040 --> 01:10:58.600
I was on the tip of my tongue to ask his name when Jordan looked around and smiled.

01:10:58.600 --> 01:11:01.640
Having a gate I'm now, she inquired.

01:11:01.640 --> 01:11:02.960
Much better.

01:11:02.960 --> 01:11:05.280
I turned again to my new acquaintance.

01:11:05.280 --> 01:11:06.760
This is an unusual party for me.

01:11:06.760 --> 01:11:08.600
I haven't even seen the host.

01:11:08.600 --> 01:11:09.960
I live over there.

01:11:09.960 --> 01:11:13.800
I waved my hand at the invisible hedge in the distance.

01:11:13.800 --> 01:11:17.360
And this man Gatsby sent over his chauffeur with an invitation.

01:11:17.360 --> 01:11:19.960
For a moment he looked at me as if he failed to understand.

01:11:20.320 --> 01:11:22.760
I'm Gatsby, he said suddenly.

01:11:22.760 --> 01:11:23.760
What?

01:11:23.760 --> 01:11:25.920
I exclaimed, oh, I pick your pardon.

01:11:25.920 --> 01:11:29.800
I thought you knew old sport, I'm afraid I'm not a very good host.

01:11:29.800 --> 01:11:32.320
He smiled, understandingly.

01:11:32.320 --> 01:11:35.200
Much more than understandingly.

01:11:35.200 --> 01:11:40.320
It was one of those rare smiles with the quality of eternal reassurance in it, that you

01:11:40.320 --> 01:11:43.840
may come across four or five times in life.

01:11:43.840 --> 01:11:49.920
It faced or seemed to face the whole external world for an instant, and then concentrate

01:11:49.920 --> 01:11:54.360
it on you, with an irresistible prejudice in your favor.

01:11:54.360 --> 01:11:58.800
It understood you just as far as you wanted to be understood, believed in you as you

01:11:58.800 --> 01:12:04.000
would like to believe in yourself, and assured you that it had precisely the impression

01:12:04.000 --> 01:12:08.200
of you that at your best you hoped to convey.

01:12:08.200 --> 01:12:10.720
Precisely at that point it vanished.

01:12:10.720 --> 01:12:16.080
And I was looking at an elegant young roughneck a year or two over thirty, whose elaborate formality

01:12:16.080 --> 01:12:19.480
of speech just missed being absurd.

01:12:19.480 --> 01:12:22.720
And before he introduced himself, I had gone to strong impression that he was picking

01:12:22.720 --> 01:12:25.680
his words with care.

01:12:25.680 --> 01:12:29.520
Almost at the moment when Mr. Gatsby identified himself, a butler hurried toward him with

01:12:29.520 --> 01:12:32.800
the information that Chicago was calling him on the wire.

01:12:32.800 --> 01:12:37.720
He excused himself with a small bow that included each of us in turn.

01:12:37.720 --> 01:12:41.440
If you want anything, just ask for it old sport, he urged me.

01:12:41.440 --> 01:12:44.520
Excuse me, I will rejoin you later.

01:12:44.520 --> 01:12:49.800
When he was gone, I turned immediately to Jordan, constrained to assure her of my surprise.

01:12:49.800 --> 01:12:54.680
I had expected that Mr. Gatsby would be a florid and corpulent person in his middle

01:12:54.680 --> 01:12:55.680
years.

01:12:55.680 --> 01:12:56.680
Who is he Gatsby?

01:12:56.680 --> 01:12:58.480
Where is he from, I mean?

01:12:58.480 --> 01:13:00.080
What does he do?

01:13:00.080 --> 01:13:04.080
Now you're started on the subject, she answered with a one smile.

01:13:04.080 --> 01:13:07.720
Well, he told me once he was an Oxford man.

01:13:07.720 --> 01:13:13.040
A dim background started to take shape behind him, but at her next remark it faded away.

01:13:13.040 --> 01:13:15.080
However I don't believe it.

01:13:15.080 --> 01:13:16.080
Why not?

01:13:16.080 --> 01:13:18.360
I don't know, she insisted.

01:13:18.360 --> 01:13:20.840
I just don't think he went there.

01:13:20.840 --> 01:13:25.340
Something in her tone reminded me of the other girls I think he killed a man and had the

01:13:25.340 --> 01:13:28.200
effect of stimulating my curiosity.

01:13:28.200 --> 01:13:32.280
I would have accepted without question the information that Gatsby sprang from the swamps

01:13:32.280 --> 01:13:35.640
of Louisiana or from the lower east side of New York.

01:13:35.640 --> 01:13:37.480
That was comprehensible.

01:13:37.480 --> 01:13:42.640
But young men didn't, at least in my provincial inexperience, I believe they didn't drift

01:13:42.640 --> 01:13:47.240
coolly out of nowhere and by a palace on Long Island's sound.

01:13:47.240 --> 01:13:51.760
Anyhow he gives large parties, so Jordan changing the subject with an urban distaste for

01:13:51.760 --> 01:13:53.400
the concrete.

01:13:53.400 --> 01:13:55.440
And I like large parties, they're so intimate.

01:13:55.440 --> 01:13:59.000
At small parties there isn't any privacy.

01:13:59.000 --> 01:14:03.960
There was the boom of a base drum and the voice of the orchestra leader rang out suddenly

01:14:03.960 --> 01:14:07.240
above the echolalea of the garden.

01:14:07.240 --> 01:14:12.440
Ladies and gentlemen he cried, at the request of Mr. Gatsby we are going to play for you Mr.

01:14:12.440 --> 01:14:18.520
Vladimir Tostov's latest work which attracted so much attention at Carnegie Hall last May.

01:14:18.520 --> 01:14:21.880
If you read the papers do you know there was a big sensation.

01:14:21.880 --> 01:14:29.000
He smiled with jovial condescension and added some sensation whereupon everybody laughed.

01:14:29.000 --> 01:14:36.240
The piece is known, he concluded lustily, as Vladimir Tostov's jazz history of the world.

01:14:36.240 --> 01:14:41.880
The nature of Mr. Tostov's composition eluded me because just as it began my eyes fell on

01:14:41.880 --> 01:14:46.920
Gatsby, standing alone on the marble steps and looking from one group to another with

01:14:46.920 --> 01:14:48.800
approving eyes.

01:14:48.800 --> 01:14:53.880
His tend skin was drawn attractively tight on his face and his short hair looked as though

01:14:53.880 --> 01:14:55.840
it were trimmed every day.

01:14:55.840 --> 01:14:58.440
I could see nothing sinister about him.

01:14:58.440 --> 01:15:02.560
I wondered if the fact that he was not drinking helped to set him off from his guests, for

01:15:02.560 --> 01:15:07.720
it seemed to me that he grew more correct as the fraternal hilarity increased.

01:15:07.720 --> 01:15:12.000
In the jazz history of the world was over girls were putting their heads on men's shoulders

01:15:12.000 --> 01:15:14.560
in a poppious, convivial way.

01:15:14.560 --> 01:15:19.880
Girls were swooning backward playfully into men's arms, even into groups knowing that someone

01:15:19.880 --> 01:15:21.800
would arrest their falls.

01:15:21.800 --> 01:15:28.040
But no one swooned backward on Gatsby and no French barbed-touched Gatsby's shoulder

01:15:28.040 --> 01:15:32.640
and no singing quartets were formed with Gatsby's head for one link.

01:15:33.640 --> 01:15:35.160
I beg your pardon.

01:15:35.160 --> 01:15:38.160
Gatsby's butler was suddenly standing beside us.

01:15:38.160 --> 01:15:40.480
Miss Baker, he inquired.

01:15:40.480 --> 01:15:44.720
I beg your pardon, but Mr. Gatsby would like to speak to you alone.

01:15:44.720 --> 01:15:46.720
With me, she exclaimed in sublime.

01:15:46.720 --> 01:15:47.720
She got up slow.

01:15:47.720 --> 01:15:52.200
Yes, raising her eyebrows at me in astonishment and followed the butler toward the house.

01:15:52.200 --> 01:15:56.800
I noticed that she wore her evening dress, all her dresses like sports clothes.

01:15:56.800 --> 01:16:00.860
There was a jauntiness about her movements as if she had first learned to walk upon golf

01:16:00.860 --> 01:16:04.580
courses on clean crisp mornings.

01:16:04.580 --> 01:16:07.460
I was alone, and it was almost two.

01:16:07.460 --> 01:16:12.540
For some time confused and intriguing sounds had issued from a long, many windowed room

01:16:12.540 --> 01:16:17.540
which overhungered the terrace, eluding Jordan's undergraduate who was now engaged in an

01:16:17.540 --> 01:16:22.940
obstetrical conversation with two chorus girls, and who implored me to join him, I went

01:16:22.940 --> 01:16:24.820
inside.

01:16:24.820 --> 01:16:27.340
The large room was full of people.

01:16:27.340 --> 01:16:31.340
One of the girls in yellow was playing the piano, and beside her stood a tall, red-haired

01:16:31.340 --> 01:16:35.260
young lady from a famous chorus engaged in song.

01:16:35.260 --> 01:16:39.740
She had drunk a quantity of champagne, and during the course of her song she had decided

01:16:39.740 --> 01:16:43.980
ineptly that everything was very, very sad.

01:16:43.980 --> 01:16:46.900
She was not only singing, she was weeping too.

01:16:46.900 --> 01:16:51.780
Whenever there was a pause in the song she filled it with gasping, broken and sobs, and

01:16:51.780 --> 01:16:54.820
then took up the lyric again in a quavering soprano.

01:16:54.820 --> 01:16:59.420
The tears coursed down her cheeks, not freely, however, for when they came into contact

01:16:59.420 --> 01:17:04.460
with her heavily beaded eyelashes they assumed an inky color, and pursued the rest of their

01:17:04.460 --> 01:17:08.140
way in slow, black rivulets.

01:17:08.140 --> 01:17:12.420
A humorous suggestion was made that she sing the notes on her face, whereupon she threw

01:17:12.420 --> 01:17:18.460
up her hands, sank into a chair, and went off into a deep, vina-sleep.

01:17:18.460 --> 01:17:23.700
She had a fight with a man who says he's her husband, explained a girl at my elbow.

01:17:23.700 --> 01:17:25.460
I looked around.

01:17:25.460 --> 01:17:29.660
Most of the remaining women were now having fights with men said to be their husbands.

01:17:29.660 --> 01:17:34.900
Even Georgian's party, the quartet from East Egg, were rent to Sunder by dissension.

01:17:34.900 --> 01:17:39.780
One of the men was talking with curious intensity to a young actress, and his wife, after attempting

01:17:39.780 --> 01:17:44.460
to laugh at the situation in a dignified and indifferent way, broke down entirely and

01:17:44.460 --> 01:17:46.460
resorted to flank attacks.

01:17:46.460 --> 01:17:51.060
At intervals she appeared suddenly at his side like an angry diamond, and hissed, you

01:17:51.060 --> 01:17:53.660
promised, into his ear.

01:17:53.660 --> 01:17:57.220
The reluctance to go home was not confined to wayward men.

01:17:57.220 --> 01:18:02.740
The hall was a present occupied by two deplorably sober men, and their highly indignant wives.

01:18:02.740 --> 01:18:06.700
The wives were sympathizing with each other and slightly raised voices.

01:18:06.700 --> 01:18:10.220
Whenever he sees I'm having a good time, he wants to go home.

01:18:10.220 --> 01:18:12.540
Never heard anything so selfish in my life.

01:18:12.540 --> 01:18:14.540
We're always the first ones to leave.

01:18:14.540 --> 01:18:15.540
So are we.

01:18:15.540 --> 01:18:19.260
Well, we're almost the last tonight, at one of the men, sheepishly.

01:18:19.260 --> 01:18:22.140
The orchestra left half an hour ago.

01:18:22.140 --> 01:18:27.560
In spite of the wives' agreement that such malevolence was beyond credibility, the dispute

01:18:27.560 --> 01:18:34.140
ended in a short struggle, and both wives were lifted kicking into the night.

01:18:34.140 --> 01:18:38.540
As I waited for my hat in the hall, the door of the library opened, and Jordan Baker and

01:18:38.540 --> 01:18:40.660
Gatsby came out together.

01:18:40.660 --> 01:18:44.940
He was saying some last word to her, but the eagerness in his manner tightened abruptly

01:18:44.940 --> 01:18:49.140
into formality, as several people approached him to say goodbye.

01:18:49.140 --> 01:18:53.100
Jordan's party were calling impatiently to her from the porch, but she lingered for

01:18:53.100 --> 01:18:54.580
a moment to shake hands.

01:18:54.580 --> 01:18:59.140
I've just heard the most amazing thing she was spreading, but how long were we in there?

01:18:59.140 --> 01:19:01.220
It was simply amazing.

01:19:01.220 --> 01:19:06.220
She repeated abstractedly, but I swore I wouldn't tell it in here I am tantalizing you.

01:19:06.220 --> 01:19:08.660
She yawned gracefully in my face.

01:19:08.660 --> 01:19:10.380
Please come and see me.

01:19:10.380 --> 01:19:14.380
Phone book under the name of Mrs. Sigourney Howard, my aunt.

01:19:14.380 --> 01:19:16.100
She was hurrying off as she talked.

01:19:16.100 --> 01:19:22.220
A brown hand waved a jaunty salute, as she melted into her party at the door.

01:19:22.220 --> 01:19:26.840
Rather ashamed, then on my first appearance I had stayed so late, I joined the last of

01:19:26.840 --> 01:19:29.620
Gatsby's guests who were clustered around him.

01:19:29.620 --> 01:19:33.420
I wanted to explain that I had hunted for him early in the evening and to apologize for

01:19:33.420 --> 01:19:35.180
not having known him in the garden.

01:19:35.180 --> 01:19:36.460
Don't mention it.

01:19:36.460 --> 01:19:38.300
He enjoined me eagerly.

01:19:38.300 --> 01:19:40.380
Don't give it another thought-old sport.

01:19:40.380 --> 01:19:46.060
The familiar expression held no more familiarity than the hand which reassuringly brushed my shoulder.

01:19:46.060 --> 01:19:49.380
And don't forget we're going up on the hydroplane tomorrow morning at nine o'clock.

01:19:49.380 --> 01:19:53.420
Then the butler behind his shoulder, Philadelphia wants you on the phone, sir.

01:19:53.420 --> 01:19:55.620
All right, in a minute tell him I'll be right there.

01:19:55.620 --> 01:19:56.620
Good night.

01:19:56.620 --> 01:19:57.620
Good night.

01:19:57.620 --> 01:19:58.860
He smiled.

01:19:58.860 --> 01:20:02.820
And suddenly there seemed to be a pleasant significance in having been among the last to

01:20:02.820 --> 01:20:06.100
go as if he had desired it all the time.

01:20:06.100 --> 01:20:07.100
Good night, old sport.

01:20:07.100 --> 01:20:08.700
Good night.

01:20:08.700 --> 01:20:13.260
But as I walked down the steps I saw that the evening was not quite over.

01:20:13.260 --> 01:20:18.740
Fifty feet from the door a dozen headlights illuminated a bizarre and tumultuous scene.

01:20:18.740 --> 01:20:23.900
In the ditch beside the road, right side up, but violently shorn of one wheel, rested a new

01:20:23.900 --> 01:20:27.980
coupé which had left Gatsby's drive not two minutes before.

01:20:27.980 --> 01:20:31.980
The sharp juts of a wall accounted for the detachment of the wheel which was now getting

01:20:31.980 --> 01:20:35.540
considerable attention from half a dozen curious chauffeurs.

01:20:35.540 --> 01:20:40.700
However, as they had left their cars blocking the road, a harsh, discordant din from those

01:20:40.700 --> 01:20:45.660
in the rear had been audible for some time and added to the already violent confusion

01:20:45.660 --> 01:20:47.460
of the scene.

01:20:47.460 --> 01:20:51.460
A man in a long duster had dismounted from the wreck and now stood in the middle of the

01:20:51.460 --> 01:20:55.940
road looking from the car to the tire and from the tire to the observers in a pleasant

01:20:55.940 --> 01:20:57.340
puzzled way.

01:20:57.340 --> 01:20:59.780
See, we explained.

01:20:59.780 --> 01:21:01.500
It went in the ditch.

01:21:01.500 --> 01:21:06.540
The fact was infinitely astonishing to him, and I recognized first the unusual quality of

01:21:06.540 --> 01:21:08.620
wonder, and then the man.

01:21:08.620 --> 01:21:11.700
It was the late patron of Gatsby's library.

01:21:11.700 --> 01:21:12.700
How did happen?

01:21:12.700 --> 01:21:14.700
He shrugged his shoulders.

01:21:14.700 --> 01:21:17.300
I know nothing, whatever, about mechanics.

01:21:17.300 --> 01:21:18.780
He said decisively.

01:21:18.780 --> 01:21:19.780
But how did it happen?

01:21:19.780 --> 01:21:21.300
Did you run into the wall?

01:21:21.300 --> 01:21:25.340
Don't ask me, said our lies, washing his hands of the whole matter.

01:21:25.340 --> 01:21:26.860
I know very little about driving.

01:21:26.860 --> 01:21:27.860
Next to nothing.

01:21:27.860 --> 01:21:29.700
It happened, and that's all I know.

01:21:29.700 --> 01:21:34.460
Well, if you're a poor driver, you're oughtin' to try driving at night.

01:21:34.460 --> 01:21:37.420
But I wasn't even trying, he explained indignantly.

01:21:37.420 --> 01:21:39.420
I wasn't even trying.

01:21:39.420 --> 01:21:42.300
An odd hush fell upon the bystanders.

01:21:42.300 --> 01:21:44.620
Do you want to commit suicide?

01:21:44.620 --> 01:21:48.540
You're lucky it was just a wheel, a bad driver, and not even trying.

01:21:48.540 --> 01:21:51.300
You don't understand, explain the criminal.

01:21:51.300 --> 01:21:52.300
I wasn't driving.

01:21:52.300 --> 01:21:55.260
There's another man in the car.

01:21:55.260 --> 01:22:02.980
The shock that followed this declaration found voice in a sustained, ah, as the door of

01:22:02.980 --> 01:22:05.780
the coupé swung slowly open.

01:22:05.780 --> 01:22:10.180
The crowd, it was now a crowd, stepped back involuntarily.

01:22:10.180 --> 01:22:13.860
And when the door had opened wide, there was a ghostly pause.

01:22:13.860 --> 01:22:20.420
Then very gradually, part by part, a pale, dangling individual stepped out of the wreck,

01:22:20.420 --> 01:22:25.780
pawing tentatively at the ground with a large, uncertain, dancing shoe.

01:22:25.780 --> 01:22:31.220
Blinded by the glare of the headlights and confused by the incessant groaning of the horns,

01:22:31.220 --> 01:22:36.900
the apparitions stood swaying for a moment before he perceived the man in the duster.

01:22:36.900 --> 01:22:38.820
What's the matter?

01:22:38.820 --> 01:22:40.140
He inquired calmly.

01:22:40.140 --> 01:22:42.940
Did we run out of gas?

01:22:42.940 --> 01:22:43.940
Look!

01:22:43.940 --> 01:22:46.660
Half a dozen fingers pointed at the amputated wheel.

01:22:46.660 --> 01:22:50.660
He stared at it for a moment and then looked upward as though he suspected that it had dropped

01:22:50.660 --> 01:22:52.260
from the sky.

01:22:52.260 --> 01:22:54.060
It came off, someone explained.

01:22:54.060 --> 01:22:56.900
At first I didn't notice we'd stopped.

01:22:56.900 --> 01:22:58.780
A pause.

01:22:58.780 --> 01:23:04.780
Then taking a long breath and straightening his shoulders he remarked in a determined voice.

01:23:04.780 --> 01:23:08.420
One of you tell me where there's a gasoline station.

01:23:08.420 --> 01:23:12.540
At least a dozen men, some of them a little better off than he was, explained to him that

01:23:12.540 --> 01:23:18.500
wheel and car were no longer joined by any physical bond.

01:23:18.500 --> 01:23:20.980
Back out he suggested after a moment.

01:23:20.980 --> 01:23:22.820
Pour in reverse.

01:23:22.820 --> 01:23:24.900
But the wheels off.

01:23:24.900 --> 01:23:25.900
He hesitated.

01:23:25.900 --> 01:23:29.660
Oh, I'm in trying, he said.

01:23:29.660 --> 01:23:34.980
The catter-walling horns had reached a crescendo, and I turned away and cut across the lawn-tored

01:23:34.980 --> 01:23:35.980
home.

01:23:35.980 --> 01:23:38.060
I glanced back once.

01:23:38.060 --> 01:23:44.900
A wafer of a moon was shining over Gatsby's house, making the night fine as before and surviving

01:23:44.900 --> 01:23:49.180
the laughter and the sound of his still glowing garden.

01:23:49.180 --> 01:23:54.620
A sudden emptiness seemed to flow now from the windows and the great doors, and dowing

01:23:54.620 --> 01:24:00.860
with complete isolation the figure of the host, who stood on the porch, his hand up in

01:24:00.860 --> 01:24:08.140
a formal gesture of farewell.

01:24:08.140 --> 01:24:12.180
Reading over what I have written so far, I see I have given the impression that the events

01:24:12.180 --> 01:24:16.140
of three nights several weeks apart were all that absorbed me.

01:24:16.140 --> 01:24:21.380
On the contrary they were merely casual events in a crowded summer, and until much later

01:24:21.380 --> 01:24:25.020
they absorbed me infinitely less than my personal affairs.

01:24:25.020 --> 01:24:26.700
Most of the time I worked.

01:24:26.700 --> 01:24:31.500
In the early morning the sun threw my shadow westward as I hurried down the white chasms

01:24:31.500 --> 01:24:34.540
of lower New York to the probity trust.

01:24:34.540 --> 01:24:39.020
I knew the other clerks and young bond salesmen by their first names and lunched with them

01:24:39.020 --> 01:24:44.940
in dark, crowded restaurants on little pig sausages and mashed potatoes and coffee.

01:24:44.940 --> 01:24:48.620
I even had a short affair with a girl who lived in Jersey City and worked in the accounting

01:24:48.620 --> 01:24:49.620
department.

01:24:49.700 --> 01:24:54.260
But her brother began throwing mean looks in my direction so when she went on her vacation

01:24:54.260 --> 01:24:58.220
in July I let it blow quietly away.

01:24:58.220 --> 01:25:02.540
I took dinner usually at the Yale Club for some reason it was the gloomiest event of my

01:25:02.540 --> 01:25:07.700
day, and then I went upstairs to the library and studied investments and securities for a

01:25:07.700 --> 01:25:09.980
conscientious hour.

01:25:09.980 --> 01:25:14.180
There were generally a few rioters around, but they never came into the library so it

01:25:14.180 --> 01:25:16.140
was a good place to work.

01:25:16.140 --> 01:25:20.460
After that of the night was mellow I strolled down Madison Avenue past the old Murray Hill

01:25:20.460 --> 01:25:25.740
hotel and over 33rd Street to the Pennsylvania station.

01:25:25.740 --> 01:25:31.540
I began to like New York, the racy adventurous feel of it at night, and the satisfaction

01:25:31.540 --> 01:25:36.740
that the constant flicker of men and women and machines gives to the restless eye.

01:25:36.740 --> 01:25:42.060
I like to walk up Fifth Avenue and pick out romantic women from the crowd and imagine

01:25:42.060 --> 01:25:46.180
that in a few minutes I was going to enter into their lives and no one would ever know

01:25:46.180 --> 01:25:48.780
or disapprove.

01:25:48.780 --> 01:25:54.100
Sometimes in my mind I followed them to their apartments on the corners of hidden streets,

01:25:54.100 --> 01:26:00.540
and they turned and smiled back at me before they faded through a door into warm darkness.

01:26:00.540 --> 01:26:06.860
At the enchanted metropolitan twilight I felt a haunting loneliness sometimes, and felt

01:26:06.860 --> 01:26:11.100
it in others poor young clerks who loitered in front of windows waiting until it was time

01:26:11.100 --> 01:26:13.540
for a solitary restaurant dinner.

01:26:13.540 --> 01:26:19.700
Young clerks in the dusk, wasting the most poignant moments of night and life.

01:26:19.700 --> 01:26:24.020
Again at eight o'clock when the dark lanes of the forties were five deep with throbbing

01:26:24.020 --> 01:26:30.020
taxi-capped bound for the theatre district I felt a sinking in my heart.

01:26:30.020 --> 01:26:35.300
Forms leaned together in the taxis as they waited in voices sang, and there was laughter

01:26:35.300 --> 01:26:41.940
from unheard jokes and lighted cigarettes outlined unintelligible gestures inside.

01:26:41.940 --> 01:26:46.780
Imagining that I too was hurrying toward gayity and sharing their intimate excitement for

01:26:46.780 --> 01:26:51.900
a while I wished the sight of Jordan Baker, and then in midsummer I found her again.

01:26:51.900 --> 01:26:55.740
At first I was flattered to go places with her because she was a golf champion and everyone

01:26:55.740 --> 01:26:57.300
knew her name.

01:26:57.300 --> 01:27:03.900
Then it was something more, I wasn't actually in love, but I felt a sort of tender curiosity.

01:27:03.900 --> 01:27:08.740
The bored, hotly face that she turned to the world concealed something.

01:27:08.740 --> 01:27:13.620
Most affectations conceal something eventually even though they don't in the beginning, and

01:27:13.620 --> 01:27:16.100
one day I found what it was.

01:27:16.100 --> 01:27:20.500
When we were on a house party together up in Warwick, she left a borrowed car out in the

01:27:20.500 --> 01:27:24.260
rain with the top down and then lied about it.

01:27:24.260 --> 01:27:29.380
And suddenly I remembered the story about her that had eluded me that night at daisies.

01:27:29.380 --> 01:27:34.020
At her first big golf tournament there was a rau that nearly reached the newspapers,

01:27:34.020 --> 01:27:39.580
a suggestion that she had moved her ball from a bad lie in the semifinal round.

01:27:39.580 --> 01:27:43.900
The thing approached the proportions of a scandal then died away.

01:27:43.900 --> 01:27:48.060
A caddy retracted his statements and the only other witness admitted that he might have

01:27:48.060 --> 01:27:49.460
been mistaken.

01:27:49.460 --> 01:27:53.460
The incident and the name had remained together in my mind.

01:27:53.460 --> 01:27:59.100
Jordan Baker instinctively avoided clever, shrewd men, and now I saw that this was

01:27:59.100 --> 01:28:03.260
because she felt safer on a plane where any divergence from a code would be thought

01:28:03.260 --> 01:28:04.860
impossible.

01:28:04.860 --> 01:28:07.980
She was inculurably dishonest.

01:28:07.980 --> 01:28:12.340
She wasn't able to endure being at a disadvantage, and given this unwillingness I suppose, she had

01:28:12.340 --> 01:28:18.060
begun dealing in subterfuges when she was very young, in order to keep that cool, insolent

01:28:18.060 --> 01:28:24.460
smile turned to the world, and yet satisfy the demands of her hard, jaunty body.

01:28:24.460 --> 01:28:26.500
It made no difference to me.

01:28:26.500 --> 01:28:30.460
This honesty in a woman is a thing you never blame deeply.

01:28:30.460 --> 01:28:33.700
I was casually sorry, and then I forgot.

01:28:33.700 --> 01:28:38.540
It was on that same house-party that we had a curious conversation about driving a car.

01:28:38.540 --> 01:28:42.620
It started because she passed so close to some workman that our fender flicked a button

01:28:42.620 --> 01:28:44.180
on one man's coat.

01:28:44.180 --> 01:28:47.180
"'You're a rotten driver,' I protested.

01:28:47.180 --> 01:28:50.740
Either you ought to be more careful or you oughtn't to drive at all.'

01:28:50.740 --> 01:28:51.740
"'I am careful.

01:28:51.740 --> 01:28:53.380
No, you're not.

01:28:53.380 --> 01:28:56.180
Well, other people are,' she said likely.

01:28:56.180 --> 01:28:57.820
"'What's that got to do with it?

01:28:57.820 --> 01:29:00.180
They'll keep out of my way,' she insisted.

01:29:00.180 --> 01:29:02.540
It takes two to make an accident.

01:29:02.540 --> 01:29:06.140
"'Suppose you met somebody just as careless as yourself.'

01:29:06.140 --> 01:29:08.500
"'I hope I never will,' she answered.

01:29:08.500 --> 01:29:09.900
I hate careless people.

01:29:09.900 --> 01:29:12.780
That's why I like you.'

01:29:12.780 --> 01:29:18.060
Her grey, sun-strained eyes stared straight ahead, but she had deliberately shifted our

01:29:18.060 --> 01:29:21.860
relations, and for a moment I thought I loved her.

01:29:22.340 --> 01:29:28.420
But I am slow-thinking and full of interior rules that act as breaks on my desires, and

01:29:28.420 --> 01:29:33.340
I knew that first I had to get myself definitely out of that tangle back home.

01:29:33.340 --> 01:29:37.620
I'd been writing letters once a week and signing them love, Nick, and all I could think

01:29:37.620 --> 01:29:43.420
of was how when that certain girl played tennis, a faint moustache of perspiration appeared

01:29:43.420 --> 01:29:45.580
on her upper lip.

01:29:45.580 --> 01:29:50.340
Nevertheless, there was a vague understanding that had to be tactfully broken off before

01:29:50.340 --> 01:29:52.340
I was free.

01:29:52.340 --> 01:29:57.620
Everyone suspects himself of at least one of the cardinal virtues, and this is mine.

01:29:57.620 --> 01:30:03.220
I am one of the few honest people that I have ever known."

01:30:03.220 --> 01:30:05.220
Chapter IV

01:30:05.220 --> 01:30:10.700
On Sunday morning, while church bells rang in the villages along shore, the world and

01:30:10.700 --> 01:30:16.220
its mistress returned to Gatsby's house and twinkled hilariously on his lawn.

01:30:16.220 --> 01:30:20.740
He's a bootlegger, so the young ladies moving somewhere between his cocktails and his

01:30:20.740 --> 01:30:22.140
flowers.

01:30:22.140 --> 01:30:26.180
One time he killed a man who would found out that he was nephew to Vaughn-Hindenburg

01:30:26.180 --> 01:30:30.300
and second cousin to the devil, reached me a rose honey and pour me a last drop into

01:30:30.300 --> 01:30:33.860
that there, crystal glass.

01:30:33.860 --> 01:30:37.940
Once I wrote down on the empty spaces of a timetable, the names of those who came to

01:30:37.940 --> 01:30:40.180
Gatsby's house that summer.

01:30:40.180 --> 01:30:44.660
It is an old timetable now, disintegrating at its folds and headed, this schedule is

01:30:44.660 --> 01:30:51.980
in effect July 5, 1922, but I can still read the grey names, and they will give you a better

01:30:51.980 --> 01:30:56.820
impression than my generalities of those who accepted Gatsby's hospitality and paid

01:30:56.820 --> 01:31:00.660
him the subtle tribute of knowing nothing whatever about him.

01:31:00.660 --> 01:31:06.180
From East Egg then came the Chester Beckers and the Leeches, and a man named Bunsen,

01:31:06.180 --> 01:31:11.460
whom I knew at Yale, and Dr. Webster Sivitt, who was drowned last summer up in Maine,

01:31:11.460 --> 01:31:15.780
and the horn beams and the Willie Voltaire's, and a whole clan named Blackbuck, who always

01:31:15.780 --> 01:31:20.660
gathered in a corner and flipped up their noses like goats at whosoever came near.

01:31:20.660 --> 01:31:25.860
And the Ismays and the Christie's, or rather Hubert, our Bach and Mr. Christie's wife,

01:31:25.860 --> 01:31:30.300
and Edgar Beaver, whose hair they say turned cotton white one winter afternoon for no good

01:31:30.300 --> 01:31:32.300
reason at all.

01:31:32.300 --> 01:31:35.900
Clarence andive was from East Egg, as I remember.

01:31:35.900 --> 01:31:40.140
He came only once, and white nickerbuckers, and had a fight with a bomb named Eddie in

01:31:40.140 --> 01:31:41.740
the garden.

01:31:41.740 --> 01:31:46.460
From further out on the island came the Cheedles, and the ORP Shraders, and the Stonewall

01:31:46.460 --> 01:31:51.060
Jackson Abrams of Georgia, and the Fish Guards, and the Ripley Snells.

01:31:51.060 --> 01:31:54.900
Snell was there three days before he went to the Penitentiary, so drunk out on the gravel

01:31:54.900 --> 01:32:00.100
drive that Mrs. Eulissie's sweats automobile ran over his right hand.

01:32:00.100 --> 01:32:05.980
The Dancies came too, and SB Whitebate, who was well over sixty, and Maurice A. Flink

01:32:05.980 --> 01:32:11.020
and the Hammerheads, and Beluga, the Tobacco Importer, and Beluga's Girls.

01:32:11.020 --> 01:32:15.980
From West Egg came the Maldreddies, and Cecil Robuck and Cecil Shohen and Gullick, the

01:32:15.980 --> 01:32:22.020
State Senator, and Newton Orchid, who controlled films par excellence, and Eckhausen, Clyde

01:32:22.020 --> 01:32:27.260
Cohen, and Don Eschworts the Sun, and Arthur McCarty, all connected with the movies in one

01:32:27.260 --> 01:32:32.380
way or another, and the Catlips and the Ben Burgues, and G. Earl Maldun, brother to that

01:32:32.380 --> 01:32:35.140
Maldun, who afterwards strangled his wife.

01:32:35.140 --> 01:32:40.580
Daffontano, the promoter, came there, and Ed Lee Gross, and James B. Rutgut Ferret,

01:32:40.580 --> 01:32:44.900
and the DeYoungs, and Ernest Lilly, they came to gamble, and when Ferret wandered into

01:32:44.900 --> 01:32:48.380
the garden, it meant he was cleaned out, and associated traction would have to fluctuate

01:32:48.380 --> 01:32:55.540
profitably next day.

01:32:55.540 --> 01:33:00.460
A man named Clipspringer was there so often, and so long that he became known as the

01:33:00.460 --> 01:33:03.940
Border, and doubt if he had any other home.

01:33:03.940 --> 01:33:08.420
Of the Atrical People, there were Gus Ways and Horusodonovan and Leicester Meyer and

01:33:08.420 --> 01:33:11.260
George Duckweed and Francis Bull.

01:33:11.260 --> 01:33:15.620
Also from New York were the Crooms and the Back Hissons and the Denikers and Russell Betty

01:33:15.620 --> 01:33:20.700
and the Cargons and the Calahers and the Duers and the Scullies, and SW Belcher and the

01:33:20.700 --> 01:33:25.540
Smirks and the Young Quins divorced now, and Henry L. Paul Meadow, who killed himself

01:33:25.540 --> 01:33:29.420
by jumping in front of a subway train in Times Square.

01:33:29.420 --> 01:33:33.020
Benny McClenahan arrived always with four girls.

01:33:33.020 --> 01:33:37.140
They were never quite the same ones, in physical person, but they were so identical one with

01:33:37.140 --> 01:33:40.860
another that it inevitably seemed they had been there before.

01:33:40.860 --> 01:33:46.460
I've forgotten their names, Jacqueline, I think, G'Riles Consuela, or Gloria, or Judy,

01:33:46.460 --> 01:33:51.740
or June, and their last names were either the melodious names of flowers and months, or

01:33:51.740 --> 01:33:56.460
the stoner ones, or the great American capitalists, whose cousins, if pressed, they were

01:33:56.460 --> 01:33:57.460
just as simple.

01:33:57.460 --> 01:34:01.740
In addition to all these, I can remember that Faustina O'Brien came there at least once,

01:34:01.740 --> 01:34:06.380
and the Beddaker girls and young Brewer, who had his nose shot off in the war, and Mr.

01:34:06.380 --> 01:34:13.020
Albrook's burger and Miss Hogg, his fiancé, and Ardita Fitzpieters and Mr. P. Jewett,

01:34:13.020 --> 01:34:18.580
once head of the American Legion, and Miss Claudia Hipp, with a man reputed to be her chauffeur,

01:34:18.580 --> 01:34:22.380
and a prince of something, whom he called Duke, and whose name if I ever knew it I have

01:34:22.380 --> 01:34:23.700
forgotten.

01:34:23.700 --> 01:34:27.860
All these people came to Gatsby's house in the summer.

01:34:27.860 --> 01:34:32.900
At nine o'clock one morning late in July, Gatsby's gorgeous car lurched up the rocky

01:34:32.900 --> 01:34:39.020
drive to my door, and gave out a burst of melody from its three-noted horn.

01:34:39.020 --> 01:34:43.140
It was the first time he had called on me, though I had gone to two of his parties mounted

01:34:43.140 --> 01:34:48.020
in his hydropane, and at his urgent invitation made frequent use of his beach.

01:34:48.020 --> 01:34:52.180
Good morning, old sport, you having lunch with me today, and I thought we'd write up together.

01:34:52.180 --> 01:34:56.980
He was balancing himself on the dashboard of his car with that resourcefulness of movement

01:34:56.980 --> 01:34:59.380
that is so peculiarly American.

01:34:59.380 --> 01:35:04.660
That comes, I suppose, with the absence of lifting work, or rigid sitting in youth, and

01:35:04.660 --> 01:35:08.460
even more with the formless grace of our nervous sporadic games.

01:35:26.980 --> 01:35:41.360
But it's all in here and there in its monstrous length with triumphant hatboxes and supper

01:35:41.360 --> 01:35:46.980
boxes and tool boxes to terrace with the labyrinth of windshields that mirrored a dozen

01:35:46.980 --> 01:35:48.460
sons.

01:35:48.460 --> 01:35:53.820
Sitting down behind many layers of glass in a sort of green leather conservatory we started

01:35:53.820 --> 01:35:55.740
to town.

01:35:55.740 --> 01:35:59.780
I had talked with him perhaps half a dozen times in the past month and found to my disappointment

01:35:59.780 --> 01:36:01.540
that he a little to say.

01:36:01.540 --> 01:36:06.860
So my first impression that he was a person of some undefined consequence had gradually

01:36:06.860 --> 01:36:13.780
faded, and he had become simply the proprietor of an elaborate roadhouse next door, and then

01:36:13.780 --> 01:36:17.340
came that disconcerting ride.

01:36:17.340 --> 01:36:23.060
We hadn't reached West Egg Village before Gatsby began leaving his elegant sentences unfinished,

01:36:23.060 --> 01:36:27.180
and slapping himself indecisively on the knee of his caramel-colored suit.

01:36:27.180 --> 01:36:31.140
Look here, old sport! he broke out surprisingly.

01:36:31.140 --> 01:36:33.180
What's your opinion of me anyhow?

01:36:33.180 --> 01:36:38.620
A little overwhelmed, I began the generalized evasions which that question deserves.

01:36:38.620 --> 01:36:42.100
Well, I'm going to tell you something about my life, he interrupted.

01:36:42.100 --> 01:36:46.380
I don't want you to get a wrong idea of me from all these stories you hear.

01:36:46.380 --> 01:36:51.700
So he was aware of the bizarre accusations that flavored conversation in his halls.

01:36:52.300 --> 01:36:57.220
I'll tell you God's truth, his right hand suddenly ordered divine retribution to stand

01:36:57.220 --> 01:36:58.540
by.

01:36:58.540 --> 01:37:02.900
I am the son of some wealthy people in the middle West, all dead now.

01:37:02.900 --> 01:37:06.540
I was brought up in America but educated at Oxford because all my ancestors have been

01:37:06.540 --> 01:37:07.940
educated there for many years.

01:37:07.940 --> 01:37:10.500
It is a family tradition.

01:37:10.500 --> 01:37:16.060
He looked at me sideways, and I knew why Jordan Baker had believed he was lying.

01:37:16.060 --> 01:37:20.380
He hurried the phrase, educated at Oxford, or swallowed it, or choked on it as though

01:37:20.380 --> 01:37:22.540
it had bothered him before.

01:37:22.540 --> 01:37:26.140
And with this doubt, his whole statement fell to pieces.

01:37:26.140 --> 01:37:30.460
And I wondered if there wasn't something a little sinister about him after all.

01:37:30.460 --> 01:37:33.700
What part of the middle West, I inquired casually?

01:37:33.700 --> 01:37:36.540
San Francisco, I see.

01:37:36.540 --> 01:37:40.060
My family all died and I came into a good deal of money.

01:37:40.060 --> 01:37:45.380
His voice was solemn, as if the memory of that sudden extinction of a clan still haunted

01:37:45.380 --> 01:37:46.380
him.

01:37:46.380 --> 01:37:52.380
There a moment I suspected that he was pulling my leg, but a glance at him convinced me otherwise.

01:37:52.380 --> 01:37:58.460
After that, I lived like a young raja on all the capitals of Europe, Paris, Venice, Rome,

01:37:58.460 --> 01:38:03.620
collecting jewels, chiefly rubies, hunting big-gain, painting a little things for myself only,

01:38:03.620 --> 01:38:06.980
and trying to forget something very sad that happened to me.

01:38:06.980 --> 01:38:09.780
I managed to restrain my incredulous laughter.

01:38:09.780 --> 01:38:14.540
The very phrases were worn so thread-bearing that they evoked no image except that of a

01:38:14.540 --> 01:38:19.340
turbulent character leaking sawdust at every pore as he pursued a tiger through the

01:38:19.340 --> 01:38:21.820
boide bologna.

01:38:21.820 --> 01:38:24.020
Then came the war, old sport.

01:38:24.020 --> 01:38:28.780
There was a great relief, and I tried very hard to die, but I seemed to bear an enchanted

01:38:28.780 --> 01:38:29.780
life.

01:38:29.780 --> 01:38:32.620
I accepted a commission as first lieutenant when it began.

01:38:32.620 --> 01:38:37.100
In the argon forest I took two machine-gun detachments so far forward that there was a half-mile

01:38:37.100 --> 01:38:40.620
gap on either side of us where the infantry couldn't advance.

01:38:40.620 --> 01:38:45.500
We stayed there two days in two nights, a hundred and thirty men with sixteen Lewis guns,

01:38:45.500 --> 01:38:49.620
and when the infantry came up at last they found the insignia of three German divisions

01:38:49.620 --> 01:38:51.380
among the piles of dead.

01:38:51.380 --> 01:38:56.340
I was promoted to be a major and every Allied government gave me a decoration.

01:38:56.340 --> 01:39:02.500
Even Montenegro, little Montenegro down on the Adriatic Sea.

01:39:02.500 --> 01:39:07.860
Little Montenegro, he lifted up the words and nodded at them with his smile.

01:39:07.860 --> 01:39:13.780
The smile, comprehended Montenegro's troubled history and sympathized with the brave struggles

01:39:13.780 --> 01:39:16.060
of the Montenegro people.

01:39:16.060 --> 01:39:20.740
It appreciated fully the chain of national circumstances which had elicited this tribute

01:39:20.740 --> 01:39:23.580
from Montenegro's warm little heart.

01:39:23.580 --> 01:39:26.180
My incredulity was submerged in fascination.

01:39:26.180 --> 01:39:29.700
Now I was like skimming hastily through a dozen magazines.

01:39:29.700 --> 01:39:35.020
He reached in his pocket in a piece of metal slung on a ribbon fell into my palm.

01:39:35.020 --> 01:39:37.340
That's the one from Montenegro.

01:39:37.340 --> 01:39:40.620
To my astonishment the thing had an authentic look.

01:39:40.620 --> 01:39:44.100
Orderi did Danilo ran the circular legend.

01:39:44.100 --> 01:39:47.020
Montenegro Nicholas Rex.

01:39:47.020 --> 01:39:48.340
Turn it.

01:39:48.340 --> 01:39:53.340
Major J. Gatsby I read for a Valor extraordinary.

01:39:53.340 --> 01:39:56.620
Here's another thing I always carry, a souvenir of Oxford days.

01:39:56.620 --> 01:39:58.500
It was taken in Trinity Quad.

01:39:58.500 --> 01:40:02.100
The man on my left is now the Earl of Dunkester.

01:40:02.100 --> 01:40:06.180
There was a photograph of half a dozen young men in blazers, loafing in an archway

01:40:06.180 --> 01:40:09.300
through which were visible a host of spires.

01:40:09.300 --> 01:40:14.940
There was Gatsby looking a little not much younger with a cricket bat in his hand.

01:40:14.940 --> 01:40:16.820
Then it was all true.

01:40:16.820 --> 01:40:21.060
I saw the skins of tigers flaming in his palace on the Grand Canal.

01:40:21.060 --> 01:40:25.860
I saw him opening a chest of rubies to ease with their crimson lighted depths, their

01:40:25.860 --> 01:40:28.900
noings of his broken heart.

01:40:28.900 --> 01:40:31.180
I'm going to make a big request of you today.

01:40:31.180 --> 01:40:34.260
He said pocketing his souvenirs with satisfaction.

01:40:34.260 --> 01:40:36.580
So I thought you ought to know something about me.

01:40:36.580 --> 01:40:39.100
I didn't want you to think I was just some nobody.

01:40:39.100 --> 01:40:43.060
You see, I usually find myself among strangers because I drift here and there trying to forget

01:40:43.060 --> 01:40:45.500
the sad thing that happened to me.

01:40:45.500 --> 01:40:46.500
He hesitated.

01:40:46.500 --> 01:40:49.460
You'll hear about it this afternoon.

01:40:49.460 --> 01:40:50.460
At lunch?

01:40:50.460 --> 01:40:52.140
No, this afternoon.

01:40:52.140 --> 01:40:55.500
I happen to find out that you're taking Miss Baker to tea.

01:40:55.500 --> 01:40:57.100
Do you mean you're in love with Miss Baker?

01:40:57.100 --> 01:40:58.100
No, old sport.

01:40:58.100 --> 01:40:59.300
I'm not.

01:40:59.300 --> 01:41:03.460
But Miss Baker has kindly consented to speak to you about this matter.

01:41:03.460 --> 01:41:09.900
I hadn't the faintest idea what this matter was, but I was more annoyed than interested.

01:41:09.900 --> 01:41:13.740
I hadn't asked Jordan to tea in order to discuss Mr. J. Gatsby.

01:41:13.740 --> 01:41:17.820
I was sure the request would be something utterly fantastic, and for a moment I was sorry

01:41:17.820 --> 01:41:21.500
I'd ever said foot upon his overpopulated lawn.

01:41:21.500 --> 01:41:23.020
He wouldn't say another word.

01:41:23.020 --> 01:41:26.260
His correctness grew on him as we near the city.

01:41:26.260 --> 01:41:31.140
We passed Port Roosevelt, where there was a glimpse of red-belted ocean-going ships,

01:41:31.140 --> 01:41:37.260
and sped along a cobbled slum lined with the dark, undesired saloons of the faded guilt

01:41:37.260 --> 01:41:38.940
1900s.

01:41:38.940 --> 01:41:44.140
Then the valley of ashes opened out on both sides of us, and I had a glimpse of Mrs. Wilson

01:41:44.140 --> 01:41:49.420
straining at the garage pump with panting vitality as we went by.

01:41:49.420 --> 01:41:55.620
With fenders spread like wings we scattered light through half a story, only half, for as

01:41:55.620 --> 01:42:01.340
we twisted among the pillars of the elevated I heard the familiar jug-jug spat of a motorcycle,

01:42:01.340 --> 01:42:03.780
and a frantic policeman rode alongside.

01:42:03.780 --> 01:42:07.780
All right, old sport, called Gatsby we slowed down.

01:42:07.780 --> 01:42:11.180
Taking a white card from his wallet he waved it before the man's eyes.

01:42:11.180 --> 01:42:14.380
Right you are, agreed the policeman tipping his cap.

01:42:14.380 --> 01:42:16.780
Know your next time Mr. Gatsby, excuse me.

01:42:16.780 --> 01:42:18.900
The picture of Oxford?

01:42:18.900 --> 01:42:22.340
I was able to do the commissioner a favor once, and he sends me a Christmas card every

01:42:22.340 --> 01:42:24.620
year.

01:42:24.620 --> 01:42:28.580
To her the great bridge, with the sunlight through the girders making a constant flicker

01:42:28.580 --> 01:42:33.700
upon the moving cars, and the city rising up across the river in white heaps, and sugar

01:42:33.700 --> 01:42:39.100
lumps all built with a wish out of none old factory money.

01:42:39.100 --> 01:42:44.260
The city seen from the Queensborough bridge is always the city seen for the first time,

01:42:44.260 --> 01:42:49.660
and its first wild promise of all the mystery and the beauty in the world.

01:42:49.660 --> 01:42:55.660
A dead man passed us in a hersseept with blooms, followed by two carriages with drawn

01:42:55.660 --> 01:42:59.420
blinds and by more cheerful carriages for friends.

01:42:59.420 --> 01:43:03.540
The friends looked out at us with the tragic eyes and short upper lips of southeastern

01:43:03.540 --> 01:43:08.120
Europe, and I was glad that the sight of Gatsby splendid car was included in their

01:43:08.120 --> 01:43:10.580
somber holiday.

01:43:10.580 --> 01:43:16.160
As we crossed Blackwell's island, a limousine passed us driven by a white chauffeur in which

01:43:16.160 --> 01:43:19.960
sat three modish Negroes, two bucks and a girl.

01:43:19.960 --> 01:43:25.960
I laughed aloud as the yokes of their eyeballs rolled toward us in hotty rivalry.

01:43:25.960 --> 01:43:30.920
Anything can happen now that we've slid over this bridge, I thought anything at all.

01:43:30.920 --> 01:43:39.800
Even Gatsby could happen without any particular wonder.

01:43:39.800 --> 01:43:45.840
Mauring noon, in a well-fanned forty-second street-cellar I met Gatsby for lunch, blinking

01:43:45.840 --> 01:43:51.000
away the brightness of the street outside my eyes picked him out obscurely in the anti-room,

01:43:51.000 --> 01:43:53.000
talking to another man.

01:43:53.000 --> 01:43:56.040
Mr. Carroway, this is my friend Mr. Wolfshime.

01:43:56.040 --> 01:44:01.920
A small, flat-nosed Jew raised his large head and regarded me with two fine growths of hair,

01:44:01.920 --> 01:44:04.840
which luxuriated in either nostril.

01:44:04.840 --> 01:44:08.480
After a moment I discovered his tiny eyes and the half darkness.

01:44:08.480 --> 01:44:12.480
So I took one look at him, said Mr. Wolfshime, shaking my hand earnestly.

01:44:12.480 --> 01:44:14.760
And what do you think I did?

01:44:14.760 --> 01:44:15.760
What?

01:44:15.760 --> 01:44:16.760
I inquired politely.

01:44:16.760 --> 01:44:21.520
But evidently he was not addressing me, or he dropped my hand and covered Gatsby with

01:44:21.520 --> 01:44:23.720
his expressive nose.

01:44:23.720 --> 01:44:27.880
I handed the money to Catch-Paw and I said, all right, Catch-Paw, don't pay him a penny

01:44:27.880 --> 01:44:29.240
till he shuts his mouth.

01:44:29.240 --> 01:44:31.560
He shut it then and there.

01:44:31.560 --> 01:44:35.720
Gatsby took an arm of each of us and moved forward into the restaurant, where upon Mr. Wolfshime

01:44:35.720 --> 01:44:40.960
swallowed a new sentence he was starting and lapsed into a some ambulatory abstraction.

01:44:40.960 --> 01:44:43.600
Hi-balls, asked the head waiter.

01:44:43.600 --> 01:44:47.680
This is a nice restaurant here, said Mr. Wolfshime, looking at the precipitarian nymphs

01:44:47.680 --> 01:44:49.080
on the ceiling.

01:44:49.080 --> 01:44:51.280
But I like a cautious street better.

01:44:51.280 --> 01:44:55.200
Yes, high balls agreed Gatsby, and then to Mr. Wolfshime.

01:44:55.200 --> 01:44:56.640
It's too hot over there.

01:44:56.640 --> 01:44:59.560
Hot and small, yes, said Mr. Wolfshime.

01:44:59.560 --> 01:45:01.960
But full of memories.

01:45:01.960 --> 01:45:04.160
What place is that, I asked?

01:45:04.160 --> 01:45:05.400
The old metropole.

01:45:05.400 --> 01:45:09.320
The old metropole, brooded Mr. Wolfshime gloomily.

01:45:09.320 --> 01:45:13.680
Filled with faces dead and gone, filled with friends gone now forever.

01:45:13.680 --> 01:45:18.880
I can't forget so long as I lived and night they shot Rosie Rolls and Thaldeer.

01:45:18.880 --> 01:45:22.600
It was six of us at the table and Rosie had eaten drunk a lot all evening.

01:45:22.600 --> 01:45:26.040
When it was almost morning, the waiter came up to him with a funny look and said somebody

01:45:26.040 --> 01:45:27.480
wants to speak to him outside.

01:45:27.480 --> 01:45:32.000
All right, says Rosie, begins to get up and I pulled him down in his chair.

01:45:32.000 --> 01:45:36.320
Like the bastards coming here if they want you Rosie, but don't you so help me move

01:45:36.320 --> 01:45:38.040
outside this room?

01:45:38.040 --> 01:45:41.160
If we devraze the blinds we'd have seen daylight.

01:45:41.160 --> 01:45:42.160
Did he go?

01:45:42.160 --> 01:45:43.160
I asked him instantly.

01:45:43.160 --> 01:45:44.160
Sure he went.

01:45:44.160 --> 01:45:47.080
Mr. Wolfshime's nose flashed at me indignantly.

01:45:47.080 --> 01:45:51.640
He turned around in the door and says, don't let that way to take away my coffee.

01:45:51.640 --> 01:45:55.240
Then he went out on the sidewalk and they shot him three times in his full belly and

01:45:55.240 --> 01:45:57.240
drove away.

01:45:57.240 --> 01:46:00.040
Four of them were electrocuted, I said, remembering.

01:46:00.040 --> 01:46:04.680
I've with Becker, his nostrils turned to me in an interested way.

01:46:04.680 --> 01:46:08.000
I understand you're looking for a business connection.

01:46:08.000 --> 01:46:10.840
The juxtaposition of these two remarks was startling.

01:46:10.840 --> 01:46:12.000
Gatsby answered for me.

01:46:12.000 --> 01:46:14.600
Oh no, he exclaimed, this isn't a man.

01:46:14.600 --> 01:46:17.840
No, Mr. Wolfshime seemed disappointed.

01:46:17.840 --> 01:46:18.840
This is just a friend.

01:46:18.840 --> 01:46:20.880
I told you we talk about that some other time.

01:46:20.880 --> 01:46:23.040
I beg your pardon, said Mr. Wolfshime.

01:46:23.040 --> 01:46:25.720
I had a wrong man.

01:46:25.720 --> 01:46:28.040
A succulent hash arrived.

01:46:28.040 --> 01:46:32.000
Mr. Wolfshime, forgetting the more sentimental atmosphere of the old metropole, began to

01:46:32.000 --> 01:46:34.640
eat with ferocious delicacy.

01:46:34.640 --> 01:46:38.840
His eyes, meanwhile, roved very slowly all around the room.

01:46:38.840 --> 01:46:42.720
He completed the arc by turning to inspect the people directly behind.

01:46:42.720 --> 01:46:46.160
I think that except for my presence he would have taken one short glance beneath our

01:46:46.160 --> 01:46:47.880
own table.

01:46:47.880 --> 01:46:50.840
Look here old sport, said Gatsby, leaning toward me.

01:46:50.840 --> 01:46:53.720
I'm afraid I made you a little angry this morning in the car.

01:46:53.720 --> 01:46:55.440
There was the smile again.

01:46:55.440 --> 01:46:57.240
But this time I held out against it.

01:46:57.240 --> 01:46:59.800
I don't like mysteries, I answered.

01:46:59.800 --> 01:47:03.520
And I don't understand why you won't come out frankly and tell me what you want.

01:47:03.520 --> 01:47:06.080
Why has it all got to come through Miss Baker?

01:47:06.080 --> 01:47:09.160
Oh, it's nothing underhand, he assured me.

01:47:09.160 --> 01:47:11.800
Miss Baker's a great sportswoman, you know, and she'd never do anything that wasn't

01:47:11.800 --> 01:47:12.800
all right.

01:47:12.800 --> 01:47:17.080
Suddenly he looked at his watch, jumped up and hurried from the room, leaving me with

01:47:17.080 --> 01:47:19.000
Mr. Wolfshime at the table.

01:47:19.000 --> 01:47:22.920
He asked to tell her phones, said Mr. Wolfshime, following him with his eyes.

01:47:22.920 --> 01:47:23.920
Fine fellow, isn't he?

01:47:23.920 --> 01:47:26.520
Handsome to look at and a perfect gentleman.

01:47:26.600 --> 01:47:27.600
Yes.

01:47:27.600 --> 01:47:29.440
He's an Oxford man.

01:47:29.440 --> 01:47:30.440
Oh.

01:47:30.440 --> 01:47:33.640
Even to Oxford College in England, you know Oxford College?

01:47:33.640 --> 01:47:35.000
I've heard of it.

01:47:35.000 --> 01:47:38.200
It's one of the most famous colleges in the world.

01:47:38.200 --> 01:47:40.960
Have you known Gatsby for a long time, mind-quand?

01:47:40.960 --> 01:47:43.960
Several years, he answered in a gratified way.

01:47:43.960 --> 01:47:47.480
I made the pleasure of his acquaintance just after the war, but I knew I had discovered

01:47:47.480 --> 01:47:50.600
a man of fine breeding after I talked with him an hour.

01:47:50.600 --> 01:47:54.600
I said to myself, there's the kind of man you'd like to take home and introduce to

01:47:54.600 --> 01:47:56.160
your mother and sister.

01:47:56.160 --> 01:47:57.160
He paused.

01:47:57.160 --> 01:47:59.440
I see you're looking at my cuff buttons.

01:47:59.440 --> 01:48:02.240
I hadn't been looking at them, but I did now.

01:48:02.240 --> 01:48:06.320
They were composed of oddly familiar pieces of ivory.

01:48:06.320 --> 01:48:11.160
Fine specimens of human molars, he informed me.

01:48:11.160 --> 01:48:13.320
Well, I inspected them.

01:48:13.320 --> 01:48:15.320
That's a very interesting idea.

01:48:15.320 --> 01:48:16.320
Yeah.

01:48:16.320 --> 01:48:18.560
He flipped his sleeves up under his coat.

01:48:18.560 --> 01:48:19.560
Yeah.

01:48:19.560 --> 01:48:22.360
Gatsby is very careful about women.

01:48:22.360 --> 01:48:24.600
He would never so much as look at a friend's wife.

01:48:24.600 --> 01:48:28.320
A distinctive trust returned to the table and sat down.

01:48:28.320 --> 01:48:31.600
Mr. Wolfshine drank his coffee with a jerk and got to his feet.

01:48:31.600 --> 01:48:36.520
I have enjoyed my lunch, he said, and I'm going to run off from you two young men before

01:48:36.520 --> 01:48:38.760
I outstay my welcome.

01:48:38.760 --> 01:48:42.120
Don't hurry, my, I said Gatsby without enthusiasm.

01:48:42.120 --> 01:48:45.560
Mr. Wolfshine raised his hand in a sort of benediction.

01:48:45.560 --> 01:48:51.000
You're very polite, but I belong to another generation, he announced solemnly.

01:48:51.000 --> 01:48:55.320
Who said he owned, discuss your sports and your young ladies and your—

01:48:55.320 --> 01:48:58.680
He's applied an imaginary noun with another wave of his hand.

01:48:58.680 --> 01:49:05.000
As for me, I am fifty years old and I've owned him, poached myself on you any longer.

01:49:05.000 --> 01:49:09.400
As he shook hands and turned away, his tragic nose was trembling.

01:49:09.400 --> 01:49:13.080
I wanted to find said anything to offend him.

01:49:13.080 --> 01:49:16.960
He becomes very sentimental sometimes, explained to Gatsby.

01:49:16.960 --> 01:49:18.720
This is one of his sentimental days.

01:49:18.720 --> 01:49:22.960
He's quite a character around New York, my denizen of Broadway.

01:49:22.960 --> 01:49:25.280
Who is he, anyhow, an actor?

01:49:25.280 --> 01:49:26.280
No.

01:49:26.280 --> 01:49:27.800
A dentist?

01:49:27.800 --> 01:49:29.800
Myo Wolfshine, no, he's a gambler.

01:49:29.800 --> 01:49:32.320
Gatsby hesitated, then added coolly.

01:49:32.320 --> 01:49:35.960
He's the man who fixed the World Series back in 1919.

01:49:35.960 --> 01:49:38.080
Fixed the World Series?

01:49:38.080 --> 01:49:39.160
I repeated.

01:49:39.160 --> 01:49:41.120
The idea staggered me.

01:49:41.120 --> 01:49:45.440
I remembered, of course, that the World Series had been fixed in 1919.

01:49:45.440 --> 01:49:46.760
What if I had thought of it at all?

01:49:46.760 --> 01:49:49.920
I would have thought of it as a thing that merely happened.

01:49:49.920 --> 01:49:52.320
The end of some inevitable chain.

01:49:52.320 --> 01:49:56.760
It never occurred to me that one man could start to play with the faith of fifty million

01:49:56.760 --> 01:50:00.080
people with the single-mindedness of a burglar blowing a safe.

01:50:00.080 --> 01:50:02.880
How did he happen to do that?

01:50:02.880 --> 01:50:04.360
I asked after a minute.

01:50:04.360 --> 01:50:06.960
He just saw the opportunity.

01:50:06.960 --> 01:50:08.480
Why isn't he in jail?

01:50:08.480 --> 01:50:09.720
I can't get him.

01:50:09.720 --> 01:50:12.120
Old sport is a smart man.

01:50:12.120 --> 01:50:14.760
I insisted on paying the check.

01:50:14.760 --> 01:50:19.760
As the waiter brought my change, I caught sight of Tom Buchanan across the crowded room.

01:50:19.760 --> 01:50:20.760
Come along with me for a minute.

01:50:20.760 --> 01:50:22.920
I said, I've got to say hello to someone.

01:50:22.920 --> 01:50:26.840
When he saw us, Tom jumped up and took half a dozen steps in our direction.

01:50:26.840 --> 01:50:28.200
Where have you been?

01:50:28.200 --> 01:50:32.000
He demanded eagerly, daisy, furious because you haven't called up.

01:50:32.000 --> 01:50:34.480
This is Mr. Gatsby, Mr. Buchanan.

01:50:34.480 --> 01:50:39.720
They shook hands briefly, and a strained, unfamiliar look of embarrassment came over Gatsby's

01:50:39.720 --> 01:50:40.720
face.

01:50:40.720 --> 01:50:43.920
How have you been, anyhow, demanded Tom of me?

01:50:43.920 --> 01:50:45.920
How'd you have to come up this far to eat?

01:50:45.920 --> 01:50:48.280
I have been having lunch with Mr. Gatsby.

01:50:48.280 --> 01:50:54.280
I turned toward Mr. Gatsby, but he was no longer there.

01:50:54.280 --> 01:51:00.160
One October day, in 1917, said Jordan Baker that afternoon, sitting up very straight on

01:51:00.160 --> 01:51:04.080
a straight chair in the tea garden at the Plaza Hotel.

01:51:04.080 --> 01:51:09.120
I was walking along from one place to another, half on the sidewalks and half on the lawns.

01:51:09.120 --> 01:51:12.880
I was happier on the lawns because I had on shoes from England with rubber knobs on the

01:51:12.880 --> 01:51:15.600
soles that bit into the soft ground.

01:51:15.600 --> 01:51:20.280
I had on a new plaid skirt also that blew a little in the wind, and whenever this happened

01:51:20.280 --> 01:51:24.640
the red, white and blue banners in front of all the houses stretched out stiff and said,

01:51:24.640 --> 01:51:28.520
ta-ta-ta-t in a disapproving way.

01:51:28.520 --> 01:51:33.800
The largest of the banners and the largest of the lawns belonged to daisy phays house.

01:51:33.800 --> 01:51:38.840
She was just 18, two years older than me, and by far the most popular of all the young

01:51:38.840 --> 01:51:40.680
girls in Louisville.

01:51:40.680 --> 01:51:45.600
She dressed in whites and had a little white roadster, and all day long the telephone rang

01:51:45.600 --> 01:51:50.400
in her house, and excited young officers from Camp Taylor demanded the privilege of monopolizing

01:51:50.400 --> 01:51:51.880
her that night.

01:51:51.880 --> 01:51:54.120
Anyways, for an hour.

01:51:54.120 --> 01:51:58.040
When I came opposite her house that morning, her white roadster was beside the curb, and

01:51:58.040 --> 01:52:01.160
she was sitting in it with a lieutenant I had never seen before.

01:52:01.160 --> 01:52:05.920
They were so engrossed in each other that she didn't see me until I was five feet away.

01:52:05.920 --> 01:52:08.520
Hello, Jordan, she called unexpectedly.

01:52:08.520 --> 01:52:09.920
Please come here.

01:52:09.920 --> 01:52:13.280
I was flatter that she wanted to speak to me because of all the older girls I admired

01:52:13.280 --> 01:52:14.280
her most.

01:52:14.280 --> 01:52:18.600
She asked me if I was going to the red cross and make bandages, I was.

01:52:18.600 --> 01:52:22.040
Well, then would I tell them that she couldn't come that day.

01:52:22.040 --> 01:52:26.920
The officer looked at daisy while she was speaking, in a way that every young girl wants

01:52:26.920 --> 01:52:29.240
to be looked at at some time.

01:52:29.240 --> 01:52:33.880
And because it seemed romantic to me, I have remembered the incident ever since.

01:52:33.880 --> 01:52:39.840
His name was Jay Gatsby, and I didn't lay eyes on him again for over four years.

01:52:39.840 --> 01:52:43.200
And after I'd met him on Long Island, I didn't realize it.

01:52:43.200 --> 01:52:46.600
By the next year I had a few bows myself, and I began to play in tournaments, so I didn't

01:52:46.600 --> 01:52:48.800
see daisy very often.

01:52:48.800 --> 01:52:53.400
She went with a slightly older crowd when she went with anyone at all.

01:52:53.400 --> 01:52:55.480
Wild rumors were circulating about her.

01:52:55.480 --> 01:52:58.840
How her mother had found her packing her bag one winter night to go to New York and say

01:52:58.840 --> 01:53:01.800
goodbye to a soldier who was going overseas.

01:53:01.800 --> 01:53:05.800
She was effectively prevented, but she wasn't on speaking terms with her family for several

01:53:05.800 --> 01:53:07.400
weeks.

01:53:07.400 --> 01:53:12.000
So that she didn't play around with the soldiers anymore, but only with a few flatfooted, short-sighted,

01:53:12.000 --> 01:53:15.640
young men in town who couldn't get into the army at all.

01:53:15.640 --> 01:53:18.480
By the next autumn she was gay again, gay as ever.

01:53:18.480 --> 01:53:23.560
She had a debut after the armistice, and in February she was presumably engaged to a man

01:53:23.560 --> 01:53:25.520
from New Orleans.

01:53:25.520 --> 01:53:30.080
In June she married Tom Buchanan of Chicago, with more pomp and circumstance than Louisville

01:53:30.080 --> 01:53:31.680
ever knew before.

01:53:31.680 --> 01:53:36.440
He came down with a hundred people in four private cars, and hired a whole floor of the

01:53:36.440 --> 01:53:38.560
seal-bock hotel.

01:53:38.560 --> 01:53:45.240
And the day before the wedding he gave her a string of pearls, valued at $350,000.

01:53:45.240 --> 01:53:46.920
I was a bride's maid.

01:53:46.920 --> 01:53:52.240
I came into her room half an hour before the bridal dinner, and found her lying on her bed

01:53:52.240 --> 01:53:57.800
as lovely as the June night in her flower dress, and as drunk as a monkey.

01:53:57.800 --> 01:54:01.440
She had a bottle of sotern and one hand and a letter in the other.

01:54:02.160 --> 01:54:04.360
Gradually, me, she muttered.

01:54:04.360 --> 01:54:08.200
Never had a drink before, but oh, how I do enjoy it.

01:54:08.200 --> 01:54:09.960
What's the matter, Daisy?

01:54:09.960 --> 01:54:11.800
I was scared, I can tell you.

01:54:11.800 --> 01:54:14.360
I'd never seen a girl like that before.

01:54:14.360 --> 01:54:16.680
Here, dears.

01:54:16.680 --> 01:54:19.920
She groped around in a waste basket she had with her on the bed and pulled out the string

01:54:19.920 --> 01:54:21.520
of pearls.

01:54:21.520 --> 01:54:25.400
Take her downstairs and give them back to whoever they belong to.

01:54:25.400 --> 01:54:28.040
Tell them all, Daisy's changed her mind.

01:54:28.040 --> 01:54:30.760
Say Daisy's changed her mind.

01:54:30.760 --> 01:54:32.240
She began to cry.

01:54:32.240 --> 01:54:34.080
She cried and cried.

01:54:34.080 --> 01:54:37.760
I rushed out and found her mother's maid, and we locked the door and got her into a cold

01:54:37.760 --> 01:54:38.840
bath.

01:54:38.840 --> 01:54:40.000
She wouldn't let go of the letter.

01:54:40.000 --> 01:54:44.320
She took it into the tub with her and squeezed it up into a wet wall, and only let me leave

01:54:44.320 --> 01:54:48.600
it in the soap dish when she saw that it was coming to pieces like snow.

01:54:48.600 --> 01:54:50.560
But she didn't say another word.

01:54:50.560 --> 01:54:54.280
We gave her spirits of ammonia and put ice on her forehead and hooked her back into

01:54:54.280 --> 01:54:58.600
her dress, and half an hour later, when we walked out of the room, the pearls were around

01:54:58.600 --> 01:55:01.560
her neck, and the incident was over.

01:55:01.560 --> 01:55:06.640
Next day at five o'clock she married Tom Buchanan without so much as a shiver, and started off

01:55:06.640 --> 01:55:10.280
on a three-month strip to the south seas.

01:55:10.280 --> 01:55:14.800
I saw them in Santa Barbara when they came back, and I thought I'd never seen a girl so

01:55:14.800 --> 01:55:16.680
mad about her husband.

01:55:16.680 --> 01:55:21.480
If he left the room for a minute she'd look around uneasily and say, where's Tom gone?

01:55:21.480 --> 01:55:25.840
And where the most abstracted expression until she saw him coming in the door.

01:55:25.840 --> 01:55:30.080
He used to sit on the sand with his head in her lap by the hour, rubbing her fingers

01:55:30.080 --> 01:55:33.400
over his eyes, and looking at him with unfathomable delight.

01:55:33.400 --> 01:55:36.400
I was touching to see them together.

01:55:36.400 --> 01:55:40.200
It made you laugh in a hushed, fascinated way.

01:55:40.200 --> 01:55:41.680
That was in August.

01:55:41.680 --> 01:55:46.720
A week after I left Santa Barbara, Tom ran into a wagon on the Ventura road one night,

01:55:46.720 --> 01:55:49.280
and ripped a front wheel off his car.

01:55:49.280 --> 01:55:53.880
The girl who was with him got into the paper's tube because her arm was broken.

01:55:53.880 --> 01:55:57.760
There was one of the chambermaids in the Santa Barbara hotel.

01:55:57.760 --> 01:56:01.560
The next April Daisy had her little girl, and they went to France for a year.

01:56:01.560 --> 01:56:06.240
I saw them one spring in Khan, and later in Doveville, and then they came back to Chicago

01:56:06.240 --> 01:56:08.040
to settle down.

01:56:08.040 --> 01:56:10.440
Daisy was popular in Chicago, as you know.

01:56:10.440 --> 01:56:15.640
They moved with a fast crowd, all of them young and rich and wild, but she came out with

01:56:15.640 --> 01:56:18.840
an absolutely perfect reputation.

01:56:18.840 --> 01:56:20.560
Perhaps because she doesn't drink.

01:56:20.560 --> 01:56:24.240
It's a great advantage not to drink among hard-drinking people.

01:56:24.240 --> 01:56:25.320
You can hold your tongue.

01:56:25.320 --> 01:56:31.120
And moreover, you can time any little irregularity of your own so that everybody else is so blind,

01:56:31.120 --> 01:56:33.640
that they don't see or care.

01:56:33.640 --> 01:56:36.480
Perhaps Daisy never went in for a more at all.

01:56:36.480 --> 01:56:39.080
And yet there's something in that voice of hers.

01:56:39.080 --> 01:56:44.280
Well, about six weeks ago she heard the name Gatsby for the first time in years.

01:56:44.280 --> 01:56:49.200
It was when I asked you, do you remember, if you knew Gatsby in West Egg?

01:56:49.200 --> 01:56:54.680
Where you had gone home, she came into my room and woke me up and said, what Gatsby?

01:56:54.680 --> 01:56:56.840
And when I described him, I was half asleep.

01:56:56.840 --> 01:57:01.480
She said in the strangest voice that it must be the man she used to know.

01:57:01.480 --> 01:57:04.960
It wasn't until then that I connected this Gatsby with the office.

01:57:04.960 --> 01:57:09.400
When Jordan Baker had finished telling all this, we had left the plaza for half an hour

01:57:09.400 --> 01:57:12.840
and were driving in a Victoria through Central Park.

01:57:12.840 --> 01:57:18.160
The sun had gone down behind the tall apartments of the movie stars in the West 50s, in the

01:57:18.160 --> 01:57:23.520
clear voices of little girls already gathered like crickets on the grass, rose through the

01:57:23.520 --> 01:57:24.520
hot twilight.

01:57:24.520 --> 01:57:31.160
I'm the chic of Arabic, your love belongs to me, at night when you're asleep into your

01:57:31.160 --> 01:57:34.480
tental creep.

01:57:34.480 --> 01:57:37.640
It was a strange coincidence, I said.

01:57:37.640 --> 01:57:40.120
But it wasn't a coincidence at all.

01:57:40.120 --> 01:57:42.120
Why not?

01:57:42.120 --> 01:57:48.800
Gatsby bought that house so that Daisy would be just across the bay.

01:57:48.800 --> 01:57:54.400
Then it had not been merely the stars to which he had spired on that June night.

01:57:54.400 --> 01:58:01.720
He came alive to me, delivered suddenly from the womb of his purposeless splendor.

01:58:01.720 --> 01:58:07.680
He wants to know, continue, Jordan, if you'll invite Daisy to your house some afternoon,

01:58:07.680 --> 01:58:10.480
and then let him come over.

01:58:10.480 --> 01:58:13.000
The modesty of the demand shook me.

01:58:13.000 --> 01:58:19.720
He had waited five years and bought a mansion where he dispensed starlight to casual moths

01:58:19.720 --> 01:58:23.600
so that he could come over some afternoon to a stranger's garden.

01:58:23.600 --> 01:58:28.480
Did I have to know all this before you can ask such a little thing?

01:58:28.480 --> 01:58:29.480
He's afraid.

01:58:29.480 --> 01:58:31.480
He's waited so long.

01:58:31.480 --> 01:58:32.480
He thought you might be offended.

01:58:32.480 --> 01:58:36.320
You see, he's a regular tough underneath at all.

01:58:36.320 --> 01:58:38.120
Something worried me.

01:58:38.120 --> 01:58:40.960
Why didn't he ask you to arrange a meeting?

01:58:40.960 --> 01:58:46.160
He wants her to see his house, she explained, and your house is right next door.

01:58:46.160 --> 01:58:47.480
Oh!

01:58:47.480 --> 01:58:52.040
I think he half expected her to wander into one of his parties some night when on, Jordan.

01:58:52.040 --> 01:58:53.480
But she never did.

01:58:53.480 --> 01:58:58.800
Then he began asking people casually if they knew her, and I was the first one he found.

01:58:58.800 --> 01:59:03.080
It was that night he sent for me at his dance, and you should have heard the elaborate way

01:59:03.080 --> 01:59:05.040
he worked up to it.

01:59:05.040 --> 01:59:09.520
Of course I immediately suggested a luncheon in New York, and I thought he'd go mad.

01:59:09.520 --> 01:59:11.720
I don't want to do anything out of the way, he kept saying.

01:59:11.720 --> 01:59:14.560
I want to see her right next door.

01:59:14.560 --> 01:59:19.400
When I said you were a particular friend of Tom's, he started to abandon the whole idea.

01:59:19.400 --> 01:59:23.400
He doesn't know very much about Tom, though he says he's read a Chicago paper for years

01:59:23.400 --> 01:59:27.720
just on the chance of catching a glimpse of Daisy's name.

01:59:27.720 --> 01:59:33.080
It was dark now, and as we dipped under a little bridge I put my arm around Jordan's

01:59:33.080 --> 01:59:37.800
shoulder, and drew her toward me and asked her to dinner.

01:59:37.800 --> 01:59:42.880
Suddenly I wasn't thinking of Daisy and Gatsby anymore, but of this clean, hard, limited

01:59:42.880 --> 01:59:48.720
person who dealt in universal skepticism, and who leaned back jauntily just within the

01:59:48.720 --> 01:59:50.560
circle of my arm.

01:59:50.560 --> 01:59:55.000
A phrase began to beat in my ears with a sort of heady excitement.

01:59:55.000 --> 02:00:02.320
There are only the pursued, the pursuing, the busy, and the tired.

02:00:02.320 --> 02:00:06.640
And Daisy ought to have something in her life, murmured Jordan to me.

02:00:06.640 --> 02:00:08.440
Does she want to see Gatsby?

02:00:08.440 --> 02:00:10.040
She is not to know about it.

02:00:10.040 --> 02:00:12.200
Gatsby doesn't want her to know.

02:00:12.200 --> 02:00:15.640
You're just supposed to invite her to tea.

02:00:15.640 --> 02:00:21.600
We passed a barrier of dark trees, and then the facade of 59th Street, a block of delicate

02:00:21.600 --> 02:00:25.200
pale light being down into the park.

02:00:25.200 --> 02:00:30.440
Unlike Gatsby and Tom Buchanan I had no girl whose disembodied face floated along the

02:00:30.440 --> 02:00:36.720
dark corners and blinding signs, and so I drew up the girl beside me, tightening my

02:00:36.720 --> 02:00:37.720
arms.

02:00:37.720 --> 02:00:48.840
Her wands, scornful mouth smiled, and so I drew her up again closer this time to my face.

02:00:48.840 --> 02:00:51.000
CHAPTER V

02:00:51.000 --> 02:00:54.560
When I came home to West Egg that night I was afraid for a moment that my house was on

02:00:54.560 --> 02:00:55.760
fire.

02:00:55.760 --> 02:01:01.240
Two o'clock in the whole corner of the peninsula was blazing with light, which fell unreal on

02:01:01.240 --> 02:01:06.720
the shrubbery and made thin elongating glints upon the roadside wires.

02:01:06.720 --> 02:01:11.880
Turning a corner I saw that it was Gatsby's house lit from tower to cellar.

02:01:11.880 --> 02:01:16.360
At first I thought it was another party, a wild route that had resolved itself into hide

02:01:16.360 --> 02:01:22.920
and go seek, or sardines in the box, with all the house thrown open to the game.

02:01:22.920 --> 02:01:25.040
But there wasn't a sound.

02:01:25.040 --> 02:01:29.760
He winded in the trees which blew the wires and made the lights go off an arm again, as

02:01:29.760 --> 02:01:32.320
if the house had winked into the darkness.

02:01:32.320 --> 02:01:37.200
As my taxi groaned away I saw Gatsby walking toward me across his lawn.

02:01:37.200 --> 02:01:40.400
«Your place looks like the world's fair,' I said.

02:01:40.400 --> 02:01:41.400
«Does it?

02:01:41.400 --> 02:01:44.040
Me turned his eyes toward it absently.

02:01:44.040 --> 02:01:46.880
I have been glancing into some of the rooms.

02:01:46.880 --> 02:01:49.480
Let's go to Coney Island Oldsport and my car.

02:01:49.480 --> 02:01:51.280
«It's too late?

02:01:51.280 --> 02:01:55.160
Well, suppose we take a plunge in the swimming pool I have made use of it all summer.

02:01:55.160 --> 02:01:57.640
I've got to go to bed.

02:01:57.640 --> 02:01:59.600
All right.

02:01:59.600 --> 02:02:03.640
He waited, looking at me with suppressed eagerness.

02:02:03.640 --> 02:02:06.560
I talked with Miss Baker, I said after a moment.

02:02:06.560 --> 02:02:10.600
I'm going to call up Daisy tomorrow and an invite her over here to tea.

02:02:10.600 --> 02:02:13.400
«Oh, that's all right,' he said carelessly.

02:02:13.400 --> 02:02:15.800
I don't want to put you to any trouble.

02:02:15.800 --> 02:02:17.480
What day would suit you?

02:02:17.480 --> 02:02:18.480
What day would suit you?'

02:02:18.480 --> 02:02:19.480
He corrected me quickly.

02:02:19.480 --> 02:02:21.880
I don't want to put you to any trouble, you see.

02:02:21.880 --> 02:02:23.560
How about the day after tomorrow?

02:02:23.560 --> 02:02:25.320
He considered for a moment.

02:02:25.320 --> 02:02:29.400
Then with reluctance, I want to get the grass cut, he said.

02:02:29.400 --> 02:02:30.400
We both looked at the grass.

02:02:30.400 --> 02:02:36.320
There was a sharp line where my ragged lawn ended and the darker well-kept expanse of his

02:02:36.320 --> 02:02:37.320
began.

02:02:37.320 --> 02:02:40.480
I suspected that he meant my grass.

02:02:40.480 --> 02:02:43.800
There's another little thing he said uncertainly and hesitated.

02:02:43.800 --> 02:02:46.320
Would you rather put it off for a few days?

02:02:46.320 --> 02:02:47.320
I asked.

02:02:47.320 --> 02:02:48.760
Oh, it isn't about that.

02:02:48.760 --> 02:02:52.320
At least he fumbled with a series of beginnings.

02:02:52.320 --> 02:02:57.120
Why I thought, why look here old sport, you don't make much money do you?

02:02:57.120 --> 02:02:58.360
Not very much.

02:02:58.360 --> 02:03:02.240
This seemed to reassure him and he continued more confidently.

02:03:02.240 --> 02:03:04.920
I thought you didn't, if you'll pardon my…

02:03:04.920 --> 02:03:09.440
You see, I carry on a little business on the side, a sort of sideline you understand.

02:03:09.440 --> 02:03:14.200
And I thought that if you don't make very much, you're selling bonds, aren't you old sport?

02:03:14.200 --> 02:03:15.200
Trying to?

02:03:15.200 --> 02:03:17.080
Wow, this would interest you.

02:03:17.080 --> 02:03:20.360
It wouldn't take up much of your time and you might pick up a nice bit of money.

02:03:20.360 --> 02:03:23.800
It happens to be a rather confidential sort of thing.

02:03:23.800 --> 02:03:28.360
I realize now that under different circumstances that conversation might have been one of the

02:03:28.360 --> 02:03:30.720
crises of my life.

02:03:30.720 --> 02:03:35.800
But because the offer was obviously and tactlessly for a service to be rendered, I had no choice

02:03:35.800 --> 02:03:37.400
except to cut him off there.

02:03:37.400 --> 02:03:39.200
I've got my hands full, I said.

02:03:39.200 --> 02:03:42.280
I'm much obliged, but I couldn't take on any more work.

02:03:42.280 --> 02:03:45.480
He wouldn't have to do any business with Wolfshine, evidently he thought that I was

02:03:45.480 --> 02:03:49.200
shying away from the Gnexion mentioned at lunch.

02:03:49.200 --> 02:03:51.000
But I assured him he was wrong.

02:03:51.000 --> 02:03:54.240
He waited a moment longer, hoping I'd begin a conversation.

02:03:54.240 --> 02:03:56.640
But I was too absorbed to be responsive, so he went out.

02:03:56.640 --> 02:03:59.560
The evening had made me light-headed and happy.

02:03:59.560 --> 02:04:03.560
I think I walked into a deep sleep as I entered my front door.

02:04:03.560 --> 02:04:08.240
So I don't know whether or not Gatsby went to Coney Island or for how many hours he glanced

02:04:08.240 --> 02:04:12.640
into rooms while his house blazed godly on.

02:04:12.640 --> 02:04:16.560
I called up Daisy from the office next morning and invited her to come to tea.

02:04:16.560 --> 02:04:19.000
Don't bring Tom, I want her.

02:04:19.000 --> 02:04:20.000
What?

02:04:20.000 --> 02:04:21.520
Don't bring Tom.

02:04:21.520 --> 02:04:25.800
Who is Tom, she asked innocently.

02:04:25.800 --> 02:04:28.200
The day agreed upon was pouring rain.

02:04:28.200 --> 02:04:33.120
At eleven o'clock a man in a raincoat dragging a lawnmower tapped at my front door and said

02:04:33.120 --> 02:04:36.400
that Mr. Gatsby had sent him over to cut my grass.

02:04:36.400 --> 02:04:40.800
This reminded me that I had forgotten to tell my fin to come back, so I drove into West

02:04:40.800 --> 02:04:45.920
Egg Village to search for her among soggy whitewashed alleys and to buy some cups and

02:04:45.920 --> 02:04:48.120
lemons and flowers.

02:04:48.120 --> 02:04:53.360
The flowers were unnecessary for a two o'clock a green house arrived from Gatsby's with

02:04:53.360 --> 02:04:56.560
innumerable receptacles to contain it.

02:04:56.560 --> 02:05:01.520
An hour later the front door opened nervously and Gatsby in a white flannel suit, silver

02:05:01.520 --> 02:05:04.600
shirt and gold-colored tie hurried in.

02:05:04.600 --> 02:05:08.880
He was pale, and there were dark signs of sleeplessness beneath his eyes.

02:05:09.440 --> 02:05:10.520
Is everything alright?

02:05:10.520 --> 02:05:12.160
He has to immediately.

02:05:12.160 --> 02:05:14.560
The grass looks fine, if that's what you mean.

02:05:14.560 --> 02:05:15.560
What grass?

02:05:15.560 --> 02:05:16.560
He inquired blankly.

02:05:16.560 --> 02:05:18.840
Oh, the grass in the yard.

02:05:18.840 --> 02:05:22.200
He looked out the window at it, but judging from his expression, I don't believe he saw

02:05:22.200 --> 02:05:23.200
a thing.

02:05:23.200 --> 02:05:25.680
It looks very good, he remarked vaguely.

02:05:25.680 --> 02:05:28.160
One of the papers said they thought the rain would stop about four.

02:05:28.160 --> 02:05:29.160
I think it was the journal.

02:05:29.160 --> 02:05:33.240
Have you gone everything you need in the shape of T?

02:05:33.240 --> 02:05:37.560
I took him into the pantry where he looked a little reproachfully at the fin.

02:05:37.560 --> 02:05:42.040
Together we scrutinise the twelve lemon cakes from the delicatessen shop.

02:05:42.040 --> 02:05:44.080
Will they do, I asked?

02:05:44.080 --> 02:05:48.480
Of course, of course, they're fine, and he added hollerly old sport.

02:05:48.480 --> 02:05:53.920
The rain cooled about half past three to a damp mist, through which occasional thin drops

02:05:53.920 --> 02:05:56.160
swam like dew.

02:05:56.160 --> 02:06:00.360
Gatsby looked with vacant eyes through a copy of Clay's economics, starting at the

02:06:00.360 --> 02:06:05.480
finish tread that shook the kitchen floor and peering toward the blear-do windows from

02:06:05.480 --> 02:06:06.480
time to time.

02:06:06.480 --> 02:06:11.440
As if his series of invisible but alarming happenings were taking place outside.

02:06:11.440 --> 02:06:15.920
Finally he got up and informed me in an uncertain voice that he was going home.

02:06:15.920 --> 02:06:17.800
Why is that?

02:06:17.800 --> 02:06:19.600
Nobody's coming to tea, it's too late.

02:06:19.600 --> 02:06:23.600
We looked at his watch, as if there was some pressing demand on his time elsewhere.

02:06:23.600 --> 02:06:25.600
I can't wait all day.

02:06:25.600 --> 02:06:29.120
Don't be silly, it's just two minutes to four.

02:06:29.120 --> 02:06:34.480
He sat down miserably as if I had pushed him, and simultaneously there was the sound of

02:06:34.480 --> 02:06:36.760
a motor turning into my lane.

02:06:36.760 --> 02:06:41.360
We both jumped up, and a little harrowed myself, I went out into the yard.

02:06:41.360 --> 02:06:46.760
Under the dripping bare lilac trees a large open car was coming up the drive.

02:06:46.760 --> 02:06:47.760
It stopped.

02:06:47.760 --> 02:06:52.800
Daisy's face, tipped sideways beneath a three-cornered lavender hat, looked out at me with a bright,

02:06:52.800 --> 02:06:54.760
ecstatic smile.

02:06:54.760 --> 02:06:58.920
Is this absolutely where you live, my dearest one?

02:06:58.920 --> 02:07:04.160
The exhilarating ripple of her voice was a wild tonic in the rain.

02:07:04.160 --> 02:07:08.400
I had to follow the sound of it for a moment up and down with my ear alone before any

02:07:08.400 --> 02:07:10.360
words came through.

02:07:10.360 --> 02:07:16.320
A damp streak of hair lay like a dash of blue paint across her cheek, and her hand was wet

02:07:16.320 --> 02:07:19.880
with glistening drops as I took it to help her from the car.

02:07:19.880 --> 02:07:27.160
Are you in love with me, she said low in my ear, or why did I have to come alone?

02:07:27.160 --> 02:07:29.760
That's the secret of castle rack rent.

02:07:29.760 --> 02:07:33.880
Tell your chauffeur to go far away from her, then in a grave murmur.

02:07:33.880 --> 02:07:35.920
His name is Ferdy.

02:07:35.920 --> 02:07:38.120
Does the gasoline affect his nose?

02:07:38.120 --> 02:07:41.960
I don't think so, she said innocently, why?

02:07:41.960 --> 02:07:43.280
We went in.

02:07:43.280 --> 02:07:46.960
To my overwhelming surprise the living room was deserted.

02:07:46.960 --> 02:07:50.520
Well, that's funny, I exclaimed.

02:07:50.520 --> 02:07:51.800
What's funny?

02:07:51.800 --> 02:07:56.360
She turned her head as there was a light dignified knocking at the front door.

02:07:56.360 --> 02:07:57.960
I went out and opened it.

02:07:57.960 --> 02:08:03.360
Gatsby, pale as death, with his hands plunged like weights in his coat pockets, was standing

02:08:03.360 --> 02:08:07.600
in a puddle of water glaring tragically into my eyes.

02:08:07.600 --> 02:08:11.680
With his hand still in his coat pockets he stalked by me into the hall, turned sharply

02:08:11.680 --> 02:08:15.200
as if he were on a wire and disappeared into the living room.

02:08:15.200 --> 02:08:17.600
It wasn't a bit funny.

02:08:17.600 --> 02:08:22.480
Aware of the loud beating of my own heart I pulled the door too against the increasing

02:08:22.480 --> 02:08:23.480
rain.

02:08:23.480 --> 02:08:27.240
For half a minute there wasn't a sound.

02:08:27.240 --> 02:08:32.000
Then from the living room I heard a sort of choking murmur, and part of a laugh, followed

02:08:32.000 --> 02:08:35.800
by Daisy's voice on a clear artificial note.

02:08:35.800 --> 02:08:39.320
I certainly am awfully glad to see you again.

02:08:39.320 --> 02:08:40.320
A pause.

02:08:40.320 --> 02:08:42.200
It endured horribly.

02:08:42.200 --> 02:08:45.840
I had nothing to do in the halls where I went into the room.

02:08:45.840 --> 02:08:50.920
Gatsby, his hand still in his pockets, was reclining against the mantelpiece in a strained

02:08:50.920 --> 02:08:54.360
counterfeit of perfect ease, even of boredom.

02:08:54.360 --> 02:08:58.400
His head leaned back so far that it rested against the face of a defunct mantelpiece

02:08:58.400 --> 02:09:04.080
clock, and from this position his distraught eyes stared down at Daisy who was sitting frightened

02:09:04.080 --> 02:09:07.400
but graceful on the edge of a stiff chair.

02:09:07.400 --> 02:09:11.520
We've met before, muttered Gatsby.

02:09:11.520 --> 02:09:16.040
His eyes glanced momentarily at me, and his lips parted with an abort of attempt at a

02:09:16.040 --> 02:09:17.040
laugh.

02:09:17.040 --> 02:09:22.400
Luckily the clock took this moment to tilt dangerously at the pressure of his head, whereupon he turned

02:09:22.400 --> 02:09:26.000
and caught it with trembling fingers and set it back in place.

02:09:26.000 --> 02:09:31.320
When he sat down, rigidly, his elbow and the arm of the sofa and his chin in his hand,

02:09:31.320 --> 02:09:34.600
I'm sorry about the clock, he said.

02:09:34.600 --> 02:09:38.120
My own face had now assumed a deep tropical burn.

02:09:38.120 --> 02:09:43.000
I couldn't muster up a single commonplace out of the thousand in my head.

02:09:43.000 --> 02:09:45.920
It's an old clock.

02:09:45.920 --> 02:09:48.000
I told them idiotically.

02:09:48.000 --> 02:09:53.160
I think we all believed for a moment that it had smashed in pieces on the floor.

02:09:53.160 --> 02:09:55.400
We haven't met for many years," said Daisy.

02:09:55.400 --> 02:10:00.440
Her voice as matter of fact is it could ever be five years next November.

02:10:00.440 --> 02:10:05.720
The automatic quality of Gatsby's answer set us all back at least another minute.

02:10:05.720 --> 02:10:09.840
I had them both on their feet with the desperate suggestion that they helped me make tea in

02:10:09.840 --> 02:10:14.840
the kitchen when the demoniac fin brought it in on a tray.

02:10:14.840 --> 02:10:21.240
Amid the welcome confusion of cups and cakes, a certain physical decency established itself,

02:10:21.240 --> 02:10:25.880
Gatsby got himself into a shadow, and while Daisy and I talked looked conscientiously

02:10:25.880 --> 02:10:29.560
from one to the other of us with tense, unhappy eyes.

02:10:29.560 --> 02:10:34.640
However, as calmness wasn't an end in itself, I made an excuse at the first possible moment

02:10:34.640 --> 02:10:36.240
and got to my feet.

02:10:36.240 --> 02:10:39.920
Where are you going, demanded Gatsby in immediate alarm?

02:10:39.920 --> 02:10:41.440
I'll be back.

02:10:41.440 --> 02:10:43.920
I've got to speak to you about something before you go.

02:10:43.920 --> 02:10:47.840
He followed me wildly into the kitchen, closed the door and whispered,

02:10:47.840 --> 02:10:51.000
oh God, in a miserable way.

02:10:51.000 --> 02:10:52.240
What's the matter?

02:10:52.240 --> 02:10:56.800
This is a terrible mistake, he said, shaking his head from side to side, a terrible, terrible

02:10:56.800 --> 02:10:58.300
mistake.

02:10:58.300 --> 02:11:01.880
You just embarrassed, that's all, and luckily I added.

02:11:01.880 --> 02:11:03.480
Daisy's embarrassed too.

02:11:03.480 --> 02:11:05.240
She's embarrassed?

02:11:05.240 --> 02:11:08.160
He repeated incredulously, just as much as you are.

02:11:08.160 --> 02:11:09.880
Don't talk so loud.

02:11:09.880 --> 02:11:14.680
You're acting like a little boy I broke out impatiently, not only that, but you're rude.

02:11:14.680 --> 02:11:16.920
Daisy's sitting in there all alone.

02:11:16.920 --> 02:11:21.840
He raised his hand to stop my words, looked at me with unforgettable reproach and opening

02:11:21.840 --> 02:11:22.840
the door cautious.

02:11:22.840 --> 02:11:27.800
I walked out the back way, just as Gatsby had when he had made his nervous circuit of the

02:11:27.800 --> 02:11:33.800
house half an hour before, and ran for a huge black knotted tree whose mast leaves made

02:11:33.800 --> 02:11:36.080
a fabric against the rain.

02:11:36.080 --> 02:11:41.760
Once more it was pouring, and my irregular lawn well shaved by Gatsby's gardener abounded

02:11:41.760 --> 02:11:45.800
in small muddy swamps and prehistoric marshes.

02:11:45.800 --> 02:11:50.280
There was nothing to look at from under the tree except Gatsby's enormous house, so I

02:11:50.280 --> 02:11:55.440
stared at it, like a cunt at his church steeple for half an hour.

02:11:55.440 --> 02:12:01.520
A brewer had built it early in the period craze, a decade before, and there was a story

02:12:01.520 --> 02:12:06.400
that he'd agreed to pay five years taxes on all the neighboring cottages if the owners

02:12:06.400 --> 02:12:09.760
would have their roofs thatched with straw.

02:12:09.760 --> 02:12:14.080
Perhaps their refusal took the heart out of his plan to found a family, he went into

02:12:14.080 --> 02:12:16.200
an immediate decline.

02:12:16.200 --> 02:12:21.280
His children sold his house with the black wreath still on the door.

02:12:21.280 --> 02:12:25.160
Americans, while occasionally willing to be surfs, have always been obstinate about

02:12:25.160 --> 02:12:27.920
being peasantry.

02:12:27.920 --> 02:12:32.200
After half an hour the sun shone again, and the grocer's automobile rounded Gatsby's

02:12:32.200 --> 02:12:37.520
drive with the raw material for his servants dinner, I felt sure he wouldn't need a spoonful.

02:12:37.520 --> 02:12:42.680
A maid began opening the upper windows of his house, appeared momentarily in each, and

02:12:42.680 --> 02:12:47.520
leaning from a large central bay spat meditatively into the garden.

02:12:47.520 --> 02:12:49.640
It was time I went back.

02:12:49.640 --> 02:12:55.160
While the rain continued, it had seemed like the murmur of their voices, rising and swelling

02:12:55.160 --> 02:12:58.120
a little now and then with gusts of emotion.

02:12:58.120 --> 02:13:03.720
But in the new silence, I felt that silence had fallen within the house, too.

02:13:03.720 --> 02:13:07.920
I went in, after making every possible noise in the kitchen, short of pushing over the

02:13:07.920 --> 02:13:11.400
stove, but I don't believe they heard a sound.

02:13:11.400 --> 02:13:14.640
They were sitting in either end of the couch looking at each other as if some question

02:13:14.640 --> 02:13:20.120
had been asked, or was in the air, and every vestige of embarrassment was gone.

02:13:20.120 --> 02:13:24.800
Daisy's face was smeared with tears, and when I came in she jumped up and began wiping

02:13:24.800 --> 02:13:27.840
at it with her handkerchief before a mirror.

02:13:27.840 --> 02:13:32.080
But there was a change in Gatsby that was simply confounding.

02:13:32.080 --> 02:13:38.840
He literally glowed, without a word or a gesture of exaltation, a new well-being radiated

02:13:38.840 --> 02:13:41.360
from him and filled the little room.

02:13:41.360 --> 02:13:43.760
Oh, hello old sport!

02:13:43.760 --> 02:13:46.920
He said as if he hadn't seen me for years, I thought for a moment it was going to shake

02:13:46.920 --> 02:13:48.560
hands.

02:13:48.560 --> 02:13:50.400
It stopped raining.

02:13:50.400 --> 02:13:51.400
Has it?

02:13:51.400 --> 02:13:55.680
When he realized what I was talking about, that there were twinkle bells of sunshine in

02:13:55.680 --> 02:14:01.400
the room, he smiled like a weatherman, like an ecstatic patron of recurrent light, and

02:14:01.400 --> 02:14:03.400
repeated in use to Daisy.

02:14:03.400 --> 02:14:04.400
What do you think of that?

02:14:04.400 --> 02:14:05.400
It stopped raining.

02:14:05.400 --> 02:14:07.560
I'm glad, Jay.

02:14:07.560 --> 02:14:12.720
Her throat full of aching, grieving beauty told only of her unexpected joy.

02:14:12.720 --> 02:14:16.080
I want you in Daisy to come over to my house, he said.

02:14:16.080 --> 02:14:18.320
I'd like to shore her around.

02:14:18.320 --> 02:14:20.320
You sure you want me to come?

02:14:20.320 --> 02:14:22.400
Absolutely old sport.

02:14:22.400 --> 02:14:24.680
Daisy went upstairs to wash her face too late.

02:14:24.680 --> 02:14:30.480
I thought with humiliation of my towels, while Gatsby and I waited on the lawn.

02:14:30.480 --> 02:14:32.680
My house looks well, doesn't it, in demand.

02:14:32.680 --> 02:14:34.800
See how the whole front of it catches the light?

02:14:34.800 --> 02:14:37.120
I agreed that it was splendid.

02:14:37.120 --> 02:14:42.000
Yes, his eyes went over it, every arch door and square tower.

02:14:42.000 --> 02:14:45.240
It took me just three years to earn the money that bought it.

02:14:45.240 --> 02:14:47.040
I thought you inherited your money.

02:14:47.040 --> 02:14:49.640
I did old sport, he said automatically.

02:14:49.640 --> 02:14:53.600
But I lost most of it in the big panic, the panic of the war.

02:14:53.600 --> 02:14:55.680
I think he hardly knew what he was saying.

02:14:55.680 --> 02:15:00.040
For when I asked him what business he was in, he answered, that's my affair before he

02:15:00.040 --> 02:15:02.360
realized that it wasn't an appropriate reply.

02:15:02.360 --> 02:15:05.760
Oh, I've been in several things, he corrected himself.

02:15:05.760 --> 02:15:08.920
I was in the drug business and then I was in the oil business, but I'm not an either

02:15:08.920 --> 02:15:09.920
one now.

02:15:09.920 --> 02:15:11.640
He looked at me with more attention.

02:15:11.640 --> 02:15:15.160
Do you mean you've been thinking over what I proposed the other night?

02:15:15.160 --> 02:15:19.360
Before I could answer, Daisy came out of the house and two rows of brass buttons on

02:15:19.360 --> 02:15:20.360
her dress.

02:15:20.360 --> 02:15:21.680
She placed there, huh?

02:15:21.680 --> 02:15:22.680
She cried, pointing.

02:15:22.680 --> 02:15:23.680
Do you like it?

02:15:23.680 --> 02:15:28.480
Well, I love it, but I don't see how you live there all alone.

02:15:28.480 --> 02:15:33.240
I keep it always full of interesting people, night and day, people who do interesting things,

02:15:33.240 --> 02:15:35.560
celebrated people.

02:15:35.560 --> 02:15:39.720
Instead of taking the shortcut along the sound, we went down to the road and entered by the

02:15:39.720 --> 02:15:42.120
big poster.

02:15:42.120 --> 02:15:47.640
With enchanting murmurs, Daisy admired this aspect or that of the feudal silhouette

02:15:47.640 --> 02:15:53.480
against the sky, admired the gardens, the sparkling odor of junkwills and the frothy odor

02:15:53.480 --> 02:15:57.800
of Hawthorne and the pale gold odor of Kiss Me at the Gate.

02:15:57.800 --> 02:16:03.640
It was strange to reach the marble steps and find no stir of bright dresses in and out

02:16:03.640 --> 02:16:08.360
of the door, and hear no sound but bird voices in the trees.

02:16:08.360 --> 02:16:14.160
And inside, as we wandered through Marie Antoinette music rooms and restoration salons,

02:16:14.160 --> 02:16:19.120
I felt that there were guests concealed behind every couch and table, under orders to be

02:16:19.120 --> 02:16:22.440
breathlessly silent until we had passed through.

02:16:22.440 --> 02:16:27.320
As Gatsby closed the door of the Martin College Library, I could have sworn I heard the

02:16:27.320 --> 02:16:31.280
out-lied man breaking too ghostly laughter.

02:16:31.280 --> 02:16:37.240
He went upstairs, through period bedrooms, swathed in rows and lavender silk and vivid

02:16:37.240 --> 02:16:43.080
with new flowers, through dressing rooms and pool rooms and bathrooms with sunken baths,

02:16:43.080 --> 02:16:47.480
intruding into one chamber where a dishevelled man in pajamas was doing liver exercises on

02:16:47.480 --> 02:16:48.480
the floor.

02:16:48.480 --> 02:16:51.320
It was Mr. Clipspringer, the border.

02:16:51.320 --> 02:16:55.760
I had seen him wandering hungrily about the beach that morning.

02:16:55.760 --> 02:17:00.960
Finally, we came to Gatsby's own apartment, a bedroom and a bath and an Adam's study,

02:17:00.960 --> 02:17:04.880
where we sat down and drank a glass of some chartreuse he took from a cupboard in the

02:17:04.880 --> 02:17:06.240
wall.

02:17:06.240 --> 02:17:11.560
He hadn't once ceased looking at Daisy, and I think he re-valued everything in his house

02:17:11.560 --> 02:17:16.760
according to the measure of response it drew from her well-loved eyes.

02:17:16.760 --> 02:17:22.360
Sometimes too, he stared around at his possessions in a dazed way, as though in her actual and astounding

02:17:22.360 --> 02:17:28.840
presence none of it was any longer real, once he nearly toppled down a flight of stairs.

02:17:28.840 --> 02:17:32.460
His bedroom was the simplest room of all, except where the dresser was garnished with a

02:17:32.460 --> 02:17:35.760
toilet set of pure dull gold.

02:17:35.760 --> 02:17:41.040
Daisy took the brush with delight and smoothed her hair, whereupon Gatsby sat down and

02:17:41.040 --> 02:17:43.320
shaded his eyes and began to laugh.

02:17:43.320 --> 02:17:47.640
It's the funniest thing old sport, he said hilariously.

02:17:47.640 --> 02:17:53.600
I can't—when I try to—he had passed visibly through two states and was entering upon

02:17:53.600 --> 02:17:54.880
a third.

02:17:54.880 --> 02:18:00.240
For his embarrassment and his unreasoning joy he was consumed with wonder at her presence.

02:18:00.240 --> 02:18:05.680
He had been full of the idea so long, dreamed it right through to the end, waited with his

02:18:05.680 --> 02:18:10.360
teeth set, so to speak at an inconceivable pitch of intensity.

02:18:10.360 --> 02:18:15.960
Now in the reaction he was running down like an overwhelmed clock.

02:18:15.960 --> 02:18:20.760
Recovering himself in a minute he opened for us two hulking patent cabinets, which held

02:18:20.760 --> 02:18:27.000
his mast suits and dressing gowns and ties in his shirts, piled like bricks and stacks

02:18:27.000 --> 02:18:28.000
a dozen high.

02:18:28.000 --> 02:18:32.640
I've got a man in England who buys me clothes, he sends over a selection of things at

02:18:32.640 --> 02:18:35.280
the beginning of each season spring and fall.

02:18:35.280 --> 02:18:40.280
He took out a pile of shirts and began throwing them one by one before us, shirts of sheer

02:18:40.280 --> 02:18:45.680
linen and thick silk and fine flannel which lost their folds as they fell and covered the

02:18:45.680 --> 02:18:48.400
table in many colored disarray.

02:18:48.400 --> 02:18:54.000
While we admired he brought more and the soft rich heap mounted higher, shirts with stripes

02:18:54.000 --> 02:19:00.280
and scrolls and plads in coral and apple green and lavender and faint orange with monograms

02:19:00.280 --> 02:19:05.920
of Indian blue, suddenly with a strange sound daisy bent her head into the shirts and began

02:19:05.920 --> 02:19:07.480
to cry stormily.

02:19:07.480 --> 02:19:14.000
There's such beautiful shirts she sobbed, her voice muffled in the thick folds.

02:19:14.000 --> 02:19:22.240
It makes me sad because I've never seen such such beautiful shirts before.

02:19:22.240 --> 02:19:28.360
This ends disc two, the great Gatsby, disc three.

02:19:28.360 --> 02:19:32.200
After the house we were to see the grounds and the swimming pool and the hydropane in the

02:19:32.200 --> 02:19:37.960
mid-summer flowers, but outside Gatsby's window it began to rain again, so we stood in

02:19:37.960 --> 02:19:42.520
a row looking at the corrugated surface of the sound.

02:19:42.520 --> 02:19:46.760
If it wasn't for the mist we could see your home across the bay, said Gatsby.

02:19:46.760 --> 02:19:51.200
He always had a green light that burns all night at the end of your dock.

02:19:51.200 --> 02:19:57.320
Daisy put her arm through his abruptly, but he seemed absorbed in what he had just said.

02:19:57.320 --> 02:20:02.440
Possibly it had occurred to him that the colossal significance of that light had now vanished

02:20:02.440 --> 02:20:04.320
forever.

02:20:04.320 --> 02:20:08.400
Compared to the great distance that had separated him from Daisy, it had seemed very near

02:20:08.400 --> 02:20:10.400
to her, almost touching her.

02:20:10.400 --> 02:20:14.120
It had seemed as close as a star to the moon.

02:20:14.120 --> 02:20:17.440
Now it was again a green light on a dock.

02:20:17.440 --> 02:20:21.920
His count of enchanted objects had diminished by one.

02:20:21.920 --> 02:20:28.360
I began to walk about the room, examining various indefinite objects in the half-darkness.

02:20:28.360 --> 02:20:33.960
A large photograph of an elderly man in Yachting-Costume attracted me, hung on the wall over his desk.

02:20:33.960 --> 02:20:34.960
Who's this?

02:20:34.960 --> 02:20:35.960
That?

02:20:35.960 --> 02:20:38.560
That's Mr. Dan Codioldsport.

02:20:38.560 --> 02:20:41.160
The name sounded faintly familiar.

02:20:41.160 --> 02:20:42.160
He's dead now.

02:20:42.160 --> 02:20:44.520
He used to be my best friend years ago.

02:20:44.520 --> 02:20:48.640
There was a small picture of Gatsby, also in Yachting-Costume on the Bureau.

02:20:48.640 --> 02:20:54.000
Gatsby, with his head thrown back defiantly, taken apparently when he was about eighteen.

02:20:54.000 --> 02:20:56.480
I adore it, exclaimed Daisy.

02:20:56.480 --> 02:20:57.800
The pompadour.

02:20:57.800 --> 02:21:01.240
You never told me you had a pompadour or a yacht.

02:21:01.240 --> 02:21:02.880
Look at this, at Gatsby quickly.

02:21:02.880 --> 02:21:05.640
Here's a lot of clippings about you.

02:21:05.640 --> 02:21:08.520
They stood side by side, examining it.

02:21:08.520 --> 02:21:13.120
I was going to ask to see the rubies when the phone rang, and Gatsby took up the receiver.

02:21:13.120 --> 02:21:14.120
Yes.

02:21:14.120 --> 02:21:16.360
Well, I can't talk now.

02:21:16.360 --> 02:21:18.480
I can't talk now, old sport.

02:21:18.480 --> 02:21:20.200
I said a small town.

02:21:20.200 --> 02:21:22.560
You must know what a small town is.

02:21:22.560 --> 02:21:26.320
Well, he's no use to us if Detroit is his idea of a small town.

02:21:26.320 --> 02:21:27.320
He rang off.

02:21:27.320 --> 02:21:28.840
Come here, quick!

02:21:28.840 --> 02:21:30.400
Cry Daisy at the window.

02:21:30.400 --> 02:21:34.560
The rain was still falling, but the darkness had parted in the west, and there was a pink

02:21:34.560 --> 02:21:38.320
and golden billow of foamy clouds above the sea.

02:21:38.320 --> 02:21:44.320
Look at that, she whispered, and then after a moment I'd like to just get one of those pink

02:21:44.320 --> 02:21:48.120
clouds and put you in it and push you around.

02:21:48.120 --> 02:21:51.520
I tried to go then, but they wouldn't hear of it.

02:21:51.520 --> 02:21:55.480
Perhaps my presence made them feel more satisfactorily alone.

02:21:55.480 --> 02:21:57.400
I know what will do, said Gatsby.

02:21:57.400 --> 02:22:01.440
He went out of the room calling the meowing, and returned in a few minutes accompanied

02:22:01.440 --> 02:22:06.720
by an embarrassed, slightly worn young man with shell-rimmed glasses and scanty blonde

02:22:06.720 --> 02:22:07.720
hair.

02:22:07.720 --> 02:22:12.800
He was now decently clothed in a sports shirt open at the neck, sneakers and duck trousers

02:22:12.800 --> 02:22:14.960
of a nebulous hue.

02:22:14.960 --> 02:22:18.960
Did we interrupt your exercises in quiet Daisy politely?

02:22:18.960 --> 02:22:24.400
I was asleep, cried Mr. Clipspringer in a spasm of embarrassment.

02:22:24.400 --> 02:22:28.000
That is, I'd been asleep, then I got up.

02:22:28.000 --> 02:22:30.840
Clipspringer plays the piano, said Gatsby, cutting him off.

02:22:30.840 --> 02:22:33.760
Don't you, you ing old sport, but I don't play well.

02:22:33.760 --> 02:22:35.280
I hardly play at all.

02:22:35.280 --> 02:22:38.640
Out of practice, we'll go downstairs, interrupted Gatsby.

02:22:38.640 --> 02:22:39.680
He flipped a switch.

02:22:39.680 --> 02:22:44.560
The grey windows disappeared as the house glowed full of light.

02:22:44.560 --> 02:22:48.320
In the music room Gatsby turned on a solitary lamp beside the piano.

02:22:48.320 --> 02:22:53.160
He lit Daisy cigarette from a trembling match and sat down with her on a couch far across

02:22:53.160 --> 02:22:57.320
the room where there was no light to save with the gleaming floor bounced in from the

02:22:57.320 --> 02:22:58.720
hall.

02:22:58.720 --> 02:23:02.760
When Clipspringer had played the lovenest, he turned around on the bench and searched

02:23:02.760 --> 02:23:05.240
unhappily for Gatsby in the gloom.

02:23:05.240 --> 02:23:08.680
I'm all out of practice, you see, I told you I couldn't play.

02:23:08.680 --> 02:23:12.960
I'm all out of practice, don't talk so much old sport, commanded Gatsby.

02:23:12.960 --> 02:23:14.480
Play!

02:23:14.480 --> 02:23:19.320
In the morning, in the evening, ain't we got fun.

02:23:19.320 --> 02:23:24.200
Outside the wind was loud and there was a faint flow of thunder along the sound.

02:23:24.200 --> 02:23:29.080
All the lights were going on in West Egg now, the electric trains, men carrying were plunging

02:23:29.080 --> 02:23:31.800
home through the rain from New York.

02:23:31.800 --> 02:23:37.080
It was the hour of a profound human change and excitement was generating on the air.

02:23:37.080 --> 02:23:42.280
One thing sure and nothing sure are the rich get rich and the poor get children.

02:23:42.280 --> 02:23:48.160
In the meantime, in between time, as I went over to say goodbye, I saw that the expression

02:23:48.160 --> 02:23:52.720
of bewilderment had come back into Gatsby's face, as though a faint doubt had occurred

02:23:52.720 --> 02:23:56.600
to him as to the quality of his present happiness.

02:23:56.600 --> 02:23:58.360
Almost five years.

02:23:58.360 --> 02:24:03.160
There must have been moments even that afternoon when Daisy tumbled short of his dreams, not

02:24:03.160 --> 02:24:08.200
through her own fault, but because of the colossal vitality of his illusion.

02:24:08.200 --> 02:24:13.160
It had gone beyond her, beyond everything, he had thrown himself into it with a creative

02:24:13.160 --> 02:24:18.320
passion, adding to it all the time, decking it out with every bright feather that drifted

02:24:18.320 --> 02:24:24.280
his way, no amount of fire or freshness can challenge what a man will store up in his

02:24:24.280 --> 02:24:26.480
ghostly heart.

02:24:26.480 --> 02:24:29.520
As I watched him, he adjusted himself a little visibly.

02:24:29.520 --> 02:24:33.800
His hand took hold of hers, and as she said something low in his ear, he turned toward

02:24:33.800 --> 02:24:35.920
her with a rush of emotion.

02:24:35.920 --> 02:24:41.640
I think that voice held him most with its fluctuating feverish warmth, because it couldn't

02:24:41.640 --> 02:24:43.240
be over-dreamed.

02:24:43.240 --> 02:24:47.120
That voice was a deathless song.

02:24:47.120 --> 02:24:51.240
They had forgotten me, but Daisy glanced up and held out her hand.

02:24:51.240 --> 02:24:53.240
Gatsby didn't know me now at all.

02:24:53.240 --> 02:24:58.200
I looked once more at them, and they looked back at me remotely, possessed by intense

02:24:58.200 --> 02:24:59.200
life.

02:24:59.200 --> 02:25:04.680
Then I went out of the room, and down the marble steps into the rain, leaving them there

02:25:04.680 --> 02:25:07.160
together.

02:25:07.160 --> 02:25:09.160
CHAPTER VI

02:25:09.160 --> 02:25:13.640
About this time an ambitious young reporter from New York arrived one morning at Gatsby's

02:25:13.640 --> 02:25:17.200
door and asked him if he had anything to say.

02:25:17.200 --> 02:25:18.960
Anything to say about what?

02:25:18.960 --> 02:25:20.440
Inquired Gatsby politely.

02:25:20.440 --> 02:25:21.440
Why?

02:25:21.440 --> 02:25:23.760
Any statement to give out?

02:25:23.760 --> 02:25:28.280
It transpired, after a confused five minutes, that the man had heard Gatsby's name around

02:25:28.280 --> 02:25:33.280
his office in a connection which he either wouldn't reveal or didn't fully understand.

02:25:33.280 --> 02:25:38.880
This was his day off, and was laudable, initiatively it hurried out to see.

02:25:38.880 --> 02:25:43.320
It was a random shot, and yet the reporter's instinct was right.

02:25:43.320 --> 02:25:47.940
These notoriety spread about by the hundreds who had accepted his hospitality, and so become

02:25:47.940 --> 02:25:53.200
authorities upon his past, had increased all summer until he fell just short of being

02:25:53.200 --> 02:25:54.200
news.

02:25:54.200 --> 02:25:59.200
Contemporary legends, such as the underground pipeline to Canada, attached themselves

02:25:59.200 --> 02:26:00.200
to him.

02:26:00.200 --> 02:26:04.760
And there was one persistent story that he didn't live in a house at all, but in a boat

02:26:04.760 --> 02:26:10.160
that looked like a house and was moved secretly up and down the Long Island shore.

02:26:10.160 --> 02:26:18.120
Why these inventions were a source of satisfaction to James Gats of North Dakota isn't easy to say?

02:26:18.120 --> 02:26:23.360
James Gats, that was really, or at least legally, his name.

02:26:23.360 --> 02:26:27.160
He had changed it at the age of seventeen, and at the specific moment that witnessed the

02:26:27.160 --> 02:26:29.240
beginning of his career.

02:26:29.240 --> 02:26:35.400
When he saw Dan Codys yacht drop anchor over the most insidious, flat-on lake superior.

02:26:35.400 --> 02:26:39.960
It was James Gats who had been loafing along the beach that afternoon in a torn green

02:26:39.960 --> 02:26:42.680
jersey in a pair of canvas pants.

02:26:42.680 --> 02:26:47.800
But it was already James Gatsby, who borrowed a rowboat, pulled out to the two alumnae,

02:26:47.800 --> 02:26:53.320
and informed Codys that a wind might catch him, and break him up in half an hour.

02:26:53.320 --> 02:26:56.920
I suppose he'd had the name ready for a long time, even then.

02:26:56.920 --> 02:26:59.920
His parents were shiftless and unsuccessful farm people.

02:26:59.920 --> 02:27:03.720
His imagination had never really accepted them as his parents at all.

02:27:03.720 --> 02:27:08.880
The truth was that James Gatsby of West Egg Long Island sprang from his platonic conception

02:27:08.880 --> 02:27:09.880
of himself.

02:27:09.880 --> 02:27:15.240
He was a son of God, a phrase which, if it means anything, means just that, and he must

02:27:15.240 --> 02:27:22.480
be about his father's business, the service of a vast, vulgar and merititious beauty.

02:27:22.480 --> 02:27:26.920
So he invented just the sort of J. Gatsby that a seventeen-year-old boy would be likely

02:27:26.920 --> 02:27:32.480
to invent, and to this conception he was faithful to the end.

02:27:32.480 --> 02:27:36.320
For over a year he had been beating his way along a south shore of Lake Superior as a

02:27:36.320 --> 02:27:41.760
clandigger and a salmon-fisher, or in any other capacity that brought him food and bed.

02:27:41.760 --> 02:27:47.240
His brown, hardening body lived naturally through the half-fierce, half-lazy work of

02:27:47.240 --> 02:27:49.160
the bracing days.

02:27:49.160 --> 02:27:54.160
He knew women early, and since they spoiled him he became contemptuous of them, of young

02:27:54.160 --> 02:27:58.240
virgins because they were ignorant of the others because they were hysterical about

02:27:58.240 --> 02:28:03.520
things which in his overwhelming self-absorption he took for granted.

02:28:03.520 --> 02:28:07.160
But his heart was in a constant, turbulent riot.

02:28:07.160 --> 02:28:12.640
The most grotesque and fantastic conceits haunted him in his bed at night.

02:28:12.640 --> 02:28:18.200
A universe of ineffable gardener spun itself out in his brain while the clock ticked on

02:28:18.200 --> 02:28:24.320
the washstand, and the moon soaked with wet light his tangled clothes upon the floor.

02:28:24.320 --> 02:28:30.040
Each night he added to the pattern of his fancies until drowsiness closed down upon some vivid

02:28:30.040 --> 02:28:32.960
scene with an oblivious embrace.

02:28:32.960 --> 02:28:37.200
For a while these reveries provided an outlet for his imagination.

02:28:37.200 --> 02:28:43.200
They were a satisfactory hint of the unreality of reality, a promise that the rock of the

02:28:43.200 --> 02:28:47.960
world was founded securely on a fairies wing.

02:28:47.960 --> 02:28:52.560
An instinct toward his future glory had led him some months before to the small Lutheran

02:28:52.560 --> 02:28:55.760
College of St. Olaf in southern Minnesota.

02:28:55.760 --> 02:29:00.360
He stayed there two weeks, dismayed at its ferocious indifference to the drums of his

02:29:00.360 --> 02:29:05.520
destiny, to destiny itself, and despising the janitors work with which he was to pay

02:29:05.520 --> 02:29:07.200
his way through.

02:29:07.200 --> 02:29:10.360
Then he drifted back to Lake Superior, and he was still searching for something to do

02:29:10.360 --> 02:29:13.120
on the day that Dan Cody's yacht dropped anchor.

02:29:13.120 --> 02:29:18.200
Cody was fifty years old then, a product of the Nevada Silver Fields, of the Yukon,

02:29:18.200 --> 02:29:21.360
of every rush for metals in 75.

02:29:21.360 --> 02:29:25.640
The transactions in Montana copper that made him many times a millionaire found him physically

02:29:25.640 --> 02:29:29.760
robust, but on the verge of soft-mindedness.

02:29:29.760 --> 02:29:34.960
One suspecting this, an infinite number of women tried to separate him from his money.

02:29:34.960 --> 02:29:40.280
The none-too-savory ramifications by which Ella Kay, the newspaper woman, played Madame,

02:29:40.280 --> 02:29:44.680
DeMent and Olt, of his weakness, and sent him to see in a yacht, were common knowledge

02:29:44.680 --> 02:29:48.440
in the turgid subjournalism of 1902.

02:29:48.440 --> 02:29:53.360
He had been coasting along all two hospitable shores for five years when he turned up as

02:29:53.360 --> 02:29:58.080
James Gatz's destiny in Little Girl Bay.

02:29:58.080 --> 02:30:02.600
To young Gatz resting on his ores and looking up at the rail deck that yacht represented

02:30:02.600 --> 02:30:05.720
all the beauty and glamour in the world.

02:30:05.720 --> 02:30:07.200
I suppose he smiled at Cody.

02:30:07.200 --> 02:30:10.960
He had probably discovered that people liked him when he smiled.

02:30:10.960 --> 02:30:15.600
At any rate, Cody asked him a few questions, one of them elicited the brand new name, and

02:30:15.600 --> 02:30:18.680
found that he was quick and extravagantly ambitious.

02:30:18.680 --> 02:30:23.720
A few days later he took him to Duluth, and bought him a blue coat, six pairs of white,

02:30:23.720 --> 02:30:26.680
duck trousers, and a yachting cap.

02:30:26.680 --> 02:30:30.760
And when the two were lonely left for the West Indies and the Barbary Coast, Gatz be

02:30:30.760 --> 02:30:32.880
left, too.

02:30:32.880 --> 02:30:36.520
He was employed in a vague personal capacity, while he remained with Cody.

02:30:36.520 --> 02:30:41.360
He was in turn steward, mate, skipper, secretary, and even jailer for Dan Cody's sober, knew

02:30:41.360 --> 02:30:47.160
what lavish doings Dan Cody drunk might soon be about, and he provided for such contingencies

02:30:47.160 --> 02:30:50.960
by reposing more and more trust in Gatz be.

02:30:50.960 --> 02:30:56.080
The arrangement lasted five years during which the boat went three times around the continent.

02:30:56.080 --> 02:30:59.960
It might have lasted indefinitely, except for the fact that Ella Kay came on board one

02:30:59.960 --> 02:31:05.920
night in Boston, and a week later Dan Cody inhospitably died.

02:31:05.920 --> 02:31:11.920
I remember the portrait of him, up in Gatz be's bedroom, a grave, florid man with a hard,

02:31:11.920 --> 02:31:13.200
empty face.

02:31:13.200 --> 02:31:18.000
The pioneer de Batshi, who, during one phase of American life, brought back to the Eastern

02:31:18.000 --> 02:31:22.880
Seaboard, the savage violence of the frontier brawl in Saloon.

02:31:22.880 --> 02:31:26.760
He was indirectly due to Cody that Gatz be drank so little.

02:31:26.760 --> 02:31:31.280
Sometimes in the course of gay parties women used to rub champagne into his hair.

02:31:31.280 --> 02:31:35.440
For himself he formed the habit of letting liquor alone.

02:31:35.440 --> 02:31:40.960
And it was from Cody that he inherited money, a legacy of twenty-five thousand dollars.

02:31:40.960 --> 02:31:42.480
He didn't get it.

02:31:42.480 --> 02:31:46.000
He never understood the legal device that was used against him, but what remained of the

02:31:46.000 --> 02:31:49.240
millions went intact to Ella Kay.

02:31:49.240 --> 02:31:52.400
He was left with his singularly appropriate education.

02:31:52.400 --> 02:31:59.160
The vague contour of Jay Gatz be had filled out to the substantiality of a man.

02:31:59.160 --> 02:32:02.960
He told me all this very much later, but I've put it down here with the idea of exploding

02:32:02.960 --> 02:32:08.200
those first wild rumours about his antecedents, which weren't even thinkly true.

02:32:08.200 --> 02:32:12.160
Moreover he told it to me at a time of confusion when I had reached the point of believing

02:32:12.160 --> 02:32:17.800
everything and us short a halt while Gatz be so to speak caught his breath, to clear this

02:32:17.800 --> 02:32:21.000
set of misconceptions away.

02:32:21.000 --> 02:32:24.960
It was a halt too in my association with his affairs.

02:32:24.960 --> 02:32:28.360
For several weeks I didn't see him or hear his voice on the phone.

02:32:28.360 --> 02:32:32.360
Mostly I was in New York, trotting around with Jordan and trying to ingratiate myself

02:32:32.360 --> 02:32:34.320
with her senile aunt.

02:32:34.320 --> 02:32:37.520
But finally I went over to his house one Sunday afternoon.

02:32:37.520 --> 02:32:42.160
I hadn't been there two minutes when somebody brought Tom Buchanan in for a drink.

02:32:42.160 --> 02:32:44.320
I was startled, naturally.

02:32:44.320 --> 02:32:48.040
What the really surprising thing was that it hadn't happened before.

02:32:48.040 --> 02:32:52.920
There were a party of three on horseback, Tom and a man named Sloan and a pretty woman

02:32:52.920 --> 02:32:56.320
in a brown riding habit who had been there previously.

02:32:56.320 --> 02:32:58.960
I'm delighted to see you, said Gatz be standing on his porch.

02:32:58.960 --> 02:33:01.560
I'm delighted that you dropped in.

02:33:01.560 --> 02:33:03.880
As though they cared.

02:33:03.880 --> 02:33:06.680
Sit right down, have a cigarette or a cigar.

02:33:06.680 --> 02:33:09.640
He walked around the room quickly, ringing bells.

02:33:09.640 --> 02:33:12.120
I'll have something for you to drink in just a minute.

02:33:12.120 --> 02:33:15.880
He was profoundly affected by the fact that Tom was there.

02:33:15.880 --> 02:33:19.400
But he would be uneasy anyhow until he had given them something, realizing in a vague

02:33:19.400 --> 02:33:21.640
way that that was all they came for.

02:33:21.640 --> 02:33:23.040
Mr. Sloan wanted nothing.

02:33:23.040 --> 02:33:26.080
Elemanade, no thanks, a little champagne, nothing at all, thanks.

02:33:26.080 --> 02:33:27.760
I'm sorry.

02:33:27.760 --> 02:33:29.720
Did you have a nice ride?

02:33:29.720 --> 02:33:31.640
Very good roads around here.

02:33:31.640 --> 02:33:36.720
I suppose the automobile's moved by an irresistible impulse.

02:33:36.720 --> 02:33:40.920
Gatz be turned to Tom, who would accept it the introduction as a stranger.

02:33:40.920 --> 02:33:43.800
I believe we've met somewhere before Mr. Buchanan.

02:33:43.800 --> 02:33:47.880
Oh, yes, said Tom, gruffly polite, but obviously not remembering.

02:33:47.880 --> 02:33:48.880
So we did.

02:33:48.880 --> 02:33:51.240
I remember very well.

02:33:51.240 --> 02:33:53.120
About two weeks ago.

02:33:53.120 --> 02:33:55.560
That's right, you were with Nick here.

02:33:55.560 --> 02:33:59.560
I know your wife continued Gatz be almost aggressively.

02:33:59.560 --> 02:34:00.560
That's so.

02:34:00.560 --> 02:34:01.880
Tom turned to me.

02:34:01.880 --> 02:34:03.600
You live near here, Nick?

02:34:03.600 --> 02:34:04.600
Next door.

02:34:04.600 --> 02:34:05.600
That's so.

02:34:05.600 --> 02:34:10.200
Mr. Sloan didn't enter into the conversation, but lounged back heartily in his chair.

02:34:10.200 --> 02:34:15.120
The woman said nothing either, until unexpectedly after two high balls she became cordial.

02:34:15.120 --> 02:34:18.760
Will all come over to your next party, Mr. Gatz be?

02:34:18.760 --> 02:34:20.600
She suggested, what do you say?

02:34:20.600 --> 02:34:21.600
Certainly.

02:34:21.600 --> 02:34:23.400
Be delighted to have you.

02:34:23.400 --> 02:34:26.040
Be very nice, said Mr. Sloan, without gratitude.

02:34:26.040 --> 02:34:28.480
Well, thank God I'd be starting home.

02:34:28.480 --> 02:34:30.680
Please don't hurry, Gatz be urge them.

02:34:30.680 --> 02:34:34.000
He had control of himself now, and he wanted to see more of Tom.

02:34:34.000 --> 02:34:35.960
Why don't you stay for supper?

02:34:35.960 --> 02:34:39.480
I wouldn't be surprised if some other people dropped in from New York.

02:34:39.480 --> 02:34:42.440
You come to supper with me, said the lady enthusiastically.

02:34:42.440 --> 02:34:43.520
Both of you.

02:34:43.520 --> 02:34:45.000
This included me.

02:34:45.000 --> 02:34:46.680
Mr. Sloan got to his feet.

02:34:46.680 --> 02:34:49.560
Come along, he said, but to her only.

02:34:49.560 --> 02:34:51.080
I mean it, she insisted.

02:34:51.080 --> 02:34:52.160
I'd love to have you.

02:34:52.160 --> 02:34:53.680
Lots of room.

02:34:53.680 --> 02:34:55.160
Gatz be looked at me questioningingly.

02:34:55.160 --> 02:34:59.440
He wanted to go, and he didn't see that Mr. Sloan had determined he shouldn't.

02:34:59.440 --> 02:35:02.560
I'm afraid I won't be able to, I said.

02:35:02.560 --> 02:35:06.160
Well, you come, she urged concentrating on Gatz be.

02:35:06.160 --> 02:35:08.720
Mr. Sloan murmured something close to her ear.

02:35:08.720 --> 02:35:12.200
She won't be late if we start now, she insisted aloud.

02:35:12.200 --> 02:35:14.040
I haven't got a horse, said Gatz be.

02:35:14.040 --> 02:35:16.520
I used to ride in the army, but I've never bought a horse.

02:35:16.520 --> 02:35:17.880
I'll have to follow you in my car.

02:35:17.880 --> 02:35:19.960
Excuse me for just a minute.

02:35:19.960 --> 02:35:23.360
The rest of his walked out on the porch, where Sloan and the lady began an impassioned

02:35:23.360 --> 02:35:24.880
conversation aside.

02:35:24.880 --> 02:35:28.200
My God, I believe the man's coming, said Tom.

02:35:28.200 --> 02:35:30.520
Doesn't he know she doesn't want him?

02:35:30.520 --> 02:35:32.360
She says she does want him.

02:35:32.360 --> 02:35:35.320
She has a big dinner party, and he won't know what's sold there.

02:35:35.320 --> 02:35:36.320
He frowned.

02:35:36.320 --> 02:35:38.480
I went away in the devil he met Daisy.

02:35:38.480 --> 02:35:42.880
By God, I may be old fashioned in my ideas, but women run around too much these days to

02:35:42.880 --> 02:35:44.080
suit me.

02:35:44.080 --> 02:35:46.560
They meet all kinds of crazy fish.

02:35:46.560 --> 02:35:50.720
Suddenly Mr. Sloan and the lady walk down the steps and mounted their horses.

02:35:50.720 --> 02:35:52.560
Come on, said Mr. Sloan to Tom.

02:35:52.560 --> 02:35:55.000
We're late, we've got to go, and then to me.

02:35:55.000 --> 02:35:57.720
Tell him we couldn't wait, will you?

02:35:57.720 --> 02:36:02.760
Tom and I shook hands, the rest of us exchanged a cool nod, and they trotted quickly down

02:36:02.760 --> 02:36:07.560
the drive, disappearing under the August foliage, just as Gatsby with hat and light overcoat

02:36:07.560 --> 02:36:12.480
in hand was evidently perturbed at Daisy's running around alone, for on the following Saturday

02:36:12.480 --> 02:36:15.840
night he came with her to Gatsby's party.

02:36:15.840 --> 02:36:20.440
Perhaps his presence gave the evening its peculiar quality of oppressiveness.

02:36:20.440 --> 02:36:24.320
It stands out in my memory from Gatsby's other parties that summer.

02:36:24.320 --> 02:36:28.400
There were the same people, or at least the same sort of people, the same profusion of

02:36:28.400 --> 02:36:33.240
champagne, the same many coloured, many keyed commotion.

02:36:33.240 --> 02:36:38.400
But I felt an unpleasantness in the air, a pervading harshness that hadn't been there

02:36:38.400 --> 02:36:39.400
before.

02:36:39.400 --> 02:36:44.240
Or perhaps I had merely grown used to it, grown to accept West Egg as a world complete in

02:36:44.240 --> 02:36:49.480
itself with its own standards and its own great figures, second to nothing because it had

02:36:49.480 --> 02:36:52.160
no consciousness of being so.

02:36:52.160 --> 02:36:56.360
And now I was looking at it again, through Daisy's eyes.

02:36:56.360 --> 02:37:01.200
It is invariably saddening to look through new eyes at things upon which you have expended

02:37:01.200 --> 02:37:04.280
your own powers of adjustment.

02:37:04.280 --> 02:37:10.080
They arrived twilight, and as we strolled out among the sparkling hundreds, Daisy's voice

02:37:10.080 --> 02:37:13.480
was playing murmurous tricks in her throat.

02:37:13.480 --> 02:37:17.680
These things excite me so, she whispered, if you want to kiss me at any time during the

02:37:17.680 --> 02:37:21.080
evening, Nick just let me know, and I'll be glad to arrange it for you.

02:37:21.080 --> 02:37:26.980
You mention my name, or present a green card, and giving out green look around, suggested

02:37:26.980 --> 02:37:27.980
Gatsby.

02:37:27.980 --> 02:37:31.880
I'm looking around, I'm having a marvelous, you must see the faces of many people you've

02:37:31.880 --> 02:37:33.200
heard about.

02:37:33.200 --> 02:37:35.880
Tom's arrogant eyes roamed the crowd.

02:37:35.880 --> 02:37:38.760
We don't go around very much, he said.

02:37:38.760 --> 02:37:42.240
In fact I was just thinking I don't know a soul here.

02:37:42.240 --> 02:37:47.360
Perhaps you know that lady, Gatsby indicated a gorgeous scarcely human orchid of a woman

02:37:47.360 --> 02:37:50.880
who sat in state under a white plumb tree.

02:37:50.880 --> 02:37:56.320
Tom and Daisy stared with that peculiarly unreal feeling that accompanies the recognition

02:37:56.320 --> 02:37:59.880
of a hitherto ghostly celebrity of the movies.

02:37:59.880 --> 02:38:03.040
She's lovely, said Daisy.

02:38:03.040 --> 02:38:05.960
The man bending over her as her director.

02:38:05.960 --> 02:38:09.960
He took them ceremoniously from group to group.

02:38:09.960 --> 02:38:15.880
Mrs. Buchanan and Mr. Buchanan, after an instance hesitation he added the polo player.

02:38:15.880 --> 02:38:19.640
Oh no, objected Tom quickly, not me.

02:38:19.640 --> 02:38:23.560
But evidently the sound of it pleased Gatsby for Tom remained the polo player for the

02:38:23.560 --> 02:38:25.440
rest of the evening.

02:38:25.440 --> 02:38:28.360
I've never met so many celebrities Daisy exclaimed.

02:38:28.360 --> 02:38:32.560
I liked that man, what was his name, with a sort of blue nose.

02:38:32.560 --> 02:38:35.520
Gatsby identified, ematting that he was a small producer.

02:38:35.520 --> 02:38:38.920
Well, I liked him anyhow.

02:38:38.920 --> 02:38:43.360
I'd a little rather not be the polo player, said Tom pleasantly.

02:38:43.360 --> 02:38:48.280
And rather look at all these famous people in oblivion.

02:38:48.280 --> 02:38:50.560
Daisy and Gatsby danced.

02:38:50.560 --> 02:38:53.680
I remember being surprised by his graceful conservative fuckstruck.

02:38:53.680 --> 02:38:55.960
I had never seen him dance before.

02:38:55.960 --> 02:39:00.000
Then they santaed over to my house and sat on the steps for half an hour.

02:39:00.000 --> 02:39:04.760
While at her request I remained watchfully in the garden, in case there is a fire or a

02:39:04.760 --> 02:39:05.760
flood, she explained.

02:39:05.760 --> 02:39:09.320
Tom appeared from his oblivion as we were sitting down to supper together.

02:39:09.320 --> 02:39:11.600
Do you mind if I eat with some people over here?

02:39:11.600 --> 02:39:14.680
He said, if Ella was getting off some funny stuff.

02:39:14.680 --> 02:39:17.480
Go ahead and so Daisy, genially.

02:39:17.480 --> 02:39:21.600
And if you want to take down any addresses, here's my little gold pencil.

02:39:21.600 --> 02:39:25.800
She looked around after a moment and told me the girl was common but pretty.

02:39:25.800 --> 02:39:29.480
And I knew that except for the half hour she'd been alone with Gatsby, she wasn't having

02:39:29.480 --> 02:39:31.440
a good time.

02:39:31.440 --> 02:39:34.320
We were at a particularly tipsy table.

02:39:34.320 --> 02:39:35.320
That was my fault.

02:39:35.320 --> 02:39:40.200
Gatsby had been called to the phone and I'd enjoyed these same people only two weeks before.

02:39:40.200 --> 02:39:45.000
But what had amused me then turned septic on the air now.

02:39:45.000 --> 02:39:47.120
How do you feel, Miss Bedaker?

02:39:47.120 --> 02:39:50.680
The girl addressed was trying unsuccessfully to slump against my shoulder.

02:39:50.680 --> 02:39:53.480
At this inquiry she sat up and opened her eyes.

02:39:53.480 --> 02:39:54.840
What?

02:39:54.840 --> 02:39:59.480
A massive and lethargic woman who had been urging Daisy to play golf with her at the local

02:39:59.480 --> 02:40:02.840
club tomorrow, spoke in Miss Bedaker's defence.

02:40:02.840 --> 02:40:04.920
Oh, she's all right now.

02:40:04.920 --> 02:40:08.600
When she's at five or six cocktails, she always starts screaming like that.

02:40:08.600 --> 02:40:10.960
I tell her she ought to leave it alone.

02:40:10.960 --> 02:40:12.920
I do leave it alone.

02:40:12.920 --> 02:40:14.960
Affirm the accused, hon.

02:40:14.960 --> 02:40:19.520
We heard you yelling, so I said to Doc Sivitt here, there's somebody that needs your help,

02:40:19.520 --> 02:40:20.520
Doc.

02:40:20.520 --> 02:40:21.520
She's much obliged.

02:40:21.520 --> 02:40:24.720
I'm sure, so another friend without gratitude.

02:40:24.720 --> 02:40:28.120
But you got her dress all wet when you stuck her head in the pool.

02:40:28.120 --> 02:40:32.120
Anything I hate is to get my head stuck in a pool, mumble Miss Bedaker.

02:40:32.120 --> 02:40:35.200
They almost drowned me once over in New Jersey.

02:40:35.200 --> 02:40:38.360
Then you ought to leave it alone, counter-doctor Sivitt.

02:40:38.360 --> 02:40:40.840
Speak for yourself, Criden Miss Bedaker, violently.

02:40:40.840 --> 02:40:41.840
Your hands shakes.

02:40:41.840 --> 02:40:44.520
I wouldn't let you operate on me.

02:40:44.520 --> 02:40:46.480
It was like that.

02:40:46.480 --> 02:40:49.880
Almost the last thing I remember was standing with Daisy and watching the moving picture

02:40:49.880 --> 02:40:52.160
director in his star.

02:40:52.160 --> 02:40:56.640
They were still under the white plum tree and her faces were touching, except for a pale,

02:40:56.640 --> 02:40:59.800
thin ray of moonlight between.

02:40:59.800 --> 02:41:04.200
It occurred to me that he had been very slowly bending toward her all evening to attain

02:41:04.200 --> 02:41:05.920
this proximity.

02:41:05.920 --> 02:41:11.400
And even while I watched I saw him stoop one ultimate degree and kiss at her cheek.

02:41:12.120 --> 02:41:15.560
I like her, so Daisy, I think she's lovely.

02:41:15.560 --> 02:41:22.040
But the rest defended her, and inarguably, because it wasn't a gesture but an emotion.

02:41:22.040 --> 02:41:27.960
She was appalled by West Egg, this unprecedented place that Broadway had begotten upon a long

02:41:27.960 --> 02:41:33.640
island fishing village, appalled by its raw vigor that chafed under the old euphemisms

02:41:33.640 --> 02:41:38.160
and by the two obtrusive fate that herded its inhabitants along a shortcut from nothing

02:41:38.160 --> 02:41:39.760
to nothing.

02:41:39.760 --> 02:41:45.320
She saw something awful, in the very simplicity she failed to understand.

02:41:45.320 --> 02:41:48.760
I sat on the front steps with them while they waited for their car.

02:41:48.760 --> 02:41:53.600
It was dark here in front, only the bright door sent ten square feet of light, volume

02:41:53.600 --> 02:41:56.720
out into the soft black morning.

02:41:56.720 --> 02:42:01.240
Sometimes a shadow moved against the dressing room blind above, gave way to another shadow,

02:42:01.240 --> 02:42:07.480
an indefinite procession of shadows that raged and powed with this gatsby anyhow demanded

02:42:07.480 --> 02:42:08.480
Tom suddenly.

02:42:08.480 --> 02:42:09.480
Some big bootlegger.

02:42:09.480 --> 02:42:12.720
Where'd you hear that, I inquired?

02:42:12.720 --> 02:42:14.240
I didn't hear it, I imagined it.

02:42:14.240 --> 02:42:17.880
A lot of these newly rich people are just big bootleggers, you know.

02:42:17.880 --> 02:42:19.960
Not Gatsby, I said shortly.

02:42:19.960 --> 02:42:21.640
He was silent for a moment.

02:42:21.640 --> 02:42:24.360
The pebbles of the drive crunched under his feet.

02:42:24.360 --> 02:42:28.440
Well, he certainly must have strained himself to get this manashery together.

02:42:28.440 --> 02:42:32.480
A breeze stirred the grey haze of Daisy's fur collar.

02:42:32.480 --> 02:42:36.160
At least they're more interesting than the people we know, she said, with an effort.

02:42:36.160 --> 02:42:37.480
You didn't look so interested?

02:42:37.480 --> 02:42:39.480
Well, I was.

02:42:39.480 --> 02:42:41.080
Tom laughed and turned to me.

02:42:41.080 --> 02:42:46.400
Did you notice Daisy's face when that girl answered to put her under a cold shower?

02:42:46.400 --> 02:42:51.200
Daisy began to sing with the music in a husky rhythmic whisper, bringing out a meaning in

02:42:51.200 --> 02:42:55.360
each word that it had never had before and would never have again.

02:42:55.360 --> 02:43:00.160
When the melody rose, her voice broke up sweetly, following it in a way contralto voices

02:43:00.160 --> 02:43:06.800
have, and each change tipped out a little of her warm human magic upon the air.

02:43:06.800 --> 02:43:10.080
Most of people come who haven't been invited, she said suddenly.

02:43:10.080 --> 02:43:11.760
That girl hadn't been invited.

02:43:11.760 --> 02:43:15.520
They simply forced their way in, and he's too polite to object.

02:43:15.520 --> 02:43:19.320
I'd like to know who he is and what he does in sister Tom, and I think I'll make a point

02:43:19.320 --> 02:43:20.640
of finding out.

02:43:20.640 --> 02:43:23.040
I can tell you right now, she answered.

02:43:23.040 --> 02:43:24.040
He owned some drugstore.

02:43:24.040 --> 02:43:25.320
There's a lot of drugstore.

02:43:25.320 --> 02:43:27.640
They built them up himself.

02:43:27.640 --> 02:43:31.160
The dilatory limousine came rolling up the drive.

02:43:31.160 --> 02:43:34.080
Good night, Nick, said Daisy.

02:43:34.080 --> 02:43:39.320
The glance left me, and saw the lighted top of the steps, where three o'clock in the

02:43:39.320 --> 02:43:45.200
morning, a neat, sad little waltz of that year, was drifting out the open door.

02:43:45.200 --> 02:43:50.700
After all, in the very casualness of Gatsby's party, there were romantic possibilities totally

02:43:50.700 --> 02:43:53.320
absent from her world.

02:43:53.320 --> 02:43:58.040
What was it up there in that song that seemed to be calling her back inside?

02:43:58.040 --> 02:44:02.200
What would happen now, in the dim, incalculable hours?

02:44:02.200 --> 02:44:04.800
Some unbelievable guest would arrive.

02:44:04.800 --> 02:44:08.240
A person infinitely rare, and to be marveled at.

02:44:08.240 --> 02:44:13.840
Some authentically radiant young girl, who, with one fresh glance at Gatsby, one moment

02:44:13.840 --> 02:44:20.760
of magical encounter, would blot out those five years of unwavering devotion.

02:44:20.760 --> 02:44:22.160
I stayed late that night.

02:44:22.160 --> 02:44:24.800
Gatsby asked me to wait until he was free.

02:44:24.800 --> 02:44:29.380
And I lingered in the garden until the inevitable swimming party had run up, chilled, and exalted

02:44:29.380 --> 02:44:35.100
from the black beach, until the lights were extinguished in the guest-rooms overhead.

02:44:35.100 --> 02:44:40.360
When he came down the steps at last, the tan-skin was drawn unusually tight on his face, and

02:44:40.360 --> 02:44:42.620
his eyes were bright and tired.

02:44:42.620 --> 02:44:44.700
She didn't like it, he said immediately.

02:44:44.700 --> 02:44:45.700
Of course she did.

02:44:45.700 --> 02:44:47.260
