02:59:18.340 --> 02:59:20.140
He went inside.

02:59:20.140 --> 02:59:22.380
Gatsby turned to me, rigidly.

02:59:22.380 --> 02:59:25.620
I can't say anything in his household sport.

02:59:25.620 --> 02:59:28.620
She's got an indescreet voice, I remarked.

02:59:28.620 --> 02:59:31.500
It's full of, I hesitated.

02:59:31.500 --> 02:59:35.100
Her voice is full of money, he said suddenly.

02:59:35.100 --> 02:59:36.420
That was it.

02:59:36.420 --> 02:59:38.340
I never understood before.

02:59:38.340 --> 02:59:40.460
It was full of money.

02:59:40.460 --> 02:59:45.660
That was the inexhaustible charm that rose and fell in it to jingle of it, the symbol's

02:59:45.660 --> 02:59:46.660
song of it.

02:59:46.660 --> 02:59:52.300
High in a white palace, the king's daughter, the golden girl.

02:59:52.300 --> 02:59:57.220
Tom came out of the house, wrapping a court-bottle in a towel, followed by Daisy and Jordan, wearing

02:59:57.220 --> 03:00:02.820
small, tight hats of metallic cloth and carrying light capes over their arms.

03:00:02.820 --> 03:00:04.180
Shall we all go in my cars?

03:00:04.180 --> 03:00:05.420
It just had Gatsby.

03:00:05.420 --> 03:00:07.580
He felt the hot green leather of the seat.

03:00:07.580 --> 03:00:09.820
I ought to have left it in the shade.

03:00:09.820 --> 03:00:12.220
Is it standard shift, demanded Tom?

03:00:12.220 --> 03:00:13.220
Yes.

03:00:13.220 --> 03:00:16.460
Well, you take my coupé, let me drive your car to town.

03:00:16.460 --> 03:00:19.220
The suggestion was distasteful to Gatsby.

03:00:19.220 --> 03:00:21.500
I don't think there's much gas, he objected.

03:00:21.500 --> 03:00:25.860
Plenty of gas at Tom, boisterously, he looked at the gauge, and if it runs on, I can stop

03:00:25.860 --> 03:00:26.860
at a drugstore.

03:00:26.860 --> 03:00:29.420
You can buy anything at a drugstore nowadays.

03:00:29.420 --> 03:00:33.780
A pause followed the apparently pointless remark.

03:00:33.780 --> 03:00:39.900
Daisy looked at Tom frowning, and an indefinable expression at once definitely unfamiliar and

03:00:39.900 --> 03:00:44.900
vaguely recognizable as if I had only heard it described in words past over Gatsby's

03:00:44.900 --> 03:00:45.900
face.

03:00:45.900 --> 03:00:50.820
Come on, Daisy, said Tom, pressing her with his hand towards Gatsby's car.

03:00:50.820 --> 03:00:53.740
I'll take you in the circus wagon.

03:00:53.740 --> 03:00:56.820
They opened the door, but she moved out from the circle of his arm.

03:00:56.820 --> 03:01:00.380
You take Nick and Jordan, we'll follow you in the coupé.

03:01:00.380 --> 03:01:04.180
She walked close to Gatsby, touching his coat with her hand.

03:01:04.180 --> 03:01:08.100
Jordan and Tom and I got into the front seat of Gatsby's car.

03:01:08.100 --> 03:01:12.820
Some pushed the unfamiliar gears tentatively, and we shot off into the oppressive heat,

03:01:12.820 --> 03:01:15.380
leaving them out of sight behind.

03:01:15.380 --> 03:01:18.340
Did you see that, demanded Tom?

03:01:18.340 --> 03:01:19.660
See what?

03:01:19.660 --> 03:01:25.300
He looked at me keenly, realizing that Jordan and I must have known all along.

03:01:25.300 --> 03:01:28.820
You think I'm pretty dumb, don't you, me suggested.

03:01:28.820 --> 03:01:34.300
Maybe I am, but I have almost a second sight sometimes that tells me what to do.

03:01:34.300 --> 03:01:37.700
Maybe you don't believe that, but science, he paused.

03:01:37.700 --> 03:01:42.860
The immediate contingency overtook him, pulled him back from the edge of the theoretical abyss.

03:01:42.860 --> 03:01:48.020
I've made a small investigation of this fellow, he continued, I could have gone deeper if

03:01:48.020 --> 03:01:52.660
I had known, do you mean you've been to a medium, and quiet, Jordan, humorously?

03:01:52.660 --> 03:01:53.660
What?

03:01:53.660 --> 03:01:56.140
Confused he stared at us as we laughed.

03:01:56.140 --> 03:01:57.300
A medium?

03:01:57.300 --> 03:01:58.500
About Gatsby.

03:01:58.500 --> 03:01:59.500
About Gatsby?

03:01:59.500 --> 03:02:00.500
No, I haven't.

03:02:00.500 --> 03:02:02.860
I said it, been making a small investigation of his past.

03:02:02.860 --> 03:02:06.500
And you found he was an Oxford man, said Jordan, helpfully.

03:02:06.500 --> 03:02:09.260
An Oxford man, he was incredulous.

03:02:09.260 --> 03:02:11.900
Like hell he is, he wears a pink suit.

03:02:11.900 --> 03:02:14.220
Nevertheless he's an Oxford man.

03:02:14.220 --> 03:02:16.620
Oxford knew Mexico snorted Tom contend to a toast.

03:02:16.620 --> 03:02:17.620
How such a snob.

03:02:17.620 --> 03:02:21.660
Why did you invite him to lunch, demanded Jordan crossly.

03:02:21.660 --> 03:02:27.140
Daisy invited him, she knew him before we were married, God knows where.

03:02:27.140 --> 03:02:33.660
We were all irritable now with a fading ale, and aware of it as we drove for a violent silence.

03:02:33.660 --> 03:02:38.020
In those Dr. T. J. Eckelberg's faded eyes came into sight down the road, I remembered

03:02:38.020 --> 03:02:40.020
Gatsby's caution about gasoline.

03:02:40.020 --> 03:02:42.980
We've got enough to get us to town, said Tom.

03:02:42.980 --> 03:02:45.900
But there's a garage right here, objected Jordan.

03:02:45.900 --> 03:02:49.620
I don't want to get stalled in this baking heat.

03:02:49.620 --> 03:02:55.500
Tom threw on both breaks impatiently, and we slid to an abrupt dusty stop under Wilson's

03:02:55.500 --> 03:02:56.900
sign.

03:02:56.900 --> 03:03:01.340
After a moment the proprietor emerged from the interior of his establishment and gazed

03:03:01.340 --> 03:03:03.220
hollow-eyed at the car.

03:03:03.220 --> 03:03:05.660
Let's have some gas, cried Tom roughly.

03:03:05.660 --> 03:03:08.500
What do you think we stopped for it, would my other view?

03:03:08.500 --> 03:03:12.060
I'm sick, said Wilson, without moving.

03:03:12.060 --> 03:03:14.300
In sick all day.

03:03:14.300 --> 03:03:15.300
What's the matter?

03:03:15.300 --> 03:03:17.140
I'm all run down.

03:03:17.140 --> 03:03:19.300
Well, shall I help myself, Tom demanded.

03:03:19.300 --> 03:03:21.860
You sounded well enough on the phone.

03:03:21.860 --> 03:03:26.860
With an effort Wilson left the shade in support of the doorway, and breathing hard, unscrewed

03:03:26.860 --> 03:03:31.780
the cap of the tank, and the sunlight his face was green.

03:03:31.780 --> 03:03:34.340
I didn't mean to interrupt your lunch, he said.

03:03:34.340 --> 03:03:39.580
But I need money pretty bad, and I was wondering what you were going to do with your old car.

03:03:39.580 --> 03:03:41.140
How do you like this one, and quiet, Tom?

03:03:41.140 --> 03:03:42.780
I bought it last week.

03:03:42.780 --> 03:03:47.500
It's a nice yellow one, said Wilson, as he strained at the handle.

03:03:47.500 --> 03:03:49.180
Like to buy it?

03:03:49.180 --> 03:03:51.780
Big chance, Wilson smiled faintly.

03:03:51.780 --> 03:03:54.940
No, but I could make some money on the other.

03:03:54.940 --> 03:03:56.940
What do you want money for all of a sudden?

03:03:56.940 --> 03:04:00.360
I've been here too long, I want to get away.

03:04:00.360 --> 03:04:03.020
My wife and I want to go west.

03:04:03.020 --> 03:04:06.580
Your wife does, exclaimed Tom startled.

03:04:06.580 --> 03:04:09.300
She's been talking about it for ten years.

03:04:09.300 --> 03:04:12.900
He rested for a moment against the pump, shading his eyes.

03:04:12.900 --> 03:04:17.260
And now she's going whether she wants to or not, I'm going to get her away.

03:04:17.260 --> 03:04:22.420
The coupé flashed by us with a flurry of dust and the flash of a waving hand.

03:04:22.420 --> 03:04:25.260
What do I owe you, demanded Tom harshly?

03:04:25.260 --> 03:04:30.140
I just got wised up to something funny the last two days, remarked Wilson.

03:04:30.140 --> 03:04:31.860
That's why I want to get away.

03:04:31.860 --> 03:04:34.580
That's why I've been bothering you about the car.

03:04:34.580 --> 03:04:37.900
What do I owe you, dollar twenty?

03:04:37.900 --> 03:04:43.260
The relentless beating heat was beginning to confuse me, and I had a bad moment there

03:04:43.260 --> 03:04:48.060
before I realized that so far his suspicions hadn't elated on Tom.

03:04:48.060 --> 03:04:53.660
He had discovered that Mertel had some sort of life apart from him in another world,

03:04:53.660 --> 03:04:56.660
and the shock had made him physically sick.

03:04:56.660 --> 03:05:00.980
I stared at him and then at Tom, who had made a parallel discovery less than an hour

03:05:00.980 --> 03:05:06.300
before, and it occurred to me that there was no difference between men in intelligence

03:05:06.300 --> 03:05:12.540
or race so profound as the difference between the sick and the well.

03:05:12.540 --> 03:05:17.620
Wilson was so sick that he looked guilty, unforgivably guilty, as if he had just got some

03:05:17.620 --> 03:05:19.660
poor girl with child.

03:05:19.660 --> 03:05:21.260
I'll let you have that car, said Tom.

03:05:21.260 --> 03:05:24.220
I'll send it over tomorrow afternoon.

03:05:24.220 --> 03:05:29.780
That locality was always vaguely disacquieting, even in the broad glare of afternoon, and now

03:05:29.780 --> 03:05:33.660
I turned my head as though I had been warned of something behind.

03:05:33.660 --> 03:05:38.820
Over the ash-heaps, the giant eyes of Dr. T. J. Eckelberg kept their vigil, but I perceived

03:05:38.820 --> 03:05:43.740
after a moment that other eyes were regarding us with peculiar intensity from less than

03:05:43.740 --> 03:05:45.100
twenty feet away.

03:05:45.100 --> 03:05:49.220
In one of the windows over the garage, the curtains had been moved aside a little, and

03:05:49.220 --> 03:05:52.740
Mertel Wilson was peering down at the car.

03:05:52.740 --> 03:05:58.220
So engrossed was she that she had no consciousness of being observed, and one emotion after another

03:05:58.220 --> 03:06:03.260
crept into her face like objects into a slowly developing picture.

03:06:03.260 --> 03:06:05.580
Her expression was curiously familiar.

03:06:05.580 --> 03:06:10.500
It was an expression I had often seen on women's faces, but on Mertel Wilson's face it seemed

03:06:10.500 --> 03:06:17.220
to purposeless and inexplicable, until I realized that her eyes, wide with jealous terror,

03:06:17.220 --> 03:06:24.700
were fixed not on Tom, but on Jordan Baker, whom she took to be his wife.

03:06:24.700 --> 03:06:30.140
There is no confusion like the confusion of a simple mind, and as we drove away, Tom

03:06:30.140 --> 03:06:33.100
was feeling the hot whips of panic.

03:06:33.100 --> 03:06:38.380
His wife and his mistress, until an hour ago secure and in violet, were slipping precipitately

03:06:38.380 --> 03:06:40.380
from his control.

03:06:40.380 --> 03:06:44.660
Mertel made him step on the accelerator with the double purpose of overtaking Daisy and

03:06:44.660 --> 03:06:50.340
leaving Wilson behind, and we sped along towards a story at fifty miles an hour, until

03:06:50.340 --> 03:06:55.540
among the spider-y girders of the elevated, we came in sight of the easy-going blue

03:06:55.540 --> 03:06:56.540
coupé.

03:06:56.540 --> 03:07:01.820
Those big movies, around fiftyth street are cool, suggested Jordan.

03:07:01.820 --> 03:07:04.660
I love New York on summer afternoons when everyone's away.

03:07:04.660 --> 03:07:07.260
There's something very sensuous about it.

03:07:07.260 --> 03:07:13.060
We're ripe, as if all sorts of funny fruits were going to fall into your hands.

03:07:13.060 --> 03:07:17.940
The word sensuous had the effect of further disquieting Tom, but before he could invent a

03:07:17.940 --> 03:07:23.140
protest, the coupé came to a stop and Daisy signaled us to draw up alongside.

03:07:23.140 --> 03:07:26.020
Where are we going, she cried?

03:07:26.020 --> 03:07:27.020
About the movies.

03:07:27.020 --> 03:07:29.100
It's so hot she complained.

03:07:29.100 --> 03:07:34.420
You go, we'll ride around and meet you after, with an effort her wit rose faintly.

03:07:34.420 --> 03:07:39.180
To meet you on some corner I'll be the man smoking two cigarettes.

03:07:39.180 --> 03:07:43.900
We can't argue about it here, Tom said impatiently as a truck gave out a cursing whistle behind

03:07:43.900 --> 03:07:44.900
us.

03:07:44.900 --> 03:07:48.860
You follow me to the south side of Central Park in front of the plaza.

03:07:48.860 --> 03:07:52.980
Several times he turned his head and looked back for their car, and if the traffic delayed

03:07:52.980 --> 03:07:55.220
them he slowed up until they came into sight.

03:07:55.220 --> 03:08:00.540
I think he was afraid they would dart down a side street and out of his life forever,

03:08:00.540 --> 03:08:01.860
but they didn't.

03:08:01.860 --> 03:08:06.540
And we all took the less explicable step of engaging the parlor of a suite in the plaza

03:08:06.540 --> 03:08:08.380
hotel.

03:08:08.380 --> 03:08:13.660
The prolonged and tumultuous argument that ended by hurting us into that room eludes

03:08:13.660 --> 03:08:18.140
me, though I have a sharp physical memory that in the course of it my underwear kept climbing

03:08:18.140 --> 03:08:25.140
like a damp snake around my legs, an intermittent beads of sweat raced cool across my back.

03:08:25.140 --> 03:08:29.700
The notion originated with Daisy's suggestion that we hire five bathrooms and take cold

03:08:29.700 --> 03:08:35.380
baths, and then assumed a more tangible form as a place to have a mint julap.

03:08:35.380 --> 03:08:38.860
Each of us said over and over that it was a crazy idea.

03:08:38.860 --> 03:08:43.580
We all talked at once to a baffled clerk and thought or pretended that the room was large

03:08:43.580 --> 03:08:47.860
and stifling, and though it was already four o'clock opening the windows admitted only

03:08:47.860 --> 03:08:50.780
a gust of hot shrubbery from the park.

03:08:50.780 --> 03:08:54.980
Daisy went to the mirror and stood with her back to us fixing her hair.

03:08:54.980 --> 03:08:59.300
It's a swell suite whispered Jordan respectfully, and everyone laughed.

03:08:59.300 --> 03:09:02.380
In another window commanded Daisy without turning around.

03:09:02.380 --> 03:09:04.020
There aren't any more.

03:09:04.020 --> 03:09:06.740
Well, we'd better telephone for an axe.

03:09:06.740 --> 03:09:09.860
The thing to do is to forget about the heat said Tom impatiently.

03:09:09.860 --> 03:09:12.380
You make it ten times worse by cramming about it.

03:09:12.380 --> 03:09:16.060
He unrolled the bottle of whiskey from the towel and put it on the table.

03:09:16.060 --> 03:09:21.380
Well, not let her alone old sport remarked Gatsby, you're the one that wanted to come to town.

03:09:21.380 --> 03:09:23.300
There was a moment of silence.

03:09:23.300 --> 03:09:28.220
A telephone book slipped from its nail and splashed to the floor, whereupon Jordan whispered,

03:09:28.220 --> 03:09:31.460
excuse me, but this time no one laughed.

03:09:31.460 --> 03:09:33.180
I'll pick it up, I offered.

03:09:33.180 --> 03:09:34.180
I've got it.

03:09:34.180 --> 03:09:39.500
Gatsby examined the parted string, muttered, home, in an interested way, and tossed the book

03:09:39.500 --> 03:09:40.500
on a chair.

03:09:40.500 --> 03:09:44.980
That's a great expression of yours, isn't it, said Tom sharply.

03:09:44.980 --> 03:09:45.980
What is?

03:09:45.980 --> 03:09:49.140
All this old sport business where you picked that up.

03:09:49.140 --> 03:09:53.340
Now see here, Tom, said Daisy turning around from the mirror.

03:09:53.340 --> 03:09:56.220
If you're going to make personal remarks, I won't stay here a minute.

03:09:56.220 --> 03:09:59.140
Call up and order some ice for the mint julep.

03:09:59.140 --> 03:10:04.020
As Tom took up the receiver, the compressed heat exploded into sound, and we were listening

03:10:04.020 --> 03:10:09.140
to the portentous cords of Mendelssohn's wedding march from the ballroom below.

03:10:09.140 --> 03:10:13.540
Imagine marrying anybody in this heat, cried Jordan dismaly.

03:10:13.540 --> 03:10:18.220
Still, I was married in the middle of June, Daisy remembered.

03:10:18.220 --> 03:10:20.340
Louisville and June, somebody fainted.

03:10:20.340 --> 03:10:22.380
Who was it fainted, Tom?

03:10:22.380 --> 03:10:24.460
Baloxie, he answered shortly.

03:10:24.460 --> 03:10:26.460
A man named Baloxie.

03:10:26.460 --> 03:10:28.060
Blocks, Baloxie.

03:10:28.060 --> 03:10:29.140
And he made boxes.

03:10:29.140 --> 03:10:30.140
That's a fact.

03:10:30.140 --> 03:10:32.300
And he was from Baloxie, Tennessee.

03:10:32.300 --> 03:10:35.900
They carried him into my house, Appendid, Jordan, because we lived just two doors from the

03:10:35.900 --> 03:10:36.900
church.

03:10:36.900 --> 03:10:40.740
And he stayed three weeks until Daddy told him he had to get out.

03:10:40.740 --> 03:10:43.380
The day after he left, Daddy died.

03:10:43.380 --> 03:10:46.220
After a moment, she added, as if she might have sounded irreverent.

03:10:46.220 --> 03:10:48.260
There wasn't any connection.

03:10:48.260 --> 03:10:51.660
I used to know a bill, Baloxie, from Memphis, I remarked.

03:10:51.660 --> 03:10:52.660
That was his cousin.

03:10:52.660 --> 03:10:54.460
I knew his whole family history before he left.

03:10:54.460 --> 03:10:57.940
He gave me an aluminum putter that I used today.

03:10:57.940 --> 03:11:01.260
The music had died down as the ceremony began.

03:11:01.260 --> 03:11:06.660
And now a long cheer floated in at the window, followed by intermittent cries of, yeah,

03:11:06.660 --> 03:11:11.100
yeah, yeah, and finally by a burst of jazz as the dancing began.

03:11:11.100 --> 03:11:13.500
We're getting old, said Daisy.

03:11:13.500 --> 03:11:16.460
If we were young, we'd rise and dance.

03:11:16.460 --> 03:11:19.340
Remember Baloxie, Jordan Wanderer?

03:11:19.340 --> 03:11:21.340
Where'd you know him, Tom?

03:11:21.340 --> 03:11:23.580
Baloxie, he concentrated with an effort.

03:11:23.580 --> 03:11:24.580
I didn't know him.

03:11:24.580 --> 03:11:25.700
He was a friend of Daisy's.

03:11:25.700 --> 03:11:26.700
He was not.

03:11:26.700 --> 03:11:27.700
She denied.

03:11:27.700 --> 03:11:28.700
I'd never seen him before.

03:11:28.700 --> 03:11:30.460
He came down in the private car.

03:11:30.460 --> 03:11:31.740
Well, he said he knew you.

03:11:31.740 --> 03:11:33.460
He said he was raised in Louisville.

03:11:33.460 --> 03:11:37.540
Aissa Bird brought him around at the last minute and asked if we had room for him.

03:11:37.540 --> 03:11:39.180
Jordan smiled.

03:11:39.180 --> 03:11:40.940
He was probably bumming his way home.

03:11:40.940 --> 03:11:44.340
He told me he was president of your class at Yale.

03:11:44.340 --> 03:11:46.820
Tom and I looked at each other blankly.

03:11:46.820 --> 03:11:48.420
Baloxie?

03:11:48.420 --> 03:11:50.900
First place we didn't have any president.

03:11:50.900 --> 03:11:54.620
It's Bees' foot beat a short restless tattoo.

03:11:54.620 --> 03:11:56.340
And Tom eyed him, suddenly.

03:11:56.340 --> 03:11:59.660
By the way, Mr. Gatsby, I understand you're an Oxford man.

03:11:59.660 --> 03:12:00.660
Not exactly.

03:12:00.660 --> 03:12:03.060
Oh, yes, I understand you went to Oxford.

03:12:03.060 --> 03:12:05.580
Yes, I went there.

03:12:05.580 --> 03:12:06.580
A pause.

03:12:06.580 --> 03:12:09.380
Then Tom's voice incredulous and insulting.

03:12:09.380 --> 03:12:13.420
You must have gone there about the time Baloxie went to New Haven.

03:12:13.420 --> 03:12:14.660
Another pause.

03:12:14.660 --> 03:12:18.340
A waiter knocked and came in with crushed mint and ice.

03:12:18.340 --> 03:12:23.340
The silence was unbroken by his thank you and the soft closing of the door.

03:12:23.340 --> 03:12:27.260
This tremendous detail was to be cleared up at last.

03:12:27.260 --> 03:12:29.900
I told you I went there, said Gatsby.

03:12:29.900 --> 03:12:32.460
I heard you, but I'd like to know when.

03:12:32.460 --> 03:12:33.980
It was in 1919.

03:12:33.980 --> 03:12:35.900
I only stayed five months.

03:12:35.900 --> 03:12:40.100
That's why I can't really calm myself down to see if we mirrored his unbelief, but we

03:12:40.100 --> 03:12:42.300
were all looking at Gatsby.

03:12:42.300 --> 03:12:46.020
It was an opportunity they gave to some of the officers after the armistice.

03:12:46.020 --> 03:12:47.100
He continued.

03:12:47.100 --> 03:12:51.140
We could go to any of the universities in England or France.

03:12:51.140 --> 03:12:54.300
I wanted to get up and slap him on the back.

03:12:54.300 --> 03:12:59.380
I had one of those renewals of complete faith in him that I'd experienced before.

03:12:59.380 --> 03:13:03.300
Daisy rose, smiling faintly and went to the table.

03:13:03.300 --> 03:13:07.180
Open the whiskey, Tom, she ordered, and I'll make you a mint julep.

03:13:07.180 --> 03:13:09.780
Then you won't seem so stupid to yourself.

03:13:09.780 --> 03:13:11.260
Look at the mint.

03:13:11.260 --> 03:13:12.900
Wait a minute, snap, Tom.

03:13:12.900 --> 03:13:15.580
I want to ask Mr. Gatsby one more question.

03:13:15.580 --> 03:13:18.060
Go on, Gatsby said politely.

03:13:18.060 --> 03:13:22.180
What kind of a rau are you trying to cause in my house anyway?

03:13:22.180 --> 03:13:26.220
They were out in the open at last, and Gatsby was content.

03:13:26.220 --> 03:13:28.260
He isn't causing a rau.

03:13:28.260 --> 03:13:30.340
Daisy looked desperately from one to the other.

03:13:30.340 --> 03:13:31.900
You're causing a rau.

03:13:31.900 --> 03:13:34.060
Please have a little self-control.

03:13:34.060 --> 03:13:36.420
Self-control repeated, Tom, incredulously.

03:13:36.420 --> 03:13:41.220
I suppose the latest thing is to sit back and let Mr. Nobody from nowhere make love to

03:13:41.220 --> 03:13:42.220
your wife.

03:13:42.220 --> 03:13:44.500
Well, if that's the idea, you can count me out.

03:13:44.500 --> 03:13:49.340
Nowadays people begin by sneering at family life and family institutions, and next they'll

03:13:49.340 --> 03:13:53.220
throw everything overboard and have intermarriage between black and white.

03:13:53.220 --> 03:13:58.580
Flushed with his impassioned gibberish, he saw himself standing alone on the last barrier

03:13:58.580 --> 03:13:59.780
of civilization.

03:13:59.780 --> 03:14:02.700
We're all white here, Merma Jordan.

03:14:02.700 --> 03:14:04.420
I know I'm not very popular.

03:14:04.420 --> 03:14:05.900
I don't give big parties.

03:14:05.900 --> 03:14:09.780
I suppose you've got to make your house into a pigstie in order to have any friends in

03:14:09.780 --> 03:14:12.340
the modern world.

03:14:12.340 --> 03:14:16.940
Every as I was, as we all were, I was tempted to laugh whenever he opened his mouth.

03:14:16.940 --> 03:14:20.220
The transition from liberty into prig was so complete.

03:14:20.220 --> 03:14:26.100
I've got something to tell you, old sport, began Gatsby, but Daisy guessed at his intention.

03:14:26.100 --> 03:14:28.100
Please don't, she interrupted helplessly.

03:14:28.100 --> 03:14:29.260
Please, let's all go home.

03:14:29.260 --> 03:14:31.260
Why don't we all go home?

03:14:31.260 --> 03:14:32.260
That's a good idea.

03:14:32.260 --> 03:14:33.260
I got up.

03:14:33.260 --> 03:14:34.260
Come on, Tom.

03:14:34.260 --> 03:14:35.260
Nobody wants a drink.

03:14:35.260 --> 03:14:37.140
I want to know what Mr. Gatsby has to tell me.

03:14:37.140 --> 03:14:39.860
Your wife doesn't love you, said Gatsby.

03:14:39.860 --> 03:14:41.140
She's never loved you.

03:14:41.140 --> 03:14:42.960
She loves me.

03:14:42.960 --> 03:14:47.220
You must be crazy, exclaimed Tom automatically.

03:14:47.220 --> 03:14:50.060
Gatsby sprang to his feet, vivid with excitement.

03:14:50.060 --> 03:14:51.580
She never loved you, do you hear?

03:14:51.580 --> 03:14:52.580
He cried.

03:14:52.580 --> 03:14:55.740
She only married you because I was poor and she was tired of waiting for me.

03:14:55.740 --> 03:15:01.020
It was a terrible mistake, but in her heart she never loved anyone except me.

03:15:01.020 --> 03:15:06.780
At this point Jordan and I tried to go, but Tom and Gatsby insisted with competitive firmness

03:15:06.780 --> 03:15:08.340
that we remain.

03:15:08.340 --> 03:15:12.100
As though neither of them had anything to conceal and it would be a privilege to partake

03:15:12.100 --> 03:15:14.700
vicariously of their emotions.

03:15:14.700 --> 03:15:16.220
Sit down, Daisy.

03:15:16.220 --> 03:15:19.260
Tom's voice groped unsuccessfully for the paternal note.

03:15:19.260 --> 03:15:22.660
What's been going on, I want to hear all about it.

03:15:22.660 --> 03:15:25.220
I told you what's been going on, said Gatsby.

03:15:25.220 --> 03:15:29.740
Going on for five years and you didn't know, Tom turned to Daisy sharply.

03:15:29.740 --> 03:15:32.340
You've been seeing this fellow for five years.

03:15:32.340 --> 03:15:34.020
Not seeing, said Gatsby.

03:15:34.020 --> 03:15:35.700
No, we couldn't meet.

03:15:35.700 --> 03:15:39.860
And both of us loved each other all that time old sport and you didn't know.

03:15:39.860 --> 03:15:44.900
I used to laugh sometimes, but there was no laughter in his eyes, to think that you didn't

03:15:44.900 --> 03:15:45.900
know.

03:15:45.900 --> 03:15:48.300
Oh, that's all!

03:15:48.300 --> 03:15:52.700
Tom tapped his thick fingers together like a clergyman and leaned back in his chair.

03:15:52.700 --> 03:15:54.820
You're crazy, he exploded.

03:15:54.820 --> 03:15:58.460
I can't speak about what happened five years ago because I didn't know Daisy then,

03:15:58.460 --> 03:16:02.340
and I'll be damned if I see how you got within a mile of her unless you brought the groceries

03:16:02.340 --> 03:16:03.940
to the back door.

03:16:03.940 --> 03:16:06.140
But all the rest of that's a god damned lie.

03:16:06.140 --> 03:16:09.220
Daisy loved me when she married me and she loves me and she loves me.

03:16:09.220 --> 03:16:10.420
She does, though.

03:16:10.420 --> 03:16:13.740
The trouble is that sometimes she gets foolish ideas in her head and doesn't know what

03:16:13.740 --> 03:16:14.740
she's doing.

03:16:14.740 --> 03:16:16.540
He nodded, sagely.

03:16:16.540 --> 03:16:18.980
And once more, I love Daisy, too.

03:16:18.980 --> 03:16:23.260
Once in a while I go off on a spree and make a fool of myself, but I always come back,

03:16:23.260 --> 03:16:25.340
and in my heart I love her all the time.

03:16:25.340 --> 03:16:28.300
You're revolting, said Daisy.

03:16:28.300 --> 03:16:31.980
She turned to me and her voice dropping an octave lower filled the room with thrilling

03:16:31.980 --> 03:16:32.980
scorn.

03:16:32.980 --> 03:16:35.700
Do you know why we left Chicago?

03:16:35.700 --> 03:16:40.860
I'm surprised that they didn't treat you to the story of that little spree.

03:16:40.860 --> 03:16:43.340
Gatsby walked over and stood beside her.

03:16:43.340 --> 03:16:45.980
Daisy, that's all over now, he said, earnestly.

03:16:45.980 --> 03:16:47.100
It doesn't matter anymore.

03:16:47.100 --> 03:16:52.300
Just tell him the truth that you never loved him, and it's all wiped out forever.

03:16:52.300 --> 03:16:54.020
She looked at him blindly.

03:16:54.020 --> 03:16:55.020
Why?

03:16:55.020 --> 03:16:57.180
How could I love him?

03:16:57.180 --> 03:16:58.180
Possibly.

03:16:58.180 --> 03:16:59.900
You never loved him.

03:16:59.900 --> 03:17:01.420
She hesitated.

03:17:01.420 --> 03:17:06.260
Her eyes fell on Jordan and me with a sort of appeal, as though she realized it last what

03:17:06.260 --> 03:17:11.940
she was doing, and as though she had never all along intended doing anything at all.

03:17:11.940 --> 03:17:13.620
But it was done now.

03:17:13.620 --> 03:17:15.620
It was too late.

03:17:15.620 --> 03:17:19.660
I never loved him, she said, with perceptible reluctance.

03:17:19.660 --> 03:17:22.700
Not at Capulani, demanded Tom suddenly.

03:17:22.700 --> 03:17:24.260
No.

03:17:24.260 --> 03:17:29.860
From the ballroom beneath muffled and suffocating cords were drifting up on hot waves of air.

03:17:29.860 --> 03:17:33.540
With that day I carried you down from the punch-bowl to keep your shoes dry.

03:17:33.540 --> 03:17:36.500
There was a husky tenderness in his tone.

03:17:36.500 --> 03:17:37.500
Daisy?

03:17:37.500 --> 03:17:39.660
Please don't.

03:17:39.660 --> 03:17:42.420
Her voice was cold, but the ranker was gone from it.

03:17:42.420 --> 03:17:43.620
She looked at Gatsby.

03:17:43.620 --> 03:17:45.700
There, Jay, she said.

03:17:45.700 --> 03:17:48.660
But her hand as she tried to light her cigarette was trembling.

03:17:48.660 --> 03:17:51.780
Suddenly she threw the cigarette and the burning match on the carpet.

03:17:51.780 --> 03:17:54.660
Oh, you want too much, she cried to Gatsby.

03:17:54.660 --> 03:17:56.180
I love you now, isn't that enough?

03:17:56.180 --> 03:17:57.820
I can't help but past.

03:17:57.820 --> 03:17:59.260
She began to sob helplessly.

03:17:59.260 --> 03:18:03.020
I did love him once, but I loved you too.

03:18:03.020 --> 03:18:05.340
Gatsby's eyes opened and closed.

03:18:05.340 --> 03:18:08.220
You loved me too, he repeated.

03:18:08.220 --> 03:18:10.940
Even that's a lie, said Tom savagely.

03:18:10.940 --> 03:18:13.100
She didn't know you were alive.

03:18:13.100 --> 03:18:16.500
Why there are things between Daisy and me that you'll never know, things that neither of

03:18:16.500 --> 03:18:18.420
us can ever forget.

03:18:18.420 --> 03:18:21.820
The words seem to bite physically into Gatsby.

03:18:21.820 --> 03:18:24.020
I want to speak to Daisy alone, he insisted.

03:18:24.020 --> 03:18:26.140
She's all excited now.

03:18:26.140 --> 03:18:28.540
Even alone I can't say I never loved Tom.

03:18:28.540 --> 03:18:30.500
She admitted in a pitiful voice.

03:18:30.500 --> 03:18:31.820
It wouldn't be true.

03:18:31.820 --> 03:18:34.300
Of course it wouldn't agree, Tom.

03:18:34.300 --> 03:18:36.380
She turned to her husband.

03:18:36.380 --> 03:18:38.420
As if it mattered to you, she said.

03:18:38.420 --> 03:18:39.420
Of course it matters.

03:18:39.420 --> 03:18:41.940
I'm going to take better care of you from now on.

03:18:41.940 --> 03:18:44.780
You don't understand, said Gatsby, with a touch of panic.

03:18:44.780 --> 03:18:47.060
You're not going to take care of her anymore.

03:18:47.060 --> 03:18:48.420
I'm not.

03:18:48.420 --> 03:18:50.700
Tom opened his eyes wide and laughed.

03:18:50.700 --> 03:18:52.780
He could afford to control himself now.

03:18:52.780 --> 03:18:53.780
Why is that?

03:18:53.780 --> 03:18:57.020
Daisy is leaving, she said, with visible effort.

03:18:57.020 --> 03:19:01.140
He's not leaving me, Tom's words suddenly leaned down over Gatsby.

03:19:01.140 --> 03:19:05.340
Certainly not for a common swindler who'd have to steal the ring he put on her finger.

03:19:05.340 --> 03:19:07.460
I won't stand this cry, Daisy.

03:19:07.460 --> 03:19:09.660
Oh, please, let's get out.

03:19:09.660 --> 03:19:10.660
Who are you, anyhow?

03:19:10.660 --> 03:19:12.060
I'll broke out, Tom.

03:19:12.060 --> 03:19:14.740
You want that bunch that hangs around with Maya Wolfshime?

03:19:14.740 --> 03:19:15.740
That much I happen to know.

03:19:15.740 --> 03:19:20.020
I've made little investigation into your affairs and I'll carry it further tomorrow.

03:19:20.020 --> 03:19:23.420
You can suit yourself about that old sports at Gatsby steadily.

03:19:23.420 --> 03:19:26.300
I found out what your drug store is where.

03:19:26.300 --> 03:19:28.500
He turned to us and spoke rapidly.

03:19:28.500 --> 03:19:32.940
He in this wolfshime bought up a lot of side-street drug stores here in in Chicago and sold

03:19:32.940 --> 03:19:35.060
grain alcohol over the counter.

03:19:35.060 --> 03:19:36.500
That's one of his little stunts.

03:19:36.500 --> 03:19:40.580
I picked him for a bootleg of the first time I saw him and I wasn't far wrong.

03:19:40.580 --> 03:19:42.780
What about it, said Gatsby politely.

03:19:42.780 --> 03:19:46.420
I guess your friend Walter Chase wasn't too proud to come in on it.

03:19:46.420 --> 03:19:48.300
And you left him in the lurch, didn't you?

03:19:48.300 --> 03:19:51.220
You let him go to jail for a month over in New Jersey.

03:19:51.220 --> 03:19:54.260
God, you ought to hear Walter on the subject of you.

03:19:54.260 --> 03:19:55.940
He came to us dead broke.

03:19:55.940 --> 03:19:58.780
He was very glad to pick up some money old sport.

03:19:58.780 --> 03:20:01.020
Don't you call me old sport?

03:20:01.020 --> 03:20:02.020
Tried Tom.

03:20:02.020 --> 03:20:03.540
Gatsby said nothing.

03:20:03.540 --> 03:20:07.540
Walter could have you up on the bedding, Lars, too, but Wolfshime scared him into shutting

03:20:07.540 --> 03:20:08.860
his mouth.

03:20:08.860 --> 03:20:13.180
That unfamiliar yet recognizable look was back again in Gatsby's face.

03:20:13.180 --> 03:20:17.460
That drug store business was just small change continued Tom slowly.

03:20:17.460 --> 03:20:21.940
But you've got something on now that Walter's afraid to tell me about.

03:20:21.940 --> 03:20:26.540
I glanced at Daisy who was staring terrified between Gatsby and her husband, and at Jordan

03:20:26.540 --> 03:20:30.860
who had begun to balance an invisible but absorbing object on the tip of her chin.

03:20:30.860 --> 03:20:34.620
Then I turned back to Gatsby and was startled at his expression.

03:20:34.620 --> 03:20:39.420
He looked, and this is said in all contempt for the babbled slander of his garden as if

03:20:39.420 --> 03:20:41.740
he had killed a man.

03:20:41.740 --> 03:20:46.980
For a moment the set of his face could be described in just that fantastic way.

03:20:46.980 --> 03:20:51.500
It passed, and he began to talk excitedly to Daisy, denying everything, defending his

03:20:51.500 --> 03:20:54.460
name against accusations that had not been made.

03:20:54.460 --> 03:20:59.420
But with every word she was drawing further and further into herself, so he gave that up,

03:20:59.420 --> 03:21:04.660
and only the dead dream fought on as the afternoon slipped away, trying to touch what was no

03:21:04.660 --> 03:21:11.500
longer tangible, struggling unhappily, undesperingly toward that lost voice across the room.

03:21:11.500 --> 03:21:15.700
The voice begged again to go, please, Tom, I can't stand this anymore.

03:21:15.700 --> 03:21:19.940
Her frightened eyes told that whatever intentions, whatever courage she had had, were definitely

03:21:19.940 --> 03:21:21.180
gone.

03:21:21.180 --> 03:21:25.780
New toes start on home, Daisy, said Tom, in Mr. Gatsby's car.

03:21:25.780 --> 03:21:29.620
She looked at Tom, alarmed now, but he insisted with magnanimous scorn.

03:21:29.620 --> 03:21:31.020
Go on, he won't annoy you.

03:21:31.020 --> 03:21:35.620
I think he realizes that his presumptuous little flirtation is over.

03:21:35.620 --> 03:21:41.140
They were gone, without a word, snapped out, made accidental, isolated, like ghosts, even

03:21:41.140 --> 03:21:43.540
from our pity.

03:21:43.540 --> 03:21:48.580
After a moment Tom got up and began wrapping the unopened bottle of whiskey in the towel.

03:21:48.580 --> 03:21:51.660
On any of this stuff, Jordan, Nick, I didn't answer.

03:21:51.660 --> 03:21:54.500
Nick, he asked again, what?

03:21:54.500 --> 03:21:55.500
What any?

03:21:55.500 --> 03:21:59.380
No, I just remember that today's my birthday.

03:21:59.380 --> 03:22:06.460
I was thirty, before me stretched to the portentous menacing road of a new decade.

03:22:06.460 --> 03:22:11.180
It was seven o'clock when we got into the coupé with him and started for Long Island.

03:22:11.180 --> 03:22:16.980
Tom talked incessantly, exulting and laughing, but his voice was as remote from Jordan,

03:22:16.980 --> 03:22:22.820
and me as the foreign clamor on the sidewalk, or the tumult of the elevated overhead.

03:22:22.820 --> 03:22:27.220
Human sympathy has its limits, and we were content to let all their tragic arguments

03:22:27.220 --> 03:22:30.940
fade with the city lights behind.

03:22:30.940 --> 03:22:31.940
Thirty.

03:22:31.940 --> 03:22:37.100
The promise of a decade of loneliness, a thinning list of single men to know, a thinning

03:22:37.100 --> 03:22:40.940
briefcase of enthusiasm, thinning hair.

03:22:40.940 --> 03:22:43.500
But there was Jordan beside me.

03:22:43.500 --> 03:22:49.860
The one like daisy was too wise ever to carry well forgotten dreams from age to age.

03:22:49.860 --> 03:22:56.380
As we passed over the dark bridge, her one face fell lazily against my coat's shoulder,

03:22:56.380 --> 03:23:03.100
and the formidable stroke of thirty died away with the reassuring pressure of her hand.

03:23:03.100 --> 03:23:07.220
So we drove on toward death through the cooling twilight.

03:23:07.220 --> 03:23:12.260
The young Greek, Mikhailus, who ran the coffee joint beside the ash-heaps, was the principal

03:23:12.260 --> 03:23:14.700
witness at the inquest.

03:23:14.700 --> 03:23:18.700
He had slept through the heat until after five, when he strolled over to the garage and

03:23:18.700 --> 03:23:21.780
found George Wilson's sick in his office.

03:23:21.780 --> 03:23:26.140
Really sick, pales his own pale hair and shaking all over.

03:23:26.140 --> 03:23:30.660
Mikhailus advised him to go to bed, but Wilson refused, saying that he'd miss a lot of business

03:23:30.660 --> 03:23:35.740
if he did, while his neighbor was trying to persuade him a violent racket broke out

03:23:35.740 --> 03:23:36.740
overhead.

03:23:37.460 --> 03:23:41.740
I've got my wife locked in up there, explained Wilson calmly.

03:23:41.740 --> 03:23:47.220
She's going to stay there till the day after tomorrow, and then we're going to move away.

03:23:47.220 --> 03:23:48.900
Mikhailus was astonished.

03:23:48.900 --> 03:23:52.740
They had been neighbors for four years, and Wilson had never seemed faintly capable of

03:23:52.740 --> 03:23:53.980
such a statement.

03:23:53.980 --> 03:23:56.700
Generally, he was one of these worn-out men.

03:23:56.700 --> 03:24:01.140
When he wasn't working, he sat on a chair in the doorway and stared at the people in

03:24:01.140 --> 03:24:03.780
the cars that passed along the road.

03:24:03.780 --> 03:24:09.220
When anyone spoke to him, he invariably laughed in an agreeable, colorless way.

03:24:09.220 --> 03:24:12.620
He was his wife's man and not his own.

03:24:12.620 --> 03:24:17.300
So naturally, Mikhailus tried to find out what had happened, but Wilson wouldn't say a

03:24:17.300 --> 03:24:18.300
word.

03:24:18.300 --> 03:24:22.820
Instead, he began to throw curious, suspicious glances at his visitor and ask him what

03:24:22.820 --> 03:24:26.660
he'd been doing at certain times on certain days.

03:24:26.660 --> 03:24:30.580
Just as the latter was getting uneasy, some workman came past the door, bound for his

03:24:30.580 --> 03:24:36.340
restaurant, and Mikhailus took the opportunity to get away, intending to come back later.

03:24:36.340 --> 03:24:37.460
But he didn't.

03:24:37.460 --> 03:24:39.980
He supposed he forgot to, and that's all.

03:24:39.980 --> 03:24:44.420
When he came outside again a little after seven, he was reminded of the conversation, because

03:24:44.420 --> 03:24:49.420
he heard Mrs. Wilson's voice loud and scolding downstairs in the garage.

03:24:49.420 --> 03:24:51.660
«Beat me!» he heard her cry.

03:24:51.660 --> 03:24:54.820
«Throw me down and beat me, you dirty little coward!

03:24:54.820 --> 03:24:59.660
A moment later she rushed out into the dusk, waving her hands and shouting.

03:24:59.660 --> 03:25:03.820
Before he could move from his door, the business was over.

03:25:03.820 --> 03:25:08.620
The death car, as the newspaper's called it, didn't stop.

03:25:08.620 --> 03:25:13.780
It came out of the gathering darkness, wavered tragically for a moment and then disappeared

03:25:13.780 --> 03:25:15.940
around the next bend.

03:25:15.940 --> 03:25:18.420
Mikhailus wasn't even sure of its color.

03:25:18.420 --> 03:25:21.300
He told the first policeman that it was light green.

03:25:21.300 --> 03:25:25.660
The other car, the one going toward New York, came to rest a hundred yards beyond, and

03:25:25.660 --> 03:25:30.980
its driver hurried back to where Mertel Wilson, her life, violently extinguished, knelt in

03:25:30.980 --> 03:25:36.140
the road, and mingled her thick dark blood with the dust.

03:25:36.140 --> 03:25:41.580
Mikhailus and this man reached her first, but when they had torn open her shirt waist,

03:25:41.580 --> 03:25:47.860
still damp with perspiration, they saw that her left breast was swinging loose like a flap,

03:25:47.860 --> 03:25:51.300
and there was no need to listen for the heart beneath.

03:25:51.300 --> 03:25:56.580
The mouth was wide open and ripped at the corners, as though she had choked a little in giving

03:25:56.580 --> 03:26:04.060
up the tremendous vitality she had stored so long.

03:26:04.060 --> 03:26:05.540
This ends disc three.

03:26:05.540 --> 03:26:09.900
Great getsby, disc four.

03:26:09.900 --> 03:26:14.580
We saw the three or four automobile's in the crowd when we were still some distance away.

03:26:14.580 --> 03:26:18.780
Rack said Tom, that's good, Wilson will have a little business at last.

03:26:18.780 --> 03:26:23.620
He slowed down, but still without any intention of stopping until, as we came nearer, the

03:26:23.620 --> 03:26:27.960
hushed intent faces of the people at the garage door made him automatically put on the

03:26:27.960 --> 03:26:28.960
brakes.

03:26:28.960 --> 03:26:32.540
We'll take a look, he said doubtfully, just a look.

03:26:32.540 --> 03:26:38.020
I became aware now of a hollow, wailing sound which issued incessantly from the garage,

03:26:38.020 --> 03:26:42.140
a sound which as we got out of the coupé and walked toward the door resolved itself into

03:26:42.140 --> 03:26:46.780
the words, oh my God uttered over and over in a gasping moan.

03:26:47.500 --> 03:26:50.540
There's some bad trouble here, said Tom excitedly.

03:26:50.540 --> 03:26:55.260
He reached up on tiptoes and peered over a circle of heads into the garage, which was lit

03:26:55.260 --> 03:26:59.700
only by a yellow light in a swinging wire basket overhead.

03:26:59.700 --> 03:27:03.740
Then he made a harsh sound in his throat and with a violent thrusting movement of his

03:27:03.740 --> 03:27:06.860
powerful arms pushed his way through.

03:27:06.860 --> 03:27:10.940
The circle closed up again with a running murmur of expostulation.

03:27:10.940 --> 03:27:13.260
It was a minute before I could see anything at all.

03:27:13.260 --> 03:27:19.620
Then new arrivals deranged the line and Jordan and I were pushed suddenly inside.

03:27:19.620 --> 03:27:24.340
Myrtle Wilson's body wrapped in a blanket and then in another blanket as though she suffered

03:27:24.340 --> 03:27:30.600
from a chill in the hot night lay on a worktable by the wall and Tom with his back to us was

03:27:30.600 --> 03:27:33.140
bending over its motionless.

03:27:33.140 --> 03:27:37.460
Next to him stood a motorcycle policeman taking down names with much sweat and correction

03:27:37.460 --> 03:27:39.820
in a little book.

03:27:39.820 --> 03:27:45.140
At first I couldn't find the source of the high, groaning words that echoed clamorously

03:27:45.140 --> 03:27:47.380
through the bear garage.

03:27:47.380 --> 03:27:53.140
Then I saw Wilson standing on the raised threshold of his office swaying back and forth and holding

03:27:53.140 --> 03:27:55.940
to the doorposts with both hands.

03:27:55.940 --> 03:28:00.140
Some man was talking to him in a low voice and attempting from time to time to lay a hand

03:28:00.140 --> 03:28:04.100
on his shoulder, but Wilson neither heard nor saw.

03:28:04.100 --> 03:28:09.800
His eyes would drop slowly from the swinging light to the laden table by the wall and

03:28:09.800 --> 03:28:15.240
then jerk back to the light again and he gave out incessantly his high, horrible call.

03:28:15.240 --> 03:28:19.120
Oh my god, oh my god, oh my god.

03:28:19.120 --> 03:28:23.280
Presently Tom lifted his head with a jerk and after staring around the garage with glazed

03:28:23.280 --> 03:28:27.840
eyes, addressed a mumbled incoherent remark to the policeman.

03:28:27.840 --> 03:28:33.960
M-A-V, the policeman was saying, oh no, R corrected the man.

03:28:33.960 --> 03:28:35.960
M-A-V-R-O.

03:28:35.960 --> 03:28:39.120
Oh, listen to me, muttered Tom fiercely.

03:28:39.120 --> 03:28:47.280
R said the policeman, oh gee, gee, he looked up as Tom's broad hand fell sharply on his shoulder.

03:28:47.280 --> 03:28:48.920
What do you want, fella?

03:28:48.920 --> 03:28:49.920
What happened?

03:28:49.920 --> 03:28:51.600
That's what I want to know.

03:28:51.600 --> 03:28:54.720
Auto-hitter instantly killed.

03:28:54.720 --> 03:28:56.080
Instantly killed.

03:28:56.080 --> 03:28:57.720
Repeated Tom staring.

03:28:57.720 --> 03:29:00.960
She ran out in the road, son of a bitch didn't even stop her car.

03:29:01.480 --> 03:29:05.600
There was two cars, Sid McAillus, one coming, one going, see?

03:29:05.600 --> 03:29:06.600
Going where?

03:29:06.600 --> 03:29:08.080
After the policeman keenly.

03:29:08.080 --> 03:29:09.480
One going each way.

03:29:09.480 --> 03:29:14.920
Well, she, his hand rose toward the blankets but stopped halfway and fell to his side.

03:29:14.920 --> 03:29:18.520
She ran out there and the one coming from New York knocked right into her, going 30 or

03:29:18.520 --> 03:29:20.520
40 miles an hour.

03:29:20.520 --> 03:29:23.360
What's the name of this place here, demanded the officer?

03:29:23.360 --> 03:29:25.040
Hasn't got any name?

03:29:25.040 --> 03:29:28.360
A pale, well-dressed negro stepped near.

03:29:28.360 --> 03:29:29.520
It was a yellow car.

03:29:29.520 --> 03:29:31.960
He said, big yellow car, new.

03:29:31.960 --> 03:29:32.960
See the accident?

03:29:32.960 --> 03:29:34.440
Ask the policeman.

03:29:34.440 --> 03:29:39.480
No but the car passed me down the road, going faster and 40, going 50, 60.

03:29:39.480 --> 03:29:40.600
Come here and let's have your name.

03:29:40.600 --> 03:29:42.560
Look out now, I want to get his name.

03:29:42.560 --> 03:29:46.640
Some words of this conversation must have reached Wilson swaying in the office door.

03:29:46.640 --> 03:29:50.920
For suddenly a new theme found voice among his gasping cries.

03:29:50.920 --> 03:29:53.480
You don't have to tell me what kind of car it was?

03:29:53.480 --> 03:29:56.000
I know what kind of car it was.

03:29:56.000 --> 03:30:00.520
Watching Tom, I saw the wand of muscle back of his shoulder tighten under his coat.

03:30:00.520 --> 03:30:04.680
He walked quickly over to Wilson and standing in front of him seized him firmly by the upper

03:30:04.680 --> 03:30:05.680
arms.

03:30:05.680 --> 03:30:06.680
You've got to pull yourself together.

03:30:06.680 --> 03:30:08.600
He fell upon Tom.

03:30:08.600 --> 03:30:12.840
He started up on his tiptoes and then would have collapsed to his knees had not Tom held

03:30:12.840 --> 03:30:13.840
him up right.

03:30:13.840 --> 03:30:16.080
Listen, said Tom, shaking him a little.

03:30:16.080 --> 03:30:18.400
I just got here a minute ago from New York.

03:30:18.400 --> 03:30:21.400
I was bringing you that coupé we've been talking about.

03:30:21.400 --> 03:30:24.320
That yellow car I was driving this afternoon wasn't mine.

03:30:24.320 --> 03:30:25.320
You're here?

03:30:25.320 --> 03:30:27.640
I haven't seen it all afternoon.

03:30:27.640 --> 03:30:32.160
Only the Negro and I were near enough to hear what he said, but the policeman caught something

03:30:32.160 --> 03:30:34.560
in the tone and looked over with truckulent eyes.

03:30:34.560 --> 03:30:36.040
What's all that?

03:30:36.040 --> 03:30:37.040
He demanded.

03:30:37.040 --> 03:30:38.360
I'm a friend of his.

03:30:38.360 --> 03:30:41.840
Tom turned his head but kept his hands firm on Wilson's body.

03:30:41.840 --> 03:30:43.800
He says he knows the car that did it.

03:30:43.800 --> 03:30:46.080
It was a yellow car.

03:30:46.080 --> 03:30:50.800
Some dim impulse moved the policeman to look suspiciously at Tom.

03:30:50.800 --> 03:30:52.360
And what color is your car?

03:30:52.680 --> 03:30:54.400
It's a blue car, a coupé.

03:30:54.400 --> 03:30:56.960
We've come straight from New York, I said.

03:30:56.960 --> 03:31:00.120
Someone who had been driving a little behind us confirmed this and the policeman turned

03:31:00.120 --> 03:31:01.120
away.

03:31:01.120 --> 03:31:05.760
Now, if you'll let me have that name again and correct picking up Wilson like a doll,

03:31:05.760 --> 03:31:10.040
Tom carried him into the office, set him down in a chair and came back.

03:31:10.040 --> 03:31:13.600
If somebody will come here and sit with him, he snapped authoritatively.

03:31:13.600 --> 03:31:17.680
He watched while the two men standing closest, glanced at each other and went unwillingly

03:31:17.680 --> 03:31:19.240
into the room.

03:31:19.240 --> 03:31:24.800
When Tom shut the door on them and came down the single step, his eyes avoiding the table.

03:31:24.800 --> 03:31:28.960
As he passed close to me, he whispered, let's get out.

03:31:28.960 --> 03:31:33.480
Self-consciously with his authoritative arms breaking the way, we pushed through the still

03:31:33.480 --> 03:31:38.900
gathering crowd, passing a hurried doctor, case in hand, who had been sent for in wild

03:31:38.900 --> 03:31:41.320
hope half an hour ago.

03:31:41.320 --> 03:31:45.080
Tom drove slowly until we were beyond the bend.

03:31:45.080 --> 03:31:49.720
When his foot came down hard and the coupé raced along through the night, in a little

03:31:49.720 --> 03:31:56.080
while I heard a low husky sob and saw that the tears were overflowing down his face,

03:31:56.080 --> 03:31:58.920
the goddamn coward he whimpered.

03:31:58.920 --> 03:32:03.080
He didn't even stop his car.

03:32:03.080 --> 03:32:08.760
The Buchanan's house floated suddenly toward us through the dark rustling trees.

03:32:08.760 --> 03:32:12.760
Tom stopped beside the porch and looked up at the second floor where two windows bloomed

03:32:12.760 --> 03:32:14.840
with light among the vines.

03:32:14.840 --> 03:32:17.520
Daisy's home, he said.

03:32:17.520 --> 03:32:20.680
As we got out of the car, he glanced at me in frowns, lightly.

03:32:20.680 --> 03:32:25.320
I, out of our drop to you in West Agnick, there's nothing we can do tonight.

03:32:25.320 --> 03:32:30.120
A change had come over him, and he spoke gravely and with decision.

03:32:30.120 --> 03:32:34.400
As we walked across the moonlight gravel to the porch, he disposed of the situation in

03:32:34.400 --> 03:32:35.960
a few brisk phrases.

03:32:35.960 --> 03:32:39.440
I'll telephone for a taxi to take you home, and while you're waiting you and Jordan

03:32:39.440 --> 03:32:42.920
better go in the kitchen and have them get you some supper if you want any.

03:32:42.920 --> 03:32:43.920
He opened the door.

03:32:43.920 --> 03:32:44.920
Come in.

03:32:44.920 --> 03:32:49.520
No, thanks, but I'd be glad if you'd order me the taxi I'll wait outside.

03:32:49.520 --> 03:32:50.960
Jordan put her hand on my arm.

03:32:50.960 --> 03:32:52.760
Would you come in, Nick?

03:32:52.760 --> 03:32:54.360
No, thanks.

03:32:54.360 --> 03:32:58.680
I was feeling a little sick and I wanted to be alone, but Jordan lingered for a moment

03:32:58.680 --> 03:32:59.680
more.

03:32:59.680 --> 03:33:02.640
It's only half past nine, she said.

03:33:02.640 --> 03:33:04.360
I'd be damned if I go in.

03:33:04.360 --> 03:33:08.760
I'd had enough of all of them for one day, and suddenly that included Jordan too.

03:33:08.760 --> 03:33:12.040
She must have seen something of this in my expression, for she turned abruptly away

03:33:12.040 --> 03:33:14.680
and ran up the porch steps into the house.

03:33:14.680 --> 03:33:19.080
I sat down for a few minutes with my head in my hands, until I heard the phone taken

03:33:19.080 --> 03:33:22.640
up inside and the butler's voice calling a taxi.

03:33:22.640 --> 03:33:27.800
Then I walked slowly down the drive away from the house, intending to wait by the gate.

03:33:27.800 --> 03:33:32.680
I hadn't gone twenty yards when I heard my name, and Gatsby stepped from between two bushes

03:33:32.680 --> 03:33:34.480
into the path.

03:33:34.480 --> 03:33:38.440
I must have felt pretty weird by that time, because I could think of nothing except

03:33:38.440 --> 03:33:42.400
the luminosity of his pink suit under the moon.

03:33:42.400 --> 03:33:47.000
What are you doing, I inquired, just standing here old sport?

03:33:47.000 --> 03:33:50.200
Somehow that seemed a despicable occupation.

03:33:50.200 --> 03:33:52.640
For all I knew he was going to rob the house in a moment.

03:33:52.640 --> 03:33:57.840
I wouldn't have been surprised to see sinister faces, the faces of wolfshimes, people behind

03:33:57.840 --> 03:33:59.640
him in the dark shrubbery.

03:33:59.640 --> 03:34:02.200
Did you see any trouble on the road?

03:34:02.200 --> 03:34:03.720
He asked after a minute.

03:34:03.720 --> 03:34:04.720
Yes.

03:34:04.720 --> 03:34:05.720
He hesitated.

03:34:05.720 --> 03:34:06.720
Was she killed?

03:34:06.720 --> 03:34:07.720
I thought so.

03:34:07.720 --> 03:34:08.720
Yes.

03:34:08.720 --> 03:34:10.880
I told Daisy I thought so.

03:34:10.880 --> 03:34:12.760
It's better that the shock should all come at once.

03:34:12.760 --> 03:34:14.720
She stood it pretty well.

03:34:14.720 --> 03:34:18.200
He spoke as if Daisy's reaction was the only thing that mattered.

03:34:18.200 --> 03:34:20.960
I got to West Eggway, a side road he went on.

03:34:20.960 --> 03:34:21.960
And left the car in my garage.

03:34:21.960 --> 03:34:25.400
I don't think anybody saw us, but of course I can't be sure.

03:34:25.400 --> 03:34:29.600
I disliked him so much by this time that I didn't find it necessary to tell him he was

03:34:29.600 --> 03:34:31.400
wrong.

03:34:31.400 --> 03:34:33.800
Who was the woman, inquired?

03:34:33.800 --> 03:34:34.800
Her name was Wilson.

03:34:34.800 --> 03:34:36.200
Her husband owns the garage.

03:34:36.200 --> 03:34:38.880
How the devil did it happen?

03:34:38.880 --> 03:34:42.280
Well, I tried to swing the wheel.

03:34:42.280 --> 03:34:43.280
He broke off.

03:34:43.280 --> 03:34:45.800
And suddenly I guessed it the truth.

03:34:45.800 --> 03:34:47.600
Was Daisy driving?

03:34:47.600 --> 03:34:48.600
Yes.

03:34:48.600 --> 03:34:51.000
You said after a moment.

03:34:51.000 --> 03:34:53.240
But of course I'll say I was.

03:34:53.240 --> 03:34:56.960
You see when we left New York she was very nervous and she thought it would steady her

03:34:56.960 --> 03:34:58.280
to drive.

03:34:58.280 --> 03:35:02.000
And this woman rushed out at us just as we were passing a car coming the other way.

03:35:02.000 --> 03:35:03.720
It all happened in a minute.

03:35:03.720 --> 03:35:06.120
But it seemed to me that she wanted to speak to us.

03:35:06.120 --> 03:35:07.840
Thought we were somebody she knew.

03:35:07.840 --> 03:35:11.680
Well, first Daisy turned away from the woman toward the other car and then she lost her

03:35:11.680 --> 03:35:13.560
nerve and turned back.

03:35:13.560 --> 03:35:17.800
The second my hand reached the wheel, I felt the shock it must have killed her instantly.

03:35:17.800 --> 03:35:19.560
It ripped her open.

03:35:19.560 --> 03:35:21.040
Don't tell me old sport.

03:35:21.040 --> 03:35:22.040
He winced.

03:35:22.040 --> 03:35:24.000
Anyhow, Daisy stepped on it.

03:35:24.000 --> 03:35:26.280
I tried to make her stop, but she couldn't.

03:35:26.280 --> 03:35:28.520
So I pulled on the emergency brake.

03:35:28.520 --> 03:35:31.920
Then she fell over into my lap and I drove on.

03:35:31.920 --> 03:35:33.600
She'll be alright tomorrow.

03:35:33.600 --> 03:35:34.600
She said presently.

03:35:34.600 --> 03:35:38.720
I'm just going to wait here and see if he tries to bother her about that unpleasantness

03:35:38.720 --> 03:35:40.120
this afternoon.

03:35:40.120 --> 03:35:43.640
She's locked herself into a room and if he tries any brutality she's going to turn the light

03:35:43.640 --> 03:35:45.480
out and on again.

03:35:45.480 --> 03:35:47.000
He won't touch her, I said.

03:35:47.000 --> 03:35:48.800
He's not thinking about her.

03:35:48.800 --> 03:35:50.840
I don't trust him old sport.

03:35:50.840 --> 03:35:52.320
How long are you going to wait?

03:35:52.320 --> 03:35:54.200
All night if necessary.

03:35:54.200 --> 03:35:56.800
Anyhow till they all go to bed.

03:35:56.800 --> 03:36:00.120
A new point of view occurred to me.

03:36:00.120 --> 03:36:03.640
Rose Tom found out that Daisy had been driving.

03:36:03.640 --> 03:36:05.920
He might think he saw a connection in it.

03:36:05.920 --> 03:36:07.920
He might think anything.

03:36:07.920 --> 03:36:08.920
I looked at the house.

03:36:08.920 --> 03:36:13.240
There were two or three bright windows downstairs and the pink glow from Daisy's room on the

03:36:13.240 --> 03:36:15.320
second floor.

03:36:15.320 --> 03:36:16.320
You wait here, I said.

03:36:16.320 --> 03:36:18.720
I'll see if there's any sign of a commotion.

03:36:18.720 --> 03:36:23.360
I walked back along the border of the lawn, traversed the gravel softly and tipped out

03:36:23.360 --> 03:36:25.360
up the veranda steps.

03:36:25.360 --> 03:36:29.760
The drawing-room curtains were open and I saw that the room was empty, crossing the porch

03:36:29.760 --> 03:36:33.160
where we had dine that June, three months before.

03:36:33.160 --> 03:36:37.640
I came to a small rectangle of light which I guess was the pantry window.

03:36:37.640 --> 03:36:40.840
The blind was drawn, but I found a rift at the sill.

03:36:40.840 --> 03:36:44.560
Daisy and Tom were sitting opposite each other at the kitchen table with a plate of cold

03:36:44.560 --> 03:36:47.680
fried chicken between them and two bottles of ale.

03:36:47.680 --> 03:36:52.240
He was talking intently across the table at her and in his earnestness his hand had fallen

03:36:52.240 --> 03:36:54.600
upon and covered her own.

03:36:54.600 --> 03:36:57.920
Once in a while she looked up at him and nodded in agreement.

03:36:57.920 --> 03:37:02.600
They weren't happy and neither of them had touched the chicken or the ale, and yet they

03:37:02.600 --> 03:37:04.720
weren't unhappy either.

03:37:04.720 --> 03:37:09.520
There was an unmistakable air of natural intimacy about the picture and anybody would have

03:37:09.520 --> 03:37:13.000
said that they were conspiring together.

03:37:13.000 --> 03:37:17.720
As I tipped out from the porch I heard my taxi feeling its way along the dark road toward

03:37:17.720 --> 03:37:18.720
the house.

03:37:18.720 --> 03:37:21.760
Gatsby was waiting where I had left him in the drive.

03:37:22.480 --> 03:37:23.920
Is it all quiet up there?

03:37:23.920 --> 03:37:25.480
He asked anxiously.

03:37:25.480 --> 03:37:27.000
Yes, it's all quiet.

03:37:27.000 --> 03:37:28.000
I hesitated.

03:37:28.000 --> 03:37:30.680
You'd better come home and get some sleep.

03:37:30.680 --> 03:37:31.840
He shook his head.

03:37:31.840 --> 03:37:34.080
I want to wait here until Daisy goes to bed.

03:37:34.080 --> 03:37:35.920
Good night, old sport.

03:37:35.920 --> 03:37:41.440
He put his hands in his coat pockets and turned back eagerly to his scrutiny of the house,

03:37:41.440 --> 03:37:45.720
as though my presence marred the sacredness of the vigil.

03:37:45.720 --> 03:37:51.320
So I walked away and left him standing there in the moonlight, watching over nothing.

03:37:52.320 --> 03:37:53.720
Chapter 8

03:37:53.720 --> 03:37:57.200
I couldn't sleep all night.

03:37:57.200 --> 03:38:03.240
A foghorn was groaning incessantly on the sound, and I tossed half-sick between grotesque

03:38:03.240 --> 03:38:07.600
reality and savage, frightening dreams.

03:38:07.600 --> 03:38:12.360
Toward dawn I heard a taxi go up Gatsby's drive, and immediately I jumped out of bed and

03:38:12.360 --> 03:38:13.720
began to dress.

03:38:13.720 --> 03:38:19.040
I felt that I had something to tell him, something to warn him about, and warning would be too

03:38:19.040 --> 03:38:20.040
late.

03:38:20.040 --> 03:38:24.000
Crossing his lawn I saw that his front door was still open, and he was leaning against

03:38:24.000 --> 03:38:28.720
a table in the hall heavy with dejection or sleep.

03:38:28.720 --> 03:38:31.040
Nothing happened, he said, wonely.

03:38:31.040 --> 03:38:35.040
I waited, and about four o'clock she came to the window and stood there for a minute,

03:38:35.040 --> 03:38:37.920
and then turned out the light.

03:38:37.920 --> 03:38:42.640
His house had never seen so enormous to me as it did that night, when we hunted through

03:38:42.640 --> 03:38:45.480
the great rooms for cigarettes.

03:38:45.480 --> 03:38:50.900
We pushed aside curtains that were like pavilions, and felt over enumerable feet of dark

03:38:50.900 --> 03:38:56.200
wall for electric light switches, once I tumbled with a sort of splash upon the keys of a

03:38:56.200 --> 03:38:58.240
ghostly piano.

03:38:58.240 --> 03:39:02.680
There was an inexplicable amount of dust everywhere, and the rooms were musty as though they hadn't

03:39:02.680 --> 03:39:04.640
been aired for many days.

03:39:04.640 --> 03:39:10.680
I found the humidor on an unfamiliar table with two stale, dry cigarettes inside.

03:39:10.680 --> 03:39:15.680
Looking open the French windows of the drawing room we sat, smoking out into the darkness.

03:39:15.680 --> 03:39:18.000
You ought to go away, I said.

03:39:18.000 --> 03:39:20.760
It was pretty certain they'll trace your car.

03:39:20.760 --> 03:39:23.280
Go away now, old sport!

03:39:23.280 --> 03:39:26.720
Go to Atlantic City for a week or up to Montreal.

03:39:26.720 --> 03:39:27.960
He wouldn't consider it.

03:39:27.960 --> 03:39:31.680
He couldn't possibly leave Daisy until he knew what she was going to do.

03:39:31.680 --> 03:39:37.320
He was clutching at some last hope, and I couldn't bear to shake him free.

03:39:37.320 --> 03:39:41.520
He was this night that he told me the strange story of his youth with Dan Cody, told

03:39:41.520 --> 03:39:47.280
it to me because Jay Gatsby had broken up like glass against Tom's hard malice, and the

03:39:47.280 --> 03:39:51.200
long, secret extravaganza was played out.

03:39:51.200 --> 03:39:54.880
I think that he would have acknowledged anything now without reserve, but he wanted to talk

03:39:54.880 --> 03:39:56.720
about Daisy.

03:39:56.720 --> 03:40:00.240
She was the first nice girl he had ever known.

03:40:00.240 --> 03:40:04.480
In various unrevealed capacities he had come in contact with such people, but always with

03:40:04.480 --> 03:40:07.520
indesernable barbed wire between.

03:40:07.520 --> 03:40:10.040
He found her excitingly desirable.

03:40:10.040 --> 03:40:14.280
He went to her house, at first with other officers from Camp Tatla, then alone.

03:40:14.280 --> 03:40:15.880
It amazed him.

03:40:15.880 --> 03:40:20.760
He had never been in such a beautiful house before, but what gave it an air of breathless

03:40:20.760 --> 03:40:23.560
intensity was that Daisy lived there.

03:40:23.560 --> 03:40:27.680
It was as casual a thing to her as his tent out at Camp was to him.

03:40:27.680 --> 03:40:33.120
There was a ripe mystery about it, a hint of bedrooms upstairs, more beautiful and cool

03:40:33.120 --> 03:40:38.200
than other bedrooms, of gay and radiant activities taking place through its corridors, and of

03:40:38.200 --> 03:40:43.560
romances that were not musty and laid away already in lavender, but fresh and breathing

03:40:43.560 --> 03:40:48.960
and redolent of this year shining motorcars and of dancers whose flowers were scarcely

03:40:48.960 --> 03:40:50.360
withered.

03:40:50.360 --> 03:40:53.080
It excited him too that many men had already loved Daisy.

03:40:53.080 --> 03:40:56.160
It increased her value in his eyes.

03:40:56.160 --> 03:41:01.600
He felt their presence all about the house, pervading the air with the shades and echoes

03:41:01.600 --> 03:41:02.600
over his eyes.

03:41:02.600 --> 03:41:05.560
But he still devised Daisy's house by a colossal accident.

03:41:05.560 --> 03:41:10.440
However glorious might be his future as Jay Gatsby, he was a present, a penniless young

03:41:10.440 --> 03:41:16.260
man without a past, and at any moment the invisible cloak of his uniform might slip from his

03:41:16.260 --> 03:41:17.680
shoulders.

03:41:17.680 --> 03:41:19.680
So he made the most of his time.

03:41:19.680 --> 03:41:22.680
He took what he could get, ravenously and unscrupulously.

03:41:22.680 --> 03:41:27.960
Eventually he took Daisy one still October night, took her because he had no real right

03:41:27.960 --> 03:41:30.200
to touch her hand.

03:41:30.200 --> 03:41:34.560
He might have despised himself, for he had certainly taken her under false pretenses.

03:41:34.560 --> 03:41:39.040
I don't mean that he had traded on his phantom millions, but he had deliberately given Daisy

03:41:39.040 --> 03:41:40.920
a sense of security.

03:41:40.920 --> 03:41:44.840
He let her believe that he was a person from much the same stratum as herself, that he

03:41:44.840 --> 03:41:49.400
was fully able to take care of her, as a matter of fact he had no such facilities.

03:41:49.400 --> 03:41:54.600
He had no comfortable family standing behind him, and he was liable at the whim of an impersonal

03:41:54.600 --> 03:41:58.280
government to be blown anywhere about the world.

03:41:58.280 --> 03:42:03.120
But he didn't despise himself, and it didn't turn out as he had imagined.

03:42:03.120 --> 03:42:08.760
He had intended probably to take what he could and go, but now he found that he had committed

03:42:08.760 --> 03:42:11.680
himself to the following of a grail.

03:42:11.680 --> 03:42:16.680
He knew that Daisy was extraordinary, but he didn't realise just how extraordinary a nice

03:42:16.680 --> 03:42:18.520
girl could be.

03:42:18.520 --> 03:42:25.440
She vanished into her rich house, into her rich, full life, leaving Gatsby nothing.

03:42:25.440 --> 03:42:27.280
He felt married to her.

03:42:27.280 --> 03:42:29.280
That was all.

03:42:29.280 --> 03:42:34.400
When they met again two days later it was Gatsby who was breathless, who was somehow betrayed.

03:42:34.400 --> 03:42:39.880
Her porch was bright with the bot-ludgery of star-shine, the wicker of the setty squeaked

03:42:39.880 --> 03:42:46.200
fashionably as she turned toward him, and he kissed her curious and lovely mouth.

03:42:46.200 --> 03:42:50.720
She had caught a cold, and had made her voice huskier and more charming than ever, and

03:42:50.720 --> 03:42:57.200
Gatsby was overwhelmingly aware of the youth and mystery that wealth in prisons and preserves,

03:42:57.200 --> 03:43:03.280
of the freshness of many clothes, and of Daisy gleaming like silver, safe and proud above

03:43:03.280 --> 03:43:06.360
the hot struggles of the poor.

03:43:06.360 --> 03:43:11.040
I can't describe to you how surprised I was to find out I loved her old sport.

03:43:11.040 --> 03:43:14.560
I even hoped for a while that she'd throw me over, but she didn't because she was in love

03:43:14.560 --> 03:43:15.960
with me, too.

03:43:15.960 --> 03:43:19.640
She thought I knew a lot because I knew different things from her.

03:43:19.640 --> 03:43:24.800
Well, there I was way off my ambitions, getting deeper in love every minute and all of a sudden

03:43:24.800 --> 03:43:26.280
I didn't care.

03:43:26.280 --> 03:43:29.940
What was the use of doing great things if I could have a better time telling her what

03:43:29.940 --> 03:43:32.640
I was going to do?

03:43:32.640 --> 03:43:37.800
On the last afternoon before he went abroad he sat with Daisy in his arms for a long,

03:43:37.800 --> 03:43:39.400
silent time.

03:43:39.400 --> 03:43:44.720
It was a cold, fall day with fire in the room, and her cheeks flushed.

03:43:44.720 --> 03:43:49.840
Now and then she moved, and he changed his arm a little, and once he kissed her dark shining

03:43:49.840 --> 03:43:51.160
hair.

03:43:51.160 --> 03:43:54.840
The afternoon had made them tranquil for a while, as if to give them a deep memory for

03:43:54.840 --> 03:43:57.280
the long parting the next day promised.

03:43:57.280 --> 03:44:02.840
They had never been closer in their month of love, nor communicated more profoundly one

03:44:02.840 --> 03:44:07.920
with another than when she brushed silent lips against his coach's shoulder, or when he

03:44:07.920 --> 03:44:10.120
touched the end of her fingers, gently.

03:44:10.120 --> 03:44:12.160
He did extraordinarily well in the war.

03:44:12.640 --> 03:44:16.480
It was a captain before he went to the front, and following the argon battles he got his

03:44:16.480 --> 03:44:20.400
majority and the command of the divisional machine guns.

03:44:20.400 --> 03:44:25.520
After the armistice he tried frantically to get home, but some complication or misunderstandings

03:44:25.520 --> 03:44:27.600
sent him to Oxford instead.

03:44:27.600 --> 03:44:31.560
He was worried now, there was a quality of nervous despair in Daisy's letters.

03:44:31.560 --> 03:44:33.600
She didn't see why he couldn't come.

03:44:33.600 --> 03:44:38.120
She was feeling the pressure of the world outside, and she wanted to see him and feel his presence

03:44:38.120 --> 03:44:43.040
beside her, and be reassured that she was doing the right thing after all.

03:44:43.040 --> 03:44:48.840
For Daisy was young, and her artificial world was redulent of orchids and pleasant cheerful

03:44:48.840 --> 03:44:54.520
snobbery and orchestras which set the rhythm of the year, summing up the sadness and suggestiveness

03:44:54.520 --> 03:44:57.080
of life in new tunes.

03:44:57.080 --> 03:45:02.680
All night the saxophones wail the hopeless comment of the Beale Street Blues, while a hundred

03:45:02.680 --> 03:45:07.720
pairs of golden and silver slippers shuffled the shining dust.

03:45:07.720 --> 03:45:13.040
At the grey tea-hour there were always rooms that throbbed incessantly with this low,

03:45:13.040 --> 03:45:18.960
sweet fever, while fresh faces drifted here and there like rose petals blown by the sad

03:45:18.960 --> 03:45:21.720
horns around the floor.

03:45:21.720 --> 03:45:27.000
Through this twilight universe Daisy began to move again with the season.

03:45:27.000 --> 03:45:31.600
Suddenly she was again keeping half a dozen dates a day with half a dozen men, and drowsing

03:45:31.600 --> 03:45:38.240
asleep at dawn the beads and chiffon of an evening dress tangled among dying orchids on

03:45:38.240 --> 03:45:40.200
the floor beside her bed.

03:45:40.200 --> 03:45:43.560
And all the time something within her was crying for a decision.

03:45:43.560 --> 03:45:49.840
She wanted her life shaped now, immediately, and the decision must be made by some force

03:45:49.840 --> 03:45:55.200
of love, of money, of unquestionable practicality that was close at hand.

03:45:55.200 --> 03:46:00.760
That force took shape in the middle of spring with the arrival of Tom Buchanan.

03:46:00.760 --> 03:46:06.840
There was a wholesome bulkiness about his person and his position, and Daisy was flattered.

03:46:06.840 --> 03:46:11.320
Doubtless there was a certain struggle and a certain relief that let her reach to

03:46:11.320 --> 03:46:15.600
Gatsby while he was still at Oxford.

03:46:15.600 --> 03:46:20.880
It was dawn now on Long Island, and we went about opening the rest of the windows downstairs,

03:46:20.880 --> 03:46:24.840
filling the house with grey, turning gold, turning light.

03:46:24.840 --> 03:46:30.000
The shadow of a tree fell abruptly across the dew and ghostly birds began to sing among

03:46:30.000 --> 03:46:31.760
the blue leaves.

03:46:31.760 --> 03:46:37.240
There was a slow, pleasant movement in the air, scarcely a wind promising a cool, lovely

03:46:37.240 --> 03:46:38.240
day.

03:46:38.240 --> 03:46:42.160
I don't think she ever loved him, Gatsby turned around from a window and looked at me

03:46:42.160 --> 03:46:43.640
challengingly.

03:46:43.640 --> 03:46:44.640
You must remember old sports.

03:46:44.640 --> 03:46:47.000
She was very excited this afternoon.

03:46:47.000 --> 03:46:50.560
He told her those things in a way that frightened her, that made it look as if I was some kind

03:46:50.560 --> 03:46:55.400
of cheap sharper, and the result was she hardly knew what she was saying.

03:46:55.400 --> 03:46:57.520
He sat down gloomily.

03:46:57.520 --> 03:47:01.080
Of course she might have loved him for just a minute when they were first married, and

03:47:01.080 --> 03:47:04.120
loved me more even better at his remark.

03:47:04.120 --> 03:47:06.480
In any case, he said it was just personal.

03:47:06.480 --> 03:47:11.840
And what could you make of that, except to suspect some intensity in his conception of

03:47:11.840 --> 03:47:14.680
the affair that couldn't be measured?

03:47:14.680 --> 03:47:19.040
He came back from France when Tom and Daisy were still on their wedding trip, and made

03:47:19.040 --> 03:47:24.080
a miserable but irresistible journey to Louisville on the last of his army pay.

03:47:24.080 --> 03:47:28.440
He stayed there a week, walking the streets where their footsteps had clicked together

03:47:28.440 --> 03:47:32.760
through the November night, and revisiting the out of the way places to which they had

03:47:32.760 --> 03:47:35.440
driven in her white car.

03:47:35.440 --> 03:47:40.560
Just as Daisy's house had always seemed to him more mysterious and gay than other houses,

03:47:40.560 --> 03:47:45.480
so his idea of the city itself, even though she was gone from it, was pervaded with a

03:47:45.480 --> 03:47:48.320
melancholy beauty.

03:47:48.320 --> 03:47:53.040
He left feeling that if he had searched harder, he might have found her, that he was leaving

03:47:53.040 --> 03:47:54.720
her behind.

03:47:54.720 --> 03:47:58.440
The day coach, he was penniless now, was hot.

03:47:58.440 --> 03:48:03.600
He went out to the open vestibule and sat down on a folding chair, and the stations slid

03:48:03.600 --> 03:48:08.240
away in the backs of unfamiliar buildings moved by.

03:48:08.240 --> 03:48:12.680
Then out into the spring fields where a yellow trolley raced them for a minute, with people

03:48:12.680 --> 03:48:18.800
in it who might once have seen the pale magic of her face along the casual street.

03:48:18.800 --> 03:48:23.780
The track curved, and now was going away from the sun, which as it sank lower seemed

03:48:23.780 --> 03:48:29.800
to spread itself in benediction over the vanishing city where she had drawn her breath.

03:48:29.800 --> 03:48:34.100
He stretched out his hand desperately as if to snatch only a whisper there to save a

03:48:34.100 --> 03:48:39.380
fragment of the spot that she had made lovely for him, but it was all going by too fast

03:48:39.380 --> 03:48:44.840
now for his blurred eyes, and he knew that he had lost that part of it the freshest

03:48:44.840 --> 03:48:48.000
and the best for ever.

03:48:48.000 --> 03:48:51.960
It was nine o'clock when we finished breakfast and went out on the porch.

03:48:51.960 --> 03:48:56.160
The night had made a sharp difference in the weather, and there was an autumn flavor in

03:48:56.160 --> 03:48:57.240
the air.

03:48:57.240 --> 03:49:01.320
The gardener, the last one of Gatsby's former servants, came to the foot of the steps.

03:49:01.320 --> 03:49:04.480
I'm going to drain the pool today, Mr. Gatsby.

03:49:04.480 --> 03:49:08.280
Leaves will start falling pretty soon, and then there's always trouble with the pipes.

03:49:08.280 --> 03:49:11.000
Don't do it today, Gatsby answered.

03:49:11.000 --> 03:49:12.640
You turned to me apologetically.

03:49:12.640 --> 03:49:16.040
You know, old sport I've never used that pool all summer?

03:49:16.040 --> 03:49:18.400
I looked at my watch and stood up.

03:49:18.400 --> 03:49:20.280
Twelve minutes to my train.

03:49:20.280 --> 03:49:21.800
I didn't want to go to the city.

03:49:21.800 --> 03:49:26.120
I wasn't worth a decent stroke of work, but it was more than that I didn't want to leave

03:49:26.120 --> 03:49:27.520
Gatsby.

03:49:27.520 --> 03:49:31.680
I missed that train, and then another before I could get myself away.

03:49:31.680 --> 03:49:34.640
I'll call you up, I said finally.

03:49:34.640 --> 03:49:35.880
Do old sport.

03:49:35.880 --> 03:49:38.160
I'll call you about noon.

03:49:38.160 --> 03:49:40.200
We walked slowly down the steps.

03:49:40.200 --> 03:49:42.720
I suppose Daisy'll call too.

03:49:42.720 --> 03:49:46.080
She looked at me anxiously as if he hoped I'd corroborate this.

03:49:46.080 --> 03:49:47.480
I suppose so.

03:49:47.480 --> 03:49:49.320
Well, goodbye.

03:49:49.320 --> 03:49:52.040
We shook hands, and I started away.

03:49:52.040 --> 03:49:55.640
Just before I reached the hedge, I remembered something and turned around.

03:49:55.640 --> 03:49:59.280
There are rotten crowd, I shouted across the lawn.

03:49:59.280 --> 03:50:02.080
You're worth the whole damn bunch put together.

03:50:02.080 --> 03:50:05.320
I've always been glad I said that.

03:50:05.320 --> 03:50:08.720
It was the only compliment I ever gave him, because I disapproved of him from beginning

03:50:08.720 --> 03:50:10.400
to end.

03:50:10.400 --> 03:50:15.960
Daisy knotted politely, and then his face broke into that radiant and understanding smile.

03:50:15.960 --> 03:50:20.240
As if we'd been an ecstatic cahoots on that fact all the time.

03:50:20.240 --> 03:50:25.800
His gorgeous pink rag of a suit made a bright spot of color against the white steps.

03:50:25.800 --> 03:50:31.160
And I thought of the night when I first came to his ancestral home three months before.

03:50:31.160 --> 03:50:36.480
The lawn and drive had been crowded with the faces of those who guessed at his corruption,

03:50:36.480 --> 03:50:42.240
and he had stood on those steps concealing his incorruptible dream as he waved them goodbye.

03:50:42.240 --> 03:50:44.080
I thanked him for his hospitality.

03:50:44.080 --> 03:50:46.440
We were always thanking him for that.

03:50:46.440 --> 03:50:48.440
I and the others.

03:50:48.440 --> 03:50:53.160
Goodbye, I call my enjoy, and be a tried for a while to list the quotations on an interminable

03:50:53.160 --> 03:50:57.280
amount of stock, then I fell asleep in my swivel chair.

03:50:57.280 --> 03:51:02.080
Just before noon the phone woke me and I started up with sweat breaking out on my forehead.

03:51:02.080 --> 03:51:03.400
It was Jordan Baker.

03:51:03.400 --> 03:51:06.840
She often called me up at this hour because the uncertainty of her own movements between

03:51:06.840 --> 03:51:12.800
hotels and clubs and private houses made her hard to find in any other way.

03:51:12.800 --> 03:51:17.900
Usually her voice came over the wire as something fresh and cool, as if it divot from the green

03:51:17.900 --> 03:51:23.200
golf links that come sailing in at the office window, but this morning it seemed harsh and

03:51:23.200 --> 03:51:24.200
dry.

03:51:24.200 --> 03:51:26.720
I've left Daisy's house, she said.

03:51:26.720 --> 03:51:31.680
I'm at Hampstead, and I'm going down to South Hampton this afternoon.

03:51:31.680 --> 03:51:36.480
We had had been tactful to leave Daisy's house, but the actor annoyed me, and her next

03:51:36.480 --> 03:51:38.880
remark made me rigid.

03:51:38.880 --> 03:51:41.320
You went so nice to me last night.

03:51:41.320 --> 03:51:48.320
How could it have mattered then, silence for a moment, then, however I want to see you,

03:51:48.320 --> 03:51:50.520
I want to see you too.

03:51:50.520 --> 03:51:53.960
Suppose I don't go to South Hampton and come into town this afternoon.

03:51:53.960 --> 03:51:59.320
No, I don't think this afternoon very well.

03:51:59.320 --> 03:52:05.480
This impossible this afternoon, various, we'd talk like that for a while, and then abruptly

03:52:05.480 --> 03:52:07.800
we weren't talking any longer.

03:52:07.800 --> 03:52:12.160
I don't know which of us hung up with a sharp click, but I know I didn't care.

03:52:12.160 --> 03:52:16.320
I couldn't have talked to her across a tea table that day if I never talked to her again

03:52:16.320 --> 03:52:18.640
in this world.

03:52:18.640 --> 03:52:21.960
I called Gatsby's house a few minutes later, but the line was busy.

03:52:21.960 --> 03:52:24.160
I tried four times.

03:52:24.160 --> 03:52:28.160
Finally an exasperated central told me the wire was being kept open for long distance

03:52:28.160 --> 03:52:29.840
from Detroit.

03:52:29.840 --> 03:52:34.120
Taking out my timetable I drew a small circle around the 350 train.

03:52:34.120 --> 03:52:37.200
Then I leaned back in my chair and tried to think.

03:52:37.200 --> 03:52:41.320
It was just noon.

03:52:41.320 --> 03:52:44.960
When I passed the ash-heaps on the train that morning I had crossed deliberately to the

03:52:44.960 --> 03:52:46.640
other side of the car.

03:52:46.640 --> 03:52:51.600
I supposed there'd be a curious crowd around there all day with little boys searching for

03:52:51.600 --> 03:52:57.000
dark spots in the dust and some garulous man telling over and over what had happened

03:52:57.000 --> 03:53:01.360
until it became less and less real even to him and he could tell it no longer, and

03:53:01.360 --> 03:53:05.320
Mertle Wilson's tragic achievement was forgotten.

03:53:05.320 --> 03:53:09.440
Now I want to go back a little until what happened at the garage after we left there the

03:53:09.440 --> 03:53:11.160
night before.

03:53:11.160 --> 03:53:14.200
They had difficulty in locating the sister, Catherine.

03:53:14.200 --> 03:53:17.440
She must have broken her rule against drinking that night for when she arrived she was

03:53:17.440 --> 03:53:23.360
stupid with liquor and unable to understand that the ambulance had already gone to flushing.

03:53:23.360 --> 03:53:27.280
When they convinced her of this she immediately fainted as if that was the intolerable part

03:53:27.280 --> 03:53:29.080
of the affair.

03:53:29.080 --> 03:53:34.200
Someone kind or curious took her in his car and drove her in the wake of her sister's

03:53:34.200 --> 03:53:35.960
body.

03:53:35.960 --> 03:53:40.800
Until long after midnight a changing crowd lapped up against the front of the garage,

03:53:40.800 --> 03:53:45.760
while George Wilson rocked himself back and forth on the couch inside.

03:53:45.760 --> 03:53:49.800
For a while the door of the office was open and everyone who came into the garage glanced

03:53:49.800 --> 03:53:51.800
irresistibly through it.

03:53:51.800 --> 03:53:55.560
Finally someone said it was a shame and closed the door.

03:53:55.560 --> 03:54:01.080
Mikaelus and several other men were with him, first four or five men, later two or three

03:54:01.080 --> 03:54:02.080
men.

03:54:02.080 --> 03:54:06.040
Still later Mikaelus had to ask the last stranger to wait there fifteen minutes longer

03:54:06.040 --> 03:54:09.640
while he went back to his own place and made a pot of coffee.

03:54:09.640 --> 03:54:14.760
After that he stayed there alone with Wilson until dawn.

03:54:14.760 --> 03:54:19.440
About three o'clock the quality of Wilson's incalherent muttering changed.

03:54:19.680 --> 03:54:23.160
He grew quieter and began to talk about the yellow car.

03:54:23.160 --> 03:54:27.520
He announced that he had a way of finding out whom the yellow car belonged to.

03:54:27.520 --> 03:54:31.200
And then he blurted out that a couple of months ago his wife had come from the city with

03:54:31.200 --> 03:54:32.200
her face.

03:54:32.200 --> 03:54:36.760
When he heard and heard no of this he flinched and began to cry, oh my God again in his

03:54:36.760 --> 03:54:38.240
groaning voice.

03:54:38.240 --> 03:54:41.280
Mikaelus made a clumsy attempt to distract him.

03:54:41.280 --> 03:54:43.280
How long have you been married George?

03:54:43.280 --> 03:54:45.960
Come on there try and sit still a minute and answer my question.

03:54:45.960 --> 03:54:47.880
How long have you been married?

03:54:47.880 --> 03:54:48.880
Twelve years.

03:54:48.960 --> 03:54:50.520
Never had any children?

03:54:50.520 --> 03:54:54.800
Come on George sit still ask you a question did you ever have any children?

03:54:54.800 --> 03:54:59.040
The hard brown beetles kept thudding against the dull light.

03:54:59.040 --> 03:55:03.760
And whenever Mikaelus heard a car go tearing along the road outside it sounded to him like

03:55:03.760 --> 03:55:06.880
the car that hadn't stopped a few hours before.

03:55:06.880 --> 03:55:10.760
He didn't like to go into the garage because the workbench was stained where the body

03:55:10.760 --> 03:55:12.200
had been lying.

03:55:12.200 --> 03:55:14.800
So he moved uncomfortably around the office.

03:55:14.800 --> 03:55:17.800
He knew every object in it before morning.

03:55:17.800 --> 03:55:22.640
And from time to time sat down beside Wilson trying to keep him more quiet.

03:55:22.640 --> 03:55:25.920
If you got a church you go to sometimes George.

03:55:25.920 --> 03:55:29.640
Maybe even if you haven't been there for a long time maybe I could call up the church

03:55:29.640 --> 03:55:32.560
and get a priest to come over and you could talk to you see.

03:55:32.560 --> 03:55:34.400
You don't belong to any.

03:55:34.400 --> 03:55:37.760
You ought to have a church George for times like this.

03:55:37.760 --> 03:55:39.760
You must have gone to church once.

03:55:39.760 --> 03:55:41.600
Didn't you get married in a church?

03:55:41.600 --> 03:55:45.040
Listen George listen to me didn't you get married in a church?

03:55:45.040 --> 03:55:47.120
That was a long time ago.

03:55:47.120 --> 03:55:51.720
The effort of answering broke the rhythm of his rocking for a moment he was silent.

03:55:51.720 --> 03:55:56.520
Then the same half-knowing, half bewildered look came back into his faded eyes.

03:55:56.520 --> 03:56:00.840
Look in the drawer there he said pointing at the desk.

03:56:00.840 --> 03:56:02.480
Which drawer?

03:56:02.480 --> 03:56:04.600
That drawer, that one.

03:56:04.600 --> 03:56:07.280
Mikaelus opened the drawer nearest his hand.

03:56:07.280 --> 03:56:13.000
There was nothing in it but a small expensive dog leash made of leather and braided silver.

03:56:13.000 --> 03:56:14.920
It was apparently new.

03:56:14.920 --> 03:56:17.240
This he inquired holding it up.

03:56:17.240 --> 03:56:18.440
Wilson stared and nodded.

03:56:18.440 --> 03:56:20.920
I found it yesterday afternoon.

03:56:20.920 --> 03:56:24.400
She tried to tell me about it but I knew it was something funny.

03:56:24.400 --> 03:56:26.280
You mean your wife bought it?

03:56:26.280 --> 03:56:30.080
She had it wrapped in tissue paper on her bureau.

03:56:30.080 --> 03:56:34.680
Mikaelus didn't see anything odd in that and he gave Wilson a dozen reasons why his wife

03:56:34.680 --> 03:56:37.120
might have bought the dog leash.

03:56:37.120 --> 03:56:41.560
But conceivably Wilson had heard some of these same explanations before from Mertle because

03:56:41.560 --> 03:56:47.240
he began saying, oh my God, again in a whisper, his comforter left several explanations in

03:56:47.240 --> 03:56:48.320
the air.

03:56:48.320 --> 03:56:51.720
Then he killed her, said Wilson.

03:56:51.720 --> 03:56:53.440
His mouth dropped open suddenly.

03:56:53.440 --> 03:56:54.440
Who did?

03:56:54.440 --> 03:56:57.000
I have a way of finding out.

03:56:57.000 --> 03:56:59.840
You're morbid, George, said his friend.

03:56:59.840 --> 03:57:03.040
This has been a strain to you and you don't know what you're saying.

03:57:03.040 --> 03:57:05.800
You better try and sit quiet till morning.

03:57:05.800 --> 03:57:07.480
He murdered her.

03:57:07.480 --> 03:57:09.640
It wasn't accident, George.

03:57:09.640 --> 03:57:11.160
Wilson shook his head.

03:57:11.160 --> 03:57:16.160
His eyes narrowed and his mouth widened slightly with the ghost of a superior.

03:57:16.160 --> 03:57:19.960
Hmm, I know, he said definitely.

03:57:19.960 --> 03:57:25.000
I'm one of these trusting fellows and I don't think any harm to nobody but when I get to

03:57:25.000 --> 03:57:26.920
know a thing I know it.

03:57:26.920 --> 03:57:29.000
It was the man in that car.

03:57:29.000 --> 03:57:33.120
She ran out to speak to him and he wouldn't stop.

03:57:33.120 --> 03:57:37.920
Mikaelus had seen this too but it hadn't occurred to him that there was any special significance

03:57:37.920 --> 03:57:39.000
in it.

03:57:39.000 --> 03:57:43.560
He believed that Mrs. Wilson had been running away from her husband rather than trying to

03:57:43.560 --> 03:57:46.800
stop any particular car.

03:57:46.800 --> 03:57:53.120
How could she have been like that, said Wilson, as if that answered the question?

03:57:53.120 --> 03:57:57.920
He began to rock again and Mikaelus stood twisting the leash in his hand.

03:57:57.920 --> 03:58:01.480
Maybe you got some friend that I could telephone for, George?

03:58:01.480 --> 03:58:03.320
This was a for-law in hope.

03:58:03.320 --> 03:58:08.360
He was almost sure that Wilson had no friend that was not enough of him for his wife.

03:58:08.360 --> 03:58:12.680
He was glad a little later when he noticed a change in the room of blue quickening by

03:58:12.680 --> 03:58:16.520
the window and realized that Don wasn't far off.

03:58:16.520 --> 03:58:21.000
About five o'clock it was blue enough outside to snap off the light.

03:58:21.000 --> 03:58:26.600
Wilson's glazed eyes turned out to the ash-heaps, where small grey clouds took on fantastic

03:58:26.600 --> 03:58:31.200
shapes and scurried here and there in the faint Don wind.

03:58:31.200 --> 03:58:32.320
I spoke to her.

03:58:32.320 --> 03:58:34.280
He muttered after a long silence.

03:58:34.760 --> 03:58:38.480
I told her she might fool me, but she couldn't fool God.

03:58:38.480 --> 03:58:42.800
I took her to the window, with an effort to go up and walk to the rear window and leaned

03:58:42.800 --> 03:58:44.720
with his face pressed against it.

03:58:44.720 --> 03:58:48.240
And I said, God knows what you've been doing.

03:58:48.240 --> 03:58:52.800
Everything you've been doing, you may fool me, but you can't fool God.

03:58:52.800 --> 03:58:56.880
Standing behind a Mikaelus song with a shock that he was looking at the eyes of Dr. T.J.

03:58:56.880 --> 03:59:03.240
Eckelberg, which had just emerged pale and enormous from the dissolving night.

03:59:03.240 --> 03:59:07.360
And sees everything, repeated Wilson.

03:59:07.360 --> 03:59:11.160
That's an advertisement, Mikaelus assured him.

03:59:11.160 --> 03:59:15.760
Something made him turn away from the window and look back into the room, but Wilson's

03:59:15.760 --> 03:59:24.080
stood there a long time, his face close to the window-pain nodding into the twilight.

03:59:24.080 --> 03:59:29.920
By six o'clock Mikaelus was worn out, and grateful for the sound of a car stopping outside.

03:59:29.920 --> 03:59:33.400
It was one of the watches of the night before who would promise to come back so he cooked

03:59:33.400 --> 03:59:37.440
breakfast for three, which he and the other man ate together.

03:59:37.440 --> 03:59:40.640
Wilson was quieter now and Mikaelus went home to sleep.

03:59:40.640 --> 03:59:46.840
When he awoke four hours later and hurried back to the garage, Wilson was gone.

03:59:46.840 --> 03:59:51.560
His movements, he was on foot all the time, were afterward traced to Port Roosevelt and

03:59:51.560 --> 03:59:56.720
then to Gads Hill, where he bought a sandwich that he didn't eat and a cup of coffee.

03:59:56.720 --> 04:00:01.720
He must have been tired and walking slowly, for he didn't reach Gads Hill until noon.

04:00:01.720 --> 04:00:04.600
Thus far there was no difficulty in accounting for his time.

04:00:04.600 --> 04:00:09.560
There were boys who had seen a man acting sort of crazy and motorists at whom he stared

04:00:09.560 --> 04:00:11.640
oddly from the side of the road.

04:00:11.640 --> 04:00:14.440
Then for three hours he disappeared from view.

04:00:14.440 --> 04:00:18.800
The police on the strength of what he said to Mikaelus that he had had a way of finding

04:00:18.800 --> 04:00:23.400
out, suppose that he spent that time going from garage to garage there about inquiring

04:00:23.400 --> 04:00:25.240
for a yellow car.

04:00:25.240 --> 04:00:29.040
On the other hand, no garage man who had seen him ever came forward and perhaps he had

04:00:29.040 --> 04:00:33.000
an easier, sure way of finding out what he wanted to know.

04:00:33.000 --> 04:00:38.280
By half past two he was in West Egg where he asked someone the way to Gatsby's house.

04:00:38.280 --> 04:00:41.720
So by that time he knew Gatsby's name.

04:00:41.720 --> 04:00:46.160
At two o'clock Gatsby put on his bathing suit and left word with the butler that if anyone

04:00:46.160 --> 04:00:49.280
phoned word was to be brought to him at the pool.

04:00:49.280 --> 04:00:54.160
He stopped at the garage for a pneumatic mattress that had amused his guests during the summer,

04:00:54.160 --> 04:00:56.360
and the chauffeur helped him pump it up.

04:00:56.360 --> 04:01:00.600
Then he gave instructions that the open car wasn't to be taken out under any circumstances

04:01:00.600 --> 04:01:05.360
and this was strange because the front shoulder and the mattresses and started for the pool.

04:01:05.360 --> 04:01:09.160
Once he stopped and shifted it a little and the chauffeur asked him if he needed help

04:01:09.160 --> 04:01:13.600
but he shook his head and in a moment disappeared among the yellowing trees.

04:01:13.600 --> 04:01:18.280
No telephone message arrived but the butler went without his sleep and waited for it until

04:01:18.280 --> 04:01:23.840
four o'clock, until long after there was anyone to give it to if it came.

04:01:23.840 --> 04:01:28.480
I have an idea that Gatsby himself didn't believe it would come when perhaps he no longer

04:01:28.480 --> 04:01:29.480
cared.

04:01:29.480 --> 04:01:34.080
If that was true he must have felt that he had lost the old warm world.

04:01:34.080 --> 04:01:38.120
Paid a high price for living too long with a single dream.

04:01:38.120 --> 04:01:42.600
He must have looked up at an unfamiliar sky through frightening leaves and shivered as

04:01:42.600 --> 04:01:48.600
he found what a grotesque thing arose is and how raw the sunlight was upon the scarcely

04:01:48.600 --> 04:01:55.800
created grass, a new world, material without being real, where poor ghosts breathing dreams

04:01:55.800 --> 04:02:01.680
like air drifted fortuitously about, like that ash in fantastic figure gliding toward

04:02:01.680 --> 04:02:05.280
him through the amorphous trees.

04:02:05.280 --> 04:02:10.840
The chauffeur, he was one of Wolfshimes' protégés, heard the shots.

04:02:10.840 --> 04:02:15.080
Afterward he could only say that he hadn't thought anything much about them.

04:02:15.080 --> 04:02:20.400
I drove from the station directly to Gatsby's house and my rushing anxiously up the front

04:02:20.400 --> 04:02:23.920
steps was the first thing that alarmed anyone.

04:02:23.920 --> 04:02:29.960
But they knew then I firmly believe, with scarcely a word said four of us, the chauffeur,

04:02:29.960 --> 04:02:35.000
butler, gardener, and I, hurried down to the pool.

04:02:35.000 --> 04:02:40.360
There was a faint, barely perceptible movement of the water as the fresh flow from one end

04:02:40.360 --> 04:02:43.720
urged its weight or the drain at the other.

04:02:43.720 --> 04:02:49.960
With little ripples that were hardly the shadows of waves, the laden mattress moved irregularly

04:02:49.960 --> 04:02:51.720
down the pool.

04:02:51.720 --> 04:02:57.800
A small gust of wind that scarcely corrugated the surface was enough to disturb its accidental

04:02:57.800 --> 04:03:00.760
course with its accidental burden.

04:03:00.760 --> 04:03:06.800
The touch of a cluster of leaves revolved it slowly, tracing like the leg of transit, a

04:03:06.800 --> 04:03:10.320
thin red circle in the water.

04:03:10.320 --> 04:03:15.080
It was after we started with Gatsby toward the house that the gardeners saw Wilson's

04:03:15.080 --> 04:03:21.520
body, a little way off in the grass, and the Holocaust was complete.

04:03:21.520 --> 04:03:24.320
CHAPTER NINE

04:03:24.320 --> 04:03:30.040
After two years I remember the rest of that day and that night and the next day only

04:03:30.040 --> 04:03:36.040
as an endless drill of police and photographers and newspaper men in and out of Gatsby's

04:03:36.040 --> 04:03:37.960
front door.

04:03:37.960 --> 04:03:43.200
A rope stretched across the main gates and a policeman by it kept out the curious,

04:03:43.200 --> 04:03:46.880
but little boys soon discovered that they could enter through my yard and there were

04:03:46.880 --> 04:03:51.760
always a few of them clustered, open-mouthed about the pool.

04:03:51.760 --> 04:03:56.600
Someone with a positive manner, perhaps a detective, used the expression madman as he

04:03:56.600 --> 04:04:02.280
bent over Wilson's body that afternoon and the adventitious authority of his voice set

04:04:02.280 --> 04:04:06.600
the key for the newspaper reports next morning.

04:04:06.600 --> 04:04:12.720
Most of these reports were a nightmare, grotesque, circumstantial, eager and untrue.

04:04:12.720 --> 04:04:17.280
When Macaillus's testimony at the inquest brought to light Wilson's suspicions of his wife,

04:04:17.280 --> 04:04:22.240
I thought the whole tale would shortly be served up in racy Pasquanade.

04:04:22.240 --> 04:04:25.480
But Catherine, who might have said anything, didn't say a word.

04:04:25.480 --> 04:04:28.680
She showed a surprising amount of character about it, too.

04:04:28.680 --> 04:04:33.360
Looked at the coroner with determined eyes under that corrected brow of hers and swore

04:04:33.360 --> 04:04:37.520
that her sister had never seen Gatsby, that her sister was completely happy with her

04:04:37.520 --> 04:04:41.760
husband, that her sister had been into no mischief, whatever.

04:04:41.760 --> 04:04:46.360
She convinced herself of it and cried into her handkerchief as if the very suggestion

04:04:46.360 --> 04:04:48.680
was more than she could endure.

04:04:48.680 --> 04:04:54.400
So Wilson was reduced to a man, deranged by grief, in order that the case might remain

04:04:54.400 --> 04:04:59.200
in its simplest form, and it rested there.

04:04:59.200 --> 04:05:02.920
But all this part of its scene remote and uneasensual.

04:05:02.920 --> 04:05:07.040
I found myself on Gatsby's side and alone.

04:05:07.040 --> 04:05:11.520
From the moment I tell a phone news of the catastrophe to West Egg every surmise about

04:05:11.520 --> 04:05:15.400
him and every practical question was referred to me.

04:05:15.400 --> 04:05:18.760
At first I was surprised and confused.

04:05:18.760 --> 04:05:24.920
Then as he lay in his house and didn't move or breathe or speak hour upon hour, it

04:05:24.920 --> 04:05:30.560
grew upon me that I was responsible because no one else was interested.

04:05:30.560 --> 04:05:35.920
Should I mean with that intense personal interest to which everyone has some vague right at

04:05:35.920 --> 04:05:36.920
the end?

04:05:36.920 --> 04:05:42.800
I called up Daisy, half an hour after we found him, called her instinctively and without

04:05:42.800 --> 04:05:44.400
hesitation.

04:05:44.400 --> 04:05:48.600
But she and Tom had gone away early than afternoon and taken baggage with them.

04:05:48.600 --> 04:05:50.880
Left no address?

04:05:50.880 --> 04:05:51.880
No.

04:05:51.880 --> 04:05:53.400
Say when they'd be back?

04:05:53.400 --> 04:05:54.400
No.

04:05:54.400 --> 04:05:57.600
Any idea where they are, how I could reach them.

04:05:57.600 --> 04:06:00.120
I don't know, can't say.

04:06:00.120 --> 04:06:02.040
I wanted to get somebody for him.

04:06:02.040 --> 04:06:06.320
I wanted to go into the room where he lay and reassure him, I'll get somebody for you,

04:06:06.320 --> 04:06:07.320
Gatsby.

04:06:07.320 --> 04:06:10.240
Don't worry, just trust me and I'll get somebody for you.

04:06:10.240 --> 04:06:13.200
My old Wolfshime's name wasn't in the phone book.

04:06:13.200 --> 04:06:17.200
The butler gave me his office address on Broadway and I called information, but by the

04:06:17.200 --> 04:06:20.200
time I had the number it was long after five and no one answered.

04:06:20.200 --> 04:06:22.320
I've run them three times.

04:06:22.320 --> 04:06:23.840
It's very important.

04:06:23.840 --> 04:06:26.560
Sorry, I'm afraid no one's there.

04:06:26.560 --> 04:06:30.760
I went back to the drawing room and thought for an instant that they were chance visitors

04:06:30.760 --> 04:06:34.080
all these official people who suddenly filled it.

04:06:34.080 --> 04:06:39.080
But as they drew back the sheet and looked at Gatsby with unmoved eyes, his protest continued

04:06:39.080 --> 04:06:40.080
in my brain.

04:06:40.080 --> 04:06:43.160
Look here, old sport, you've got to get somebody for me.

04:06:43.160 --> 04:06:46.640
You've got to try hard, I can't go through this alone.

04:06:46.640 --> 04:06:50.800
Someone started to ask me questions, but I broke away and going upstairs looked hastily

04:06:50.800 --> 04:06:53.040
through the unlocked parts of his desk.

04:06:53.040 --> 04:06:56.160
He'd never told me definitely that his parents were dead.

04:06:56.160 --> 04:07:01.080
But there was nothing, only the picture of Dan Cody, a token of forgotten violence staring

04:07:01.080 --> 04:07:03.560
down from the wall.

04:07:03.560 --> 04:07:07.560
Next morning I sent the butler to New York with a letter to Wolfshime, which asked for

04:07:07.560 --> 04:07:11.440
information and urged him to come out on the next train.

04:07:11.440 --> 04:07:13.720
That request seemed superfluous when I wrote it.

04:07:13.720 --> 04:07:17.840
I was sure he'd start when he saw the newspapers just as I was sure there'd be a wire from

04:07:17.840 --> 04:07:19.840
Daisy before noon.

04:07:19.840 --> 04:07:23.080
But neither a wire nor Mr. Wolfshime arrived.

04:07:23.080 --> 04:07:28.520
No one arrived, except more police and photographers and newspapermen.

04:07:28.520 --> 04:07:33.480
When the butler brought back Wolfshime's answer, I began to have a feeling of defiance, of

04:07:33.480 --> 04:07:38.640
scornful solidarity between Gatsby and me against them all.

04:07:38.640 --> 04:07:44.040
Dear Mr. Carrey, this has been one of the most terrible shocks of my life to me I can

04:07:44.040 --> 04:07:46.800
hardly believe that it is true at all.

04:07:46.800 --> 04:07:50.400
Such a mad act as that man did should make us all think.

04:07:50.400 --> 04:07:55.160
I cannot come down now as I am tied up some very important business and cannot get mixed

04:07:55.160 --> 04:07:56.960
up in this thing now.

04:07:56.960 --> 04:08:00.920
If there is anything I can do a little later, let me know in a letter by Edgar.

04:08:00.920 --> 04:08:05.440
I hardly know where I am when I hear about a thing like this and I'm completely knocked

04:08:05.440 --> 04:08:09.480
down and out, yours truly my of Wolfshime.

04:08:09.480 --> 04:08:11.600
And then hasty addenda beneath.

04:08:11.600 --> 04:08:17.080
Let me know about the funeral, etc. do not know his family at all.

04:08:17.080 --> 04:08:21.000
And the phone rang that afternoon and long distance said Chicago was calling.

04:08:21.000 --> 04:08:23.480
I thought this would be daisy at last.

04:08:23.480 --> 04:08:28.320
But the connection came through as a man's voice very thin and far away.

04:08:28.320 --> 04:08:30.160
This is slaggle speaking.

04:08:30.160 --> 04:08:32.360
Yes, the name was unfamiliar.

04:08:32.360 --> 04:08:33.360
How of a note, isn't it?

04:08:33.360 --> 04:08:34.360
Get my wire?

04:08:34.360 --> 04:08:36.920
There haven't been any wires.

04:08:36.920 --> 04:08:39.000
Young parks and trouble he stood rapidly.

04:08:39.000 --> 04:08:41.080
They picked him up when he handed the bonds over the counter.

04:08:41.080 --> 04:08:44.480
They got a circular from New York giving him the numbers just five minutes before.

04:08:44.480 --> 04:08:45.480
What do you know about that?

04:08:45.480 --> 04:08:47.400
Hey, you can never tell on these hick towns.

04:08:47.400 --> 04:08:49.680
Hello, I interrupted breathlessly.

04:08:49.680 --> 04:08:50.840
Look here.

04:08:50.840 --> 04:08:52.640
This isn't Mr. Gatsby.

04:08:52.640 --> 04:08:54.760
Mr. Gatsby's dead.

04:08:54.760 --> 04:08:59.360
There was a long silence on the other end of the wire, followed by an exclamation.

04:08:59.360 --> 04:09:02.160
Then a quick squawk as the connection was broken.

04:09:02.160 --> 04:09:08.160
I think it was on the third day that a telegram signed Henry C. Gats arrived from a town

04:09:08.160 --> 04:09:09.680
in Minnesota.

04:09:09.680 --> 04:09:15.360
He said only that the sender was leaving immediately and to postpone the funeral until he came.

04:09:15.360 --> 04:09:23.360
It was Gatsby's father, a solemn old man, very helpless and as made, bundled up in a long,

04:09:23.360 --> 04:09:26.960
cheap oster against the warm September day.

04:09:26.960 --> 04:09:29.920
His eyes leaked continuously with excitement.

04:09:29.920 --> 04:09:34.800
When I took the bag and umbrella from his hands, he began to pull so incessantly at his

04:09:34.800 --> 04:09:39.800
bar-scraved beard that I had difficulty in getting off his coat.

04:09:39.800 --> 04:09:44.360
He was on the point of collapse, so I took him into the music room and made him sit down

04:09:44.360 --> 04:09:46.920
while I sent for something to eat.

04:09:46.920 --> 04:09:52.480
But he wouldn't eat, and the glass of milk spilled from his trembling hand.

04:09:52.480 --> 04:09:55.840
I saw it in the Chicago newspaper, he said.

04:09:55.840 --> 04:09:58.040
It was all in the Chicago newspaper.

04:09:58.040 --> 04:10:00.200
I started right away.

04:10:00.200 --> 04:10:02.280
I didn't know how to reach you.

04:10:02.280 --> 04:10:06.280
His eyes, seeing nothing, moved ceaselessly about the room.

04:10:06.280 --> 04:10:09.160
It was a mad man, he said.

04:10:09.160 --> 04:10:10.720
He must have been mad.

04:10:10.720 --> 04:10:13.680
Wouldn't you like some coffee, I urged him?

04:10:13.680 --> 04:10:14.680
I don't want anything.

04:10:14.680 --> 04:10:16.840
I'm all right now, Mr. Eard.

04:10:16.840 --> 04:10:17.840
Carroway.

04:10:17.840 --> 04:10:19.440
Well, I'm all right now.

04:10:19.440 --> 04:10:21.840
Where have they got, Jimmy?

04:10:21.840 --> 04:10:26.240
I took him into the drawing room where his son lay and left him there.

04:10:26.240 --> 04:10:29.440
Some little boys had come up on the steps and were looking into the hall.

04:10:29.440 --> 04:10:34.400
When I told them who had arrived, they went reluctantly away.

04:10:34.400 --> 04:10:37.360
After a little while, Mr. Gatz opened the door and came out.

04:10:37.360 --> 04:10:40.360
His mouth, a jar, his face flushed slightly.

04:10:40.360 --> 04:10:44.520
His eyes leaking, isolated, and unpunctual tears.

04:10:44.520 --> 04:10:50.240
He had reached an age where death no longer has the quality of ghastly surprise.

04:10:50.240 --> 04:10:54.600
And when he looked around him now for the first time and saw the height and splendor of

04:10:54.600 --> 04:11:00.960
the hall and the great rooms opening out from it into other rooms, his grief began to

04:11:00.960 --> 04:11:03.840
be mixed with an odd pride.

04:11:03.840 --> 04:11:05.560
I helped him to a bedroom upstairs.

04:11:05.560 --> 04:11:09.520
While he took off his coat and vest, I told him that all arrangements had been deferred

04:11:09.520 --> 04:11:11.560
until he came.

04:11:11.560 --> 04:11:15.040
I didn't know what you'd want, Mr. Gatzby.

04:11:15.040 --> 04:11:16.600
Gatz is my name.

04:11:16.600 --> 04:11:20.320
Mr. Gatz, I thought you might want to take the body west.

04:11:20.320 --> 04:11:21.800
He shook his head.

04:11:21.800 --> 04:11:24.480
Jimmy always liked it better down east.

04:11:24.480 --> 04:11:26.760
He rose up to his position in the east.

04:11:26.760 --> 04:11:31.600
Were you a friend of my boys, Mr. We were close friends.

04:11:31.600 --> 04:11:34.320
He had a big future before him, you know.

04:11:34.320 --> 04:11:38.600
He was only a young man, but he had a lot of brain power here.

04:11:38.600 --> 04:11:41.640
He touched his head impressively and I nodded.

04:11:41.640 --> 04:11:44.480
If he'd have lived, he'd have been a great man.

04:11:44.480 --> 04:11:49.400
A man like James J. Hill, he'd have helped build up the country.

04:11:49.400 --> 04:11:52.560
That's true, I said, uncomfortably.

04:11:52.560 --> 04:11:57.880
We fumbled that the embroidered coverlet, trying to take it from the bed and lay down stiffly,

04:11:57.880 --> 04:12:00.520
was instantly asleep.

04:12:00.520 --> 04:12:04.680
That night an obviously frightened person called up and demanded to know who I was before

04:12:04.680 --> 04:12:06.360
he would give his name.

04:12:06.360 --> 04:12:08.440
This is Mr. Carroway, I said.

04:12:08.440 --> 04:12:10.680
Oh, he sounded relieved.

04:12:10.680 --> 04:12:12.400
This is Clip Springer.

04:12:12.400 --> 04:12:16.360
I was relieved too for that scene to promise another friend at Gatzby's grave.

04:12:16.360 --> 04:12:21.080
I didn't want it to be in the papers and draw sightseeing crowds, so I'd been calling

04:12:21.080 --> 04:12:23.160
up a few people myself.

04:12:23.160 --> 04:12:25.200
They were hard to find.

04:12:25.200 --> 04:12:27.920
The funeral's tomorrow, I said, three o'clock here at the house.

04:12:27.920 --> 04:12:30.000
I wish you'd tell anybody who'd be interested.

04:12:30.000 --> 04:12:33.120
Oh, I will, he broke out hastily.

04:12:33.120 --> 04:12:38.840
Of course, I'm not likely to see anybody, but if I do, his tone made me suspicious.

04:12:38.840 --> 04:12:40.520
Of course you'll be there yourself.

04:12:40.520 --> 04:12:43.080
Well, I'll certainly try.

04:12:43.080 --> 04:12:45.880
What I called up about is, wait a minute, I interrupted.

04:12:45.880 --> 04:12:47.360
How about saying you'll come?

04:12:48.000 --> 04:12:52.040
Well, the fact is, the truth of the matter is that I'm staying with some people up here

04:12:52.040 --> 04:12:55.920
in Greenwich and they'd rather expect me to be with them tomorrow.

04:12:55.920 --> 04:12:57.600
In fact, there's a sort of picnic or something.

04:12:57.600 --> 04:13:00.240
Of course, I'll do my very best to get away.

04:13:00.240 --> 04:13:03.960
I had jackulated in an unrestrained, ha!

04:13:03.960 --> 04:13:06.320
And you must have heard me very, very unnervously.

04:13:06.320 --> 04:13:08.640
What I called up about was a pair of shoes I left there.

04:13:08.640 --> 04:13:12.000
I wonder if it'd be too much trouble to have the butler send them on.

04:13:12.000 --> 04:13:15.240
You see, they're tennis shoes and I'm sort of helpless without them.

04:13:15.240 --> 04:13:21.120
My address is Car of B. F. I didn't hear the rest of the name because I hung up the receiver.

04:13:21.120 --> 04:13:26.080
After that I felt a certain shame for Gatsby, one gentleman to whom I telephoned imply

04:13:26.080 --> 04:13:28.000
that he had got what he deserved.

04:13:28.000 --> 04:13:32.280
However that was my fault, for he was one of those who used to sneer most bitterly at Gatsby

04:13:32.280 --> 04:13:35.720
on the courage of Gatsby's liquor, and I showed him morning that the funeral I went

04:13:35.720 --> 04:13:38.240
up to New York to see my old wolfshime.

04:13:38.240 --> 04:13:40.680
I couldn't seem to reach him any other way.

04:13:40.680 --> 04:13:44.880
The door that I pushed open on the advice of an elevator boy was marked the swastika

04:13:44.880 --> 04:13:49.600
holding company, and at first they didn't seem to be anyone inside.

04:13:49.600 --> 04:13:55.400
But when I shouted hello several times in vain, an argument broke out behind a partition,

04:13:55.400 --> 04:14:00.720
and presently a lovely Jewish appeared at an interior door and scrutinized me with black

04:14:00.720 --> 04:14:03.000
hostile eyes.

04:14:03.000 --> 04:14:04.280
Nobody's in, she said.

04:14:04.280 --> 04:14:06.960
Mr. Wolfshime's gone to Chicago.

04:14:06.960 --> 04:14:11.360
The first part of this was obviously untrue for someone had begun to whistle the rosary

04:14:11.360 --> 04:14:13.680
tunelessly inside.

04:14:13.680 --> 04:14:16.200
Please say that Mr. Carroway wants to see him.

04:14:16.200 --> 04:14:19.000
I can't get him back from Chicago, can I?

04:14:19.000 --> 04:14:25.040
At this moment a voice unmistakably wolfshimes called Stella from the other side of the door.

04:14:25.040 --> 04:14:28.360
Leave your name on the desk, she said quickly, I'll give it to him when he gets back.

04:14:28.360 --> 04:14:30.360
But I know he's there.

04:14:30.360 --> 04:14:35.840
She took a step toward me and began to slide her hands indignantly up and down her hips.

04:14:35.840 --> 04:14:39.520
Two young men think you can force your way in here any time she scolded.

04:14:39.520 --> 04:14:41.280
We're getting sick and tired of it.

04:14:41.280 --> 04:14:43.800
When I say he's in Chicago, he's in Chicago.

04:14:43.800 --> 04:14:45.600
I mentioned Gatsby.

04:14:45.600 --> 04:14:48.520
Oh, she looked at me over again.

04:14:48.520 --> 04:14:51.320
Will you just, what was your name?

04:14:51.320 --> 04:14:52.400
She vanished.

04:14:52.400 --> 04:14:57.600
In a moment Maya Wolfshime stood solemnly in the doorway holding out both hands.

04:14:57.600 --> 04:15:02.000
He drew me into his office, remarking in a reverent voice that it was a sad time for

04:15:02.000 --> 04:15:05.360
us all and offered me a cigar.

04:15:05.360 --> 04:15:08.920
My memory goes back to when I first met him, he said.

04:15:08.920 --> 04:15:13.960
A young major just out of the army and covered over with metals he got in the war.

04:15:13.960 --> 04:15:18.720
He was so hard up he had to keep on wearing his uniform because he couldn't buy some regular

04:15:18.720 --> 04:15:19.720
clothes.

04:15:19.720 --> 04:15:24.320
First time I saw him was when he come into Vine Brenners Pool Room at 43rd Street and

04:15:24.320 --> 04:15:25.880
asked for a job.

04:15:25.880 --> 04:15:27.720
He hadn't eaten anything for a couple of days.

04:15:27.720 --> 04:15:30.040
Come on have some lunch with me, I said.

04:15:30.040 --> 04:15:33.560
He ate more than four dollars with a food and half an hour.

04:15:33.560 --> 04:15:35.120
Did you start him in business?

04:15:35.120 --> 04:15:36.120
I inquired.

04:15:36.120 --> 04:15:38.280
Start him, I made him.

04:15:38.280 --> 04:15:42.560
Oh, I raised him up part of nothing right out of the gutter.

04:15:42.560 --> 04:15:46.920
I saw him at a way he was a fine appearing gentlemanly young man and when he told me he

04:15:46.920 --> 04:15:49.960
was an Oxford I knew I could use him good.

04:15:49.960 --> 04:15:55.120
I got him to join up in the American Legion and he used to stand high there.

04:15:55.120 --> 04:15:57.800
Right off he did some work for a client of mine up to Albany.

04:15:57.800 --> 04:16:00.320
We were so sick like that in everything.

04:16:00.320 --> 04:16:03.680
He held up two bulbous fingers, always together.

04:16:03.680 --> 04:16:09.360
I wondered if this partnership had included the World Series transaction in 1919.

04:16:09.360 --> 04:16:12.280
Now he's dead, I said after a moment.

04:16:12.280 --> 04:16:17.720
You were his closest friend, so I know you'll want to come to his funeral this afternoon.

04:16:17.720 --> 04:16:19.720
I'd like to come.

04:16:19.720 --> 04:16:21.440
Well come then.

04:16:21.440 --> 04:16:26.360
The hair in his nostrils quivered slightly and as he shook his head his eyes filled with

04:16:26.360 --> 04:16:27.860
tears.

04:16:27.860 --> 04:16:29.360
I can't do it.

04:16:29.360 --> 04:16:32.280
I can't get mixed up in it, he said.

04:16:32.280 --> 04:16:36.080
There's nothing to get mixed up in, it's all over now.

04:16:36.080 --> 04:16:41.800
When a man gets killed I never like to get mixed up in it in any way I keep out.

04:16:41.800 --> 04:16:46.120
When I was a young man it was different if a friend of mine died no matter how I stuck

04:16:46.120 --> 04:16:48.040
with them to the end.

04:16:48.040 --> 04:16:50.200
You may think that's sentimental but I mean it.

04:16:50.200 --> 04:16:53.680
He was determined not to come so I stood up.

04:16:53.680 --> 04:16:56.200
Are you a college man?

04:16:56.200 --> 04:16:57.840
He inquired suddenly.

04:16:57.840 --> 04:17:01.760
For a moment I thought it was going to suggest a connection but he only nodded and shook

04:17:01.760 --> 04:17:02.760
my hand.

04:17:02.760 --> 04:17:09.320
Let us learn to show our friendship for a man when he is alive and not after he is dead

04:17:09.320 --> 04:17:11.040
he suggested.

04:17:11.040 --> 04:17:15.080
After that my own rule is to let everything alone.

04:17:15.080 --> 04:17:21.000
When I left his office the sky had turned dark and I got back to West Egg in a drizzle.

04:17:21.000 --> 04:17:25.480
After changing my clothes I went next door and found Mr. Gats walking up and down excitedly

04:17:25.480 --> 04:17:26.680
in the hall.

04:17:26.680 --> 04:17:31.560
His pride in his son and in his son's possessions was continually increasing.

04:17:31.560 --> 04:17:33.280
Now he had something to show me.

04:17:33.280 --> 04:17:35.080
Jimmy sent me this picture.

04:17:35.080 --> 04:17:37.240
He took out his wallet with trembling fingers.

04:17:37.240 --> 04:17:38.600
Look there.

04:17:38.600 --> 04:17:43.520
It was a photograph of the house cracked in the corners and dirty with many hands.

04:17:43.520 --> 04:17:46.160
He pointed out every detail to me eagerly.

04:17:46.160 --> 04:17:49.960
Look there and then sought admiration for my eyes.

04:17:49.960 --> 04:17:54.400
He had shown it so often that I think it was more real to him now than the house itself.

04:17:54.400 --> 04:17:56.000
Jimmy sent it to me.

04:17:56.000 --> 04:17:57.880
I think it's a very pretty picture.

04:17:57.880 --> 04:18:00.000
It shows up well.

04:18:00.000 --> 04:18:01.000
Very well.

04:18:01.000 --> 04:18:02.640
Had you seen him lately?

04:18:02.640 --> 04:18:07.200
He come out to see me two years ago and bought me the house I live in now.

04:18:07.200 --> 04:18:11.080
Of course we would broke up when he run off from home, but I see now there was a reason

04:18:11.080 --> 04:18:12.240
for it.

04:18:12.240 --> 04:18:17.000
He knew he had a big future in front of him, and ever since he made a success he was very

04:18:17.000 --> 04:18:18.840
generous with me.

04:18:18.840 --> 04:18:23.000
He seemed reluctant to put away the picture, held it for another minute lingeringly before

04:18:23.000 --> 04:18:24.000
my eyes.

04:18:24.000 --> 04:18:28.960
Then he returned the wallet and pulled from his pocket a ragged old copy of a book called

04:18:28.960 --> 04:18:31.560
Hoppelong Cassidy.

04:18:31.560 --> 04:18:32.560
Look here.

04:18:32.560 --> 04:18:33.880
This is a book he had when he was a boy.

04:18:33.880 --> 04:18:35.920
It just shows you.

04:18:35.920 --> 04:18:39.880
He opened it at the back cover and turned it around for me to see.

04:18:39.880 --> 04:18:46.240
On the last fly leaf was printed the word schedule and the date September 12, 1906.

04:18:46.240 --> 04:18:50.560
An underneath rise from bed, six o'clock a.m.

04:18:50.560 --> 04:18:55.160
Some bell exercise and wall scaling, 615 to 630.

04:18:55.160 --> 04:18:56.840
Study electricity, etc.

04:18:56.840 --> 04:18:59.200
715 to 815.

04:18:59.200 --> 04:19:02.480
Work 830 a.m. to 430 p.m.

04:19:02.480 --> 04:19:06.240
Baseball and sports, 430 p.m. to 5 o'clock.

04:19:06.240 --> 04:19:10.800
Practice allocation, poise and how to attain it, 5 o'clock to 6 o'clock.

04:19:10.800 --> 04:19:15.160
Study needed inventions, 7 o'clock to 9 o'clock.

04:19:15.160 --> 04:19:16.560
General resolves.

04:19:16.560 --> 04:19:20.920
No wasting time at Shafters or a name indecisurable.

04:19:20.920 --> 04:19:23.480
No more smoking or chewing.

04:19:23.480 --> 04:19:25.240
Bath every other day.

04:19:25.240 --> 04:19:28.440
Read one improving book or magazine per week.

04:19:28.440 --> 04:19:32.240
Save five dollars, crossed out three dollars per week.

04:19:32.240 --> 04:19:34.400
Be better to parents.

04:19:34.400 --> 04:19:37.400
I come across this book by accident, so the old man.

04:19:37.400 --> 04:19:39.880
It just shows you, don't it?

04:19:39.880 --> 04:19:41.400
It just shows you.

04:19:41.400 --> 04:19:43.400
Jimmy was bound to get ahead.

04:19:43.400 --> 04:19:46.360
He always had some resolves like this or something.

04:19:46.360 --> 04:19:48.800
Do you notice what he's got about improving his mind?

04:19:48.800 --> 04:19:50.520
He was always great for that.

04:19:50.520 --> 04:19:54.360
He told me I ate like a hog once and I beat him for it.

04:19:54.360 --> 04:19:58.480
He was reluctant to close the book, reading each item aloud and then looking eagerly

04:19:58.480 --> 04:19:59.480
at me.

04:19:59.480 --> 04:20:04.040
I think he rather expected me to copy down the list for my own use.

04:20:04.040 --> 04:20:09.000
A little before three, the Lutheran minister arrived from flushing, and I began to look

04:20:09.000 --> 04:20:12.040
involuntarily out the windows for other corners.

04:20:12.040 --> 04:20:14.000
So did Gatsby's father.

04:20:14.000 --> 04:20:19.120
And as the time passed and the servants came in and stood waiting in the hall, his eyes

04:20:19.120 --> 04:20:20.920
began to blink anxiously.

04:20:20.920 --> 04:20:24.720
And he spoke of the rain in a worried uncertain way.

04:20:24.720 --> 04:20:29.040
The minister glanced several times at his watch, so I took him aside and asked him to

04:20:29.040 --> 04:20:31.240
wait for half an hour.

04:20:31.240 --> 04:20:32.920
But it wasn't any use.

04:20:32.920 --> 04:20:37.320
About five o'clock on procession of three cars reached the cemetery and stopped in a thick

04:20:37.320 --> 04:20:39.560
drizzle beside the gate.

04:20:39.560 --> 04:20:43.240
First a motor, herse, horribly black and wet.

04:20:43.240 --> 04:20:47.960
And Mr. Gats and the minister and I in the limousine, and a little later four or five

04:20:47.960 --> 04:20:53.960
servants and the postman from Westeg in Gatsby's station wagon all wet to the skin.

04:20:53.960 --> 04:20:58.520
As we started through the gate into the cemetery, I heard a car stop and then the sound of someone

04:20:58.520 --> 04:21:01.880
splashing after us over the soggy ground.

04:21:01.880 --> 04:21:03.480
I looked around.

04:21:03.480 --> 04:21:08.480
It was the man with our lied glasses whom I had found marvelling over Gatsby's books in

04:21:08.480 --> 04:21:12.040
the library one night three months before.

04:21:12.040 --> 04:21:14.280
I'd never seen him since then.

04:21:14.280 --> 04:21:18.120
I don't know how he knew about the funeral or even his name.

04:21:18.120 --> 04:21:22.560
The rain poured down his thick glasses and he took them off and wiped them to see the

04:21:22.560 --> 04:21:26.400
protecting canvas unrolled from Gatsby's grave.

04:21:26.400 --> 04:21:31.720
I tried to think about Gatsby then for a moment, but he was already too far away.

04:21:31.720 --> 04:21:36.960
And I could only remember without resentment the daisy hadn't sent a message or a flower.

04:21:36.960 --> 04:21:42.360
Dimly I heard someone murmur blessed, either dead that the rain falls on.

04:21:42.360 --> 04:21:46.720
And then the allied man said, I'm end to that in a brave voice.

04:21:46.720 --> 04:21:50.360
We straggled down quickly through the rain to the cars.

04:21:50.360 --> 04:21:52.480
Our lives spoke to me by the gate.

04:21:52.480 --> 04:21:55.360
I couldn't get to the house, he remarked.

04:21:55.360 --> 04:21:57.000
Neither could anybody else.

04:21:57.000 --> 04:21:58.680
Go on, he started.

04:21:58.680 --> 04:22:02.760
Why, my God, they used to go there by the hundreds.

04:22:02.760 --> 04:22:06.880
He took off his glasses and wiped them again, outside and in.

04:22:06.880 --> 04:22:15.040
The poor son of a bitch, he said.

04:22:15.040 --> 04:22:20.320
One of my most vivid memories is of coming back west from prep school and later from college

04:22:20.320 --> 04:22:22.360
at Christmas time.

04:22:22.360 --> 04:22:26.640
Those who went farther than Chicago would gather in the old Dimm Union station at six o'clock

04:22:26.640 --> 04:22:31.680
of a December evening with a few Chicago friends, already caught up into their own holiday

04:22:31.680 --> 04:22:34.800
gateies to bid them a hasty goodbye.

04:22:34.800 --> 04:22:39.680
I remember the fur coats of the girls returning from Miss This-O-That's, and the chatter of

04:22:39.680 --> 04:22:44.840
frozen breaths and the hands waving overhead as we caught sight of all acquaintances.

04:22:44.840 --> 04:22:50.000
And the matchings of invitations are you going to the Ordways, the Herses, the Schultzys,

04:22:50.000 --> 04:22:55.480
and the long green tickets clasped tight in our gloved hands, and last the murky yellow

04:22:55.480 --> 04:23:00.880
cars of the Chicago-Mill-Walky and St. Paul Railroad looking cheerful as Christmas itself

04:23:00.880 --> 04:23:03.880
on the tracks beside the gate.

04:23:03.880 --> 04:23:08.880
When we pulled out into the winter night and the real snow, our snow began to stretch out

04:23:08.880 --> 04:23:14.000
beside us and twinkle against the windows and the dim lights of small Wisconsin stations

04:23:14.000 --> 04:23:18.880
moved by a sharp wild brace came suddenly into the air.

04:23:18.880 --> 04:23:24.000
We drew in deep breaths of it as we walked back from dinner through the cold of estibules,

04:23:24.000 --> 04:23:29.280
unutterably aware of our identity with this country for one strange hour before we melted

04:23:29.280 --> 04:23:32.000
into sunshine with a little west.

04:23:32.000 --> 04:23:38.640
Not the weets or the prairies or the lost swedetowns, but the thrilling returning trains of

04:23:38.640 --> 04:23:44.260
my youth and the street lamps and sleigh bells and the frosty dark and the shadows of

04:23:44.260 --> 04:23:48.280
holly wreaths thrown by lighted windows on the snow.

04:23:48.280 --> 04:23:54.240
I am part of that, a little solemn with the feel of those long winters, a little complacent

04:23:54.240 --> 04:23:58.320
from growing up in the Caraway House in a city where dwellings are still called through

04:23:58.400 --> 04:24:01.240
decades by a family's name.

04:24:01.240 --> 04:24:05.360
I see now that this has been a story of the West after all.

04:24:05.360 --> 04:24:11.800
Tom and Gatsby, Daisy and Jordan and I were all Westerners, and perhaps we possessed

04:24:11.800 --> 04:24:19.120
some deficiency in common, which made us subtly unadaptable to Eastern life.

04:24:19.120 --> 04:24:23.720
Even when the East excited me most, even when I was most keenly aware of its superiority

04:24:23.880 --> 04:24:29.880
to the boards sprawling swollen towns beyond the Ohio with their interminable inquisitions

04:24:29.880 --> 04:24:35.880
which spared only the children and the very old, even then it had always for me a quality

04:24:35.880 --> 04:24:38.240
of distortion.

04:24:38.240 --> 04:24:44.960
West egg, especially still figures in my more fantastic dreams, I see it as a night scene

04:24:44.960 --> 04:24:52.040
by El Greco, a hundred houses at once conventional and grotesque, crouching under a sullen overhanging

04:24:52.040 --> 04:24:54.640
sky and a lustrous moon.

04:24:54.640 --> 04:24:59.640
In the foreground four solemn men in dress suits are walking along the sidewalk with a

04:24:59.640 --> 04:25:05.680
stretcher on which lies a drunken woman in a white evening dress, her hand which dangles

04:25:05.680 --> 04:25:13.600
over the side sparkles cold with jewels, gravely the men turning at a house, the wrong house,

04:25:13.600 --> 04:25:18.440
but no one knows the woman's name and no one cares.

04:25:18.440 --> 04:25:23.860
After Gatsby's death the East was haunted for me like that, distorted beyond my eyes

04:25:23.860 --> 04:25:29.260
power of correction, so when the blue smoke of brittle leaves was in the air and the wind

04:25:29.260 --> 04:25:35.000
blew the wet laundry stiff on the line I decided to come back home.

04:25:35.000 --> 04:25:41.040
There was one thing to be done before I left, an awkward, unpleasant thing, that perhaps

04:25:41.040 --> 04:25:46.840
had better have been let alone, but I wanted to leave things in order and not just trust

04:25:46.920 --> 04:25:51.680
that obliging in indifference see to sweep my refuse away.

04:25:51.680 --> 04:25:56.960
I saw Jordan Baker and talked over and around what had happened to us together, and what

04:25:56.960 --> 04:26:03.760
had happened afterward to me, and she lay perfectly still listening in a big chair.

04:26:03.760 --> 04:26:09.080
She was dressed to play golf, and I remember thinking she looked like a good illustration.

04:26:09.080 --> 04:26:13.960
Her chin raised a little jauntily, her hair the color of an autumn leaf, her face the

04:26:13.960 --> 04:26:18.000
same brown tint as the fingerless glove on her knee.

04:26:18.000 --> 04:26:22.960
When I had finished she told me without comment that she was engaged to another man.

04:26:22.960 --> 04:26:28.240
I doubted that, though there were several she could have married and a nod of her head,

04:26:28.240 --> 04:26:30.400
but I pretended to be surprised.

04:26:30.400 --> 04:26:35.200
For just a minute I wondered if I wasn't making a mistake, and I thought it all over again.

04:26:35.200 --> 04:26:38.360
Nevertheless you did throw me over, said Jordan suddenly.

04:26:38.360 --> 04:26:40.560
You threw me over on the telephone.

04:26:40.560 --> 04:26:44.040
I don't give a damn about you now, but it was a new experience for me, and I felt a

04:26:44.040 --> 04:26:46.600
little dizzy for a while.

04:26:46.600 --> 04:26:48.120
We shook hands.

04:26:48.120 --> 04:26:54.400
Oh, and do you remember, she added, a conversation we had once about driving a car?

04:26:54.400 --> 04:26:56.840
Why, not exactly.

04:26:56.840 --> 04:27:00.920
You said a bad driver was only safe until she met another bad driver.

04:27:00.920 --> 04:27:04.920
Well, I met another bad driver, didn't I?

04:27:04.920 --> 04:27:08.080
I mean, it was careless of me to make such a wrong guess.

04:27:08.080 --> 04:27:11.760
I thought you were rather an honest, straightforward person.

04:27:11.760 --> 04:27:14.280
I thought it was your secret pride.

04:27:14.280 --> 04:27:16.840
I'm thirty, I said.

04:27:16.840 --> 04:27:22.080
I'm five years too old to lie to myself and call it honor.

04:27:22.080 --> 04:27:24.040
She didn't answer.

04:27:24.040 --> 04:27:30.520
Angry and half in love with her, and tremendously sorry I turned away.

04:27:30.520 --> 04:27:33.520
One afternoon late in October I saw Tom Buchanan.

04:27:33.520 --> 04:27:37.600
He was walking ahead of me along Fifth Avenue in his alert aggressive way.

04:27:37.600 --> 04:27:41.160
His hands out a little from his body as if to fight off interference.

04:27:41.160 --> 04:27:46.640
His head moving sharply here and there adapting itself to his restless eyes.

04:27:46.640 --> 04:27:50.940
Just as I slowed up to avoid overtaking him, he stopped and began frowning into the windows

04:27:50.940 --> 04:27:53.000
of a jewelry store.

04:27:53.000 --> 04:27:56.480
Suddenly he saw me and walked back holding out his hand.

04:27:56.480 --> 04:27:57.480
What's the matter, Nick?

04:27:57.480 --> 04:27:59.680
Do you object to shaking hands with me?

04:27:59.680 --> 04:28:00.680
Yes.

04:28:00.680 --> 04:28:02.400
You know what I think of you?

04:28:02.400 --> 04:28:03.400
You're crazy, Nick.

04:28:03.400 --> 04:28:04.400
He said quickly.

04:28:04.400 --> 04:28:05.400
Crazy as hell.

04:28:05.400 --> 04:28:06.400
I don't know what's the matter with you.

04:28:06.400 --> 04:28:07.400
Tom.

04:28:07.400 --> 04:28:08.720
I was so quiet.

04:28:08.720 --> 04:28:11.520
What did you say to Wilson that afternoon?

04:28:11.520 --> 04:28:17.800
He stared at me without a word, and I knew I had guessed right about those missing hours.

04:28:17.800 --> 04:28:21.520
I started to turn away, but he took a step after me and grabbed my arm.

04:28:21.520 --> 04:28:23.680
I told him the truth, he said.

04:28:23.680 --> 04:28:27.360
He came to the door while we were getting ready to leave, and when I sent down word that

04:28:27.360 --> 04:28:30.400
we weren't in he tried to force his way upstairs.

04:28:30.400 --> 04:28:34.040
He was crazy enough to kill me if I hadn't told him who owned the car.

04:28:34.040 --> 04:28:35.920
His hand was on a revolver in his pocket.

04:28:35.920 --> 04:28:37.200
Every minute he was in the house.

04:28:37.200 --> 04:28:39.160
He broke off defiantly.

04:28:39.160 --> 04:28:40.400
What if I did tell him?

04:28:40.400 --> 04:28:41.680
That fellow had it coming to him.

04:28:41.680 --> 04:28:45.640
He threw dust into your eyes just like he did in daisies, but he was a tough one.

04:28:45.640 --> 04:28:50.480
He ran over a myrtle like you'd run over a dog and never even stopped his car.

04:28:50.480 --> 04:28:57.200
There was nothing I could say except the one unutterable fact that it wasn't true.

04:28:57.200 --> 04:29:00.680
And if you think I didn't have my share of suffering, look here.

04:29:00.680 --> 04:29:04.640
When I went to give up that flat and saw that damn box of dog biscuits sitting there

04:29:04.640 --> 04:29:08.160
on the sideboard, I sat down and cried like a baby.

04:29:08.160 --> 04:29:11.320
By God it was awful.

04:29:11.320 --> 04:29:16.920
I couldn't forgive him or like him, but I saw that what he had done was to him entirely

04:29:16.920 --> 04:29:18.680
justified.

04:29:18.680 --> 04:29:22.200
It was all very careless and confused.

04:29:22.200 --> 04:29:24.960
They were careless people, Tom and Daisy.

04:29:24.960 --> 04:29:29.720
They smashed up things and creatures and then retreated back into their money or their

04:29:29.720 --> 04:29:34.360
vast carelessness, or whatever it was that kept them together and let other people clean

04:29:34.360 --> 04:29:37.640
up the mess they had made.

04:29:37.640 --> 04:29:39.360
I shook hands with him.

04:29:39.360 --> 04:29:44.560
It seemed silly not to, for I felt suddenly as though I were talking to a child.

04:29:44.560 --> 04:29:48.120
Then he went into the jewelry store to buy a pearl necklace or perhaps only a pair of

04:29:48.120 --> 04:29:54.560
cuff buttons, rid of my provincial squeamishness forever.

04:29:54.560 --> 04:29:57.040
Gatsby's house was still empty when I left.

04:29:57.040 --> 04:30:00.000
The grass on his lawn had grown as long as mine.

04:30:00.000 --> 04:30:03.440
One of the taxi drivers in the village never took a fair past the entrance gate without

04:30:03.440 --> 04:30:05.680
stopping for a minute and pointing inside.

04:30:05.680 --> 04:30:10.760
Perhaps it was he who drove Daisy and Gatsby over to East Egg the night of the accident.

04:30:10.760 --> 04:30:14.160
And perhaps he had made a story about it all his own.

04:30:14.160 --> 04:30:15.160
I didn't want to hear it.

04:30:15.160 --> 04:30:16.160
I have heard it.

04:30:16.160 --> 04:30:21.080
I spent my Saturday nights in New York because those gleaming dazzling parties of his were

04:30:21.080 --> 04:30:26.680
with me so vividly that I could still hear the music and the laughter faint and incessant

04:30:26.680 --> 04:30:31.480
from his garden and the cars going up and down his drive.

04:30:31.480 --> 04:30:36.880
One night I did hear a material car there and saw its lights stop at his front steps,

04:30:36.880 --> 04:30:38.840
but I didn't investigate.

04:30:38.840 --> 04:30:42.680
Probably it was some final guest who had been away at the ends of the earth and didn't

04:30:42.680 --> 04:30:45.520
know that the party was over.

04:30:45.520 --> 04:30:50.240
On the last night, with my trunk packed and my car sold to the grocer, I went over and

04:30:50.240 --> 04:30:55.080
looked at that huge, incoherent failure of a house once more.

04:30:55.080 --> 04:30:59.800
On the white steps and obscene words crawled by some boy with a piece of bricks stood out

04:30:59.800 --> 04:31:06.960
clearly in the moonlight, and I erased it, drawing my shoe raspingly along the stone.

04:31:06.960 --> 04:31:12.560
Then I wandered down to the beach and sprawled out on the sand.

04:31:12.560 --> 04:31:17.840
Most of the big shore-places were closed now, and there were hardly any lights except the

04:31:17.840 --> 04:31:22.680
shadowy moving glow of a ferryboat across the sound.

04:31:22.680 --> 04:31:27.760
And as the moon rose higher, the inessential houses began to melt away.

04:31:27.760 --> 04:31:32.160
Until gradually I became aware of the old island here that flowered once for Dutch

04:31:32.160 --> 04:31:33.760
sailor's eyes.

04:31:33.760 --> 04:31:37.640
A fresh, green breast of the new world.

04:31:37.640 --> 04:31:39.480
Its vanished trees.

04:31:39.480 --> 04:31:45.040
The trees that had made way for Gatsby's house had once pandered in whispers to the last

04:31:45.040 --> 04:31:48.200
and greatest of all human dreams.

04:31:48.200 --> 04:31:54.400
For a transitory, enchanted moment, man must have held his breath in the presence of this

04:31:54.400 --> 04:31:55.400
continent.

04:31:55.400 --> 04:32:00.720
Compeld into an aesthetic contemplation he neither understood nor desired, face to face

04:32:00.720 --> 04:32:07.720
for the last time in history with something commensurate to his capacity for wonder.

04:32:07.720 --> 04:32:13.560
And as I sat there brooding on the old, unknown world, I thought of Gatsby's wonder when

04:32:13.560 --> 04:32:18.040
he first picked out the green light at the end of Daisy's dark.

04:32:18.040 --> 04:32:23.400
He had come a long way to this blue lawn, and his dream must have seemed so close that

04:32:23.400 --> 04:32:26.240
he could hardly fail to grasp it.

04:32:26.240 --> 04:32:32.040
He did not know that it was already behind him, somewhere back in that vast obscurity

04:32:32.040 --> 04:32:38.600
beyond the city, where the dark fields of the republic rolled on under the night.

04:32:38.600 --> 04:32:46.360
Gatsby believed in the green light, the orgyastic future that year by year recedes before us.

04:32:46.360 --> 04:32:48.960
He looted us then, but that's no matter.

04:32:48.960 --> 04:32:56.400
Tomorrow we will run faster, stretch out our arms farther, and one fine morning.

04:32:56.400 --> 04:33:06.000
So we beat on, boats against the current, born back ceaselessly into the past.

04:33:06.000 --> 04:33:10.640
The End

